er record-keeping regime that Article 12 sits within.\n\nFrom my Compliance Officer vantage point:\n\n**Pros:**\n- Directly addresses Recital 71's requirement for up-to-date technical documentation\n- Automation reduces risk of documentation falling behind, which is a real compliance failure mode\n- Standardized regulatory template ensures consistency across systems\n- Pulling from source-of-truth (version control, experiment tracking) means the docs are grounded in actual system state\n- \"During every model commit\" provides continuous compliance rather than periodic catch-up\n- Reduces reliance on manual processes that are error-prone and hard to audit\n\n**Cons/Concerns:**\n- It addresses documentation generation but NOT the core Article 12(1) requirement for automatic event logging. These are different technical requirements. A compliance officer needs both.\n- \"Standardized regulatory template\" - need to ensure it actually maps to all required fields in Annex IV (technical documentation). If the template is incomplete, we're still non-compliant.\n- The proposal says \"replaces manual reporting with an automated pipeline\" - from a compliance perspective, I'd want human review/validation steps retained for certain critical documents. Full automation without human sign-off could create issues if the auto-generated doc misrepresents something.\n- \"Preserves our scientific ambition by minimizing administrative burden on senior researchers\" - while this is good for retention, as a compliance officer I'm slightly wary of framing compliance as a burden to be minimized rather than a control to be maintained. But practically, reducing manual effort does reduce error risk.\n- The scope seems narrow: it covers hyperparameters, dataset hashes, validation results. Article 12 and Annex IV require much more - risk management system documentation, training/testing/validation processes, general characteristics, capabilities, limitations, etc.\n\n**Overall assessment:** Thi