        "reasoning_content": "The user wants me to evaluate a single proposal (P1766) from the vantage point of a Development Engineer at an R&D institution focused on technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping, in the context of EU AI Act Article 12 compliance.\n\nLet me analyze this proposal:\n\n**Proposal P1766: Unified High-Risk Logging Middleware**\n- A standardized software library providing a consistent API for automatic event recording across all AI prototypes\n- Asynchronous logging wrapper capturing timestamps, input metadata, and system state\n- Non-blocking to core model execution / no performance degradation\n- Goal: prevent teams from wasting prototyping time on repetitive compliance plumbing\n\n**From my vantage point (technical viability + speed/quality of prototyping):**\n\nStrengths:\n1. This directly addresses a real pain point in an R&D lab with multiple AI prototypes. Every new high-risk system needs logging per Article 12(1) - \"shall technically allow for the automatic recording of events.\" If each team builds their own logging, that's repetitive work that slows prototyping.\n2. A standardized API reduces cognitive load and integration time for new projects. Instead of designing a logging schema from scratch, teams plug into an existing interface.\n3. The asynchronous design is technically sound - it avoids the common mistake of synchronous I/O in the hot path degrading model inference latency.\n4. It's architecturally reasonable: a middleware/wrapper layer that sits alongside the model without modifying core logic. This is a well-understood pattern (AOP, decorators, middleware).\n5. \"Consistent API across all AI prototypes regardless of architecture\" - this is ambitious but the scope is bounded to logging (not full observability or tracing), which makes it more achievable.\n\nConcerns/Weaknesses:\n1. \"Regardless of their specific architecture\" is a very strong claim. Different model architectures (transformers, RAG