esses/concerns:\n- \"Before any external deployment or testing\" — this is good but might be too narrow. What about internal use? However, for compliance purposes, external deployment is where regulatory exposure begins, so this is reasonable scope.\n- \"Lead scientists must prove that the necessary events are being logged and stored securely\" — this places technical verification burden on scientists rather than a dedicated compliance/IT team. As a compliance officer, I'd want to ensure there's an independent verification component too. However, having scientists certify their system's logging is appropriate since they know the architecture.\n- \"Monthly audit of logs\" — for a high-risk AI system operating continuously, monthly might be insufficient for some critical use cases (e.g., biometric identification under Annex III point 1(a) which has very specific minimum logging requirements). But as a baseline institutional protocol, monthly is reasonable and could be escalated per-system.\n- The proposal doesn't explicitly mention the specific minimum logging requirements in Article 12(3) for Annex III point 1(a) systems (start/end time of each use, reference database, input data matching, identification of natural persons verifying results). However, the \"checklist-based\" approach could encompass these.\n- No explicit mention of log retention periods or security measures beyond \"stored securely.\"\n\nOverall: This is a strong compliance-oriented proposal that directly serves my function's core concerns. It creates institutional process to verify Article 12 compliance before systems go live and maintains ongoing verification. The internal paper trail protects against regulatory enforcement actions. It's well-scoped for an R&D institution (not over-engineered, not under-specified).\n\nThe main gap I see is that it doesn't explicitly reference the specific minimum requirements in Article 12(3) for Annex III 1(a) systems, and the \"stored securely\" language is thin.