es/limitations narrative, etc.) is harder than the proposal suggests.\n\n4. **What are the gaps?**\n   - Not all required documentation content lives naturally in MLflow or Git. Risk management assessments, capability/limitation narratives, and compliance statements are often written documents that don't map cleanly to structured experiment data.\n   - \"Template-based generator\" is a simplification - the actual technical documentation required by Annex IV of the AI Act has specific structural requirements that may not map trivially onto MLflow metadata.\n   - The proposal somewhat conflates \"keeping documentation current\" with \"generating new content.\" Some elements (like risk management system descriptions) are authored documents, not auto-generated from experiment logs.\n\n5. **Speed/Quality of Prototyping impact:** This is actually quite positive for my function. If documentation updates are automated from existing tooling (which we already use), it reduces the manual overhead that currently slows down prototyping cycles. Engineers can focus on building rather than updating compliance docs after each change. However, the initial build of the pipeline itself takes engineering resources away from other work.\n\n6. **Risk to my domain:** The main risk is over-scoping. If this becomes a large platform project before proving value, it could consume R&D capacity. A lean MVP (e.g., auto-generate training data provenance docs and model version history) would be more immediately prototypable.\n\n**Overall Assessment:**\nThe idea is technically viable and addresses a real pain point for my function (documentation burden slowing down iteration). The core integration pattern is well-understood and prototypable. However, the \"real-time\" and comprehensive framing may overstate what's achievable quickly - some required documentation elements don't naturally flow from experiment trackers. As a prototype, a narrow-slice version (auto-generating data provenance + model architecture docs from MLflow) is very feasible within a sprint or two. The broader vision requires more work but the direction is sound.\n\nI'd lean toward approve with moderate confidence. It's technically viable, it directly reduces friction in my domain (speed of prototyping), and the core tech stack is standard. My concern is scope creep and the gap between \"MLflow metadata\" and \"full Annex IV technical documentation.\" But as a proposal to commit resources to prototype, it clears the bar.\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 72-78. It's solid, viable, addresses my domain directly, but has some gaps in scope realism that need addressing before full implementation. The prototyping angle is strong - we can get a useful MVP fast from existing infrastructure.\n",
