h S3 Object Lock, or append-only databases).\n- However, the proposal as stated is somewhat generic. It describes a high-level architectural pattern without specifying:\n  - What message broker specifically?\n  - What storage backend for immutability?\n  - How does it handle Article 12(3) specific fields (period of use, reference database, input data matches, natural person identification)?\n  - What's the retention period and how is \"lifetime\" operationalized?\n  - Schema design for log entries?\n  \n- That said, as a *proposal* to commit resources toward, it's technically viable and addresses a real need. The pattern is proven, prototyping would be fast (days to weeks for a working prototype), and it directly serves the regulatory requirement.\n\n**Concerns from my vantage point:**\n- The proposal is somewhat high-level/architectural. It doesn't specify implementation details that would matter for prototyping speed. But as a directional commitment, it's clear enough.\n- \"Immutable\" adds operational complexity (can't fix bugs in logging logic without breaking the chain), but this is actually required by the regulation (traceability, verification of compliance).\n- The decoupling via message broker is the right call technically — you don't want to block inference on I/O for log writes. This is standard practice and low-risk.\n- One concern: \"without impacting inference speed\" — while async logging minimizes impact, there's still CPU overhead for serialization into the queue. But this is negligible in practice.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a technically sound, well-understood architectural pattern that directly addresses Article 12(1). It's viable, prototypable quickly (the infrastructure components are all mature and off-the-shelf), and it solves a real problem (compliance logging without degrading performance). The main gap is that it's somewhat generic — it doesn't get into the specific log schema requirements of Article 12(3) for Annex III point 1(a) systems. But as a foundational infrastructure proposal, it clears my bar.\n\nI'd rate this fairly high from a technical viability and prototyping speed perspective. The pattern is proven, components are mature, and it directly addresses the regulatory need. I'll say around 78-82. It's solid but somewhat generic in its specification, which means more design work will be needed before implementation can truly begin. But the architectural direction is correct and low-risk.\n\nLet me settle on a score: 80. Technically very viable, fast to prototype the core pattern, directly addresses the regulation. Slightly held back by being somewhat high-level and not addressing the specific field requirements of Art 12(3).\n",
