it does address real documentation currency issues\n\nI think I should approve this with moderate promise. It addresses a genuine compliance need (documentation currency per Recital 71) and creates traceability infrastructure. But it's incomplete relative to Article 12's full scope and has some concerning framing. From a pure \"does this help me meet my obligations\" standpoint, it helps but doesn't fully solve the problem. It's a partial solution that reduces one compliance risk (stale documentation) while not addressing others (continuous logging, runtime event capture).\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The question is: does this clear MY bar as Compliance Officer? My job is to ensure the institution meets regulatory requirements and survives regulatory scrutiny. \n\nThis proposal:\n- Helps with Recital 71 documentation currency ✓\n- Creates audit trails through versioning ✓\n- Reduces risk of outdated documentation (a common enforcement finding) ✓\n- Does NOT address Art 12(1) built-in logging ✗\n- Does NOT address Art 12(3) specific runtime capture requirements ✗\n- \"Replaces manual\" is a liability concern - compliance docs need human accountability ⚠️\n- Trigger on \"major model release\" may not be sufficient for lifetime tracking ⚠️\n\nI'll approve it because it addresses a real and necessary component of the record-keeping regime (documentation currency), but with moderate promise score because it's incomplete relative to Article 12's full requirements and has some concerning design choices from a compliance standpoint. It's a useful tool but not sufficient on its own, and I'd want significant modifications before implementation.\n\nPromise: I'll say around 55-60. It helps, but the gaps are significant and the \"replaces manual\" framing worries me for accountability purposes.\n",
