ead is low. However, if the library is too rigid or requires deep modification for each project, it becomes a bottleneck rather than an accelerator.\n- The core engineering team building this is a resource commitment. While reducing redundant work across projects is appealing, the initial development cost of getting it right (covering diverse system architectures, compliance requirements that evolve) could be substantial.\n- For prototyping speed specifically: if researchers need to wait for the library to support their specific use case, or if the library adds complexity to quick experiments, it actually slows prototyping down.\n- The proposal mentions \"timestamps and event triggers\" as the modules - this is extremely basic. Real Article 12 compliance requires capturing much more nuanced information (reference databases used, input data matching details, verification person identification for biometric systems).\n\n**Assessment:**\nThe general idea of a shared logging infrastructure has merit from an engineering perspective - avoiding each team reinventing wheels is good practice. However, the specific framing here is problematic:\n1. It's scoped too narrowly (training scripts) for what Article 12 actually requires (lifetime logging).\n2. The \"plug-and-play\" claim is technically optimistic given the diversity of AI system architectures and the domain-specific nature of compliant events.\n3. It may add overhead to prototyping rather than reduce it, especially in early/experimental phases where the logging requirements aren't fully clear yet.\n4. The core engineering team's time spent building this could be better spent on actual model development during prototyping phases.\n\nThat said, some form of standardized logging infrastructure IS valuable and technically feasible for the basic parts (timestamps, system events). The issue is the scope and integration approach described. I'd say this is partially viable but the specific implementation as described has tech