"force multiplier\" type investment: it doesn't directly produce new science, but it frees researcher-hours for science.\n- The cost is moderate relative to the ongoing savings. It's not a moonshot; it's building on existing tooling (MLflow/W&B are common in ML labs already).\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- This directly leverages what the lab likely already has: experiment tracking (MLflow/W&B), CI/CD systems, and the metadata that's already being generated during experiments.\n- The \"documentation lag\" problem it addresses is a real, well-known pain point in ML labs — researchers prototype fast, then scramble to write up documentation for review/filing.\n- It's an integration play on existing infrastructure rather than a greenfield build.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- \"Human-readable compliance reports\" generated automatically may still need human review and sign-off (the AI Act requires certain attestations). So it's not fully hands-off — but it dramatically reduces the time needed.\n- The mapping from raw experimental metadata to the specific information required by Recital 71 (\"general characteristics, capabilities and limitations... algorithms, data, training, testing and validation processes... risk-management system\") is non-trivial. Not all of this lives in MLflow/W&B natively. You'd need to ensure the experiment tracking captures enough semantic structure.\n- Sphinx for compliance documentation is a reasonable choice but may need customization to match regulatory formatting expectations.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThis is a strong, practical proposal that aligns well with my domain concerns. It's:\n- Resource-efficient (ongoing savings on human time)\n- Leverages existing infrastructure (MLflow/W&B already in use likely)\n- Doesn't constrain scientific ambition — actually enables more of it\n- Addresses a real pain point (documentation lag)\n- Moderate cost, high ongoing ROI\n\nThe main risk is that the initial build might be more