        "reasoning_content": "The user asks me to evaluate proposal P2772 as a Development Engineer at an R&D institution, focusing on technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nLet me analyze the proposal:\n\n**Proposal P2772**: \"Centralized Compliance-Logging Infrastructure\" — A modular, cross-project software library that standardizes automatic event recording for all high-risk AI systems. A small DevOps team creates a standardized API capturing timestamps and input data across a shared compute cluster. Goal: prevent redundant bespoke logging per project.\n\n**My vantage point**: Technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nLet me think through this carefully:\n\n**Technical Viability:**\n- A centralized logging library is technically straightforward — it's essentially a structured logging framework with compliance-specific fields (timestamps, input data, event types). This is well-trodden engineering territory.\n- However, the proposal says \"across our shared compute cluster\" which implies a specific infrastructure assumption. High-risk AI systems may not all run on the same cluster, or may involve heterogeneous architectures (edge devices, cloud, hybrid). A single library across a shared cluster might work for training/inference pipelines but could be limiting if some high-risk systems deploy differently.\n- The \"standardized API\" concept is sound — it's essentially an SDK with required fields mapped to Article 12 requirements (timestamps per Art 12(3)(a), reference database, input data for matches, natural person IDs).\n- One concern: the proposal focuses on \"timestamps and input data\" but Article 12 has broader requirements depending on the system type. For Annex III point 1(a) systems specifically, it needs reference database info, match data, verification person IDs. The proposal seems to narrow the scope somewhat.\n- \"Modular\" is good — suggests extensibility for different risk categories and system types.\n