ent it each time — that's a speed win for the compliance-critical parts.\n- The risk: over-engineering a \"unified\" architecture before we know exactly what logging points are needed per system type (Article 12(3) has specific requirements only for Annex III point 1(a) systems, which are biometric/identity verification systems). Building for the most complex case from day one could slow down simpler deployments.\n- The \"mapped directly to Article 12\" mapping is a good idea — it creates a traceability between engineering artifacts and regulatory requirements, which is valuable.\n\n**My assessment as a Development Engineer:**\nThe core concept (a shared logging SDK/module) is technically sound and would reduce duplication. However, the framing as a single \"unified architecture\" that intercepts everything across all pipelines overstates technical feasibility for heterogeneous model types. The middleware approach could work if designed as an interface/SDK with per-pipeline adapters rather than a monolithic interceptor. From a prototyping speed perspective, having this available would help teams get to market faster on the compliance front, but the \"universal\" claim is risky and could introduce integration friction that slows down novel experiments.\n\nI'd say this is a reasonable direction but the proposal as stated has some over-promising in its \"universal\" and \"without duplicating engineering effort\" language. The technical viability is moderate-high (the core idea works), but the speed/quality of prototyping benefit depends heavily on how flexible the architecture actually is. I lean toward approving it because:\n1. The fundamental engineering approach (shared logging module) is sound and reduces per-project work.\n2. It directly addresses Article 12's \"technically allow for automatic recording\" requirement in a structured way.\n3. Even if not perfectly \"universal,\" having a starting framework accelerates individual implementations.\n\nBut I'm somewhat