abase, input data for matches, and identification of natural persons involved in verification (Article 14(5)). The proposal mentions \"input-output pairs\" and \"reference database queries\" which partially covers this, but the identification of natural persons is not explicitly mentioned.\n2. \"Immutable, encrypted ledger\" — while good for integrity, need to consider whether this conflicts with any data protection requirements or if there are retention period concerns. However, from a compliance standpoint, immutability supports audit trails.\n3. The proposal says it applies to \"all high-risk AI prototypes\" — but Article 12(3) specific minimums only apply to Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification). The framework should be modular enough to handle different levels of logging requirements. The proposal does say \"modular API\" which suggests this flexibility exists.\n4. From a market reputation standpoint, having a unified compliance infrastructure demonstrates organizational maturity and regulatory readiness — positive for clients and regulators.\n\n**Assessment:**\nThis is a strong, directly relevant proposal that addresses the core requirement of Article 12(1) and partially covers (2) and (3). The standardization approach reduces institutional risk significantly. The main gap I see is explicit mention of all Article 12(3) minimums (particularly identification of natural persons in verification), but the modular design likely accommodates this. From a compliance officer's perspective, this is exactly the kind of infrastructure investment that protects the institution from non-compliance liability and supports audit readiness.\n\nThe proposal is well-aligned with regulatory requirements, addresses institutional survival (compliance failure = existential risk under EU AI Act penalties), and supports market reputation (demonstrating systematic compliance capability). I would approve this.\n\nPromise score: This is highly relevant and directly implements a core regulatory requirement in a scalable way. The only minor gap is the explicitness of all 12(3) sub-requirements, but the modular design likely covers it. I'd rate this around 82-85. It's clearly something to pursue, with some implementation details to be specified (particularly around Article 14(5) person identification and ensuring the framework handles different risk categories appropriately).\n",
