The \"wrapper around existing logging utilities\" approach is pragmatic - it doesn't require building from scratch, which keeps initial development fast.\n- In an R&D environment, systems evolve quickly. A unified framework needs to be flexible enough to accommodate different types of high-risk AI systems (biometrics under Annex III 1(a) have very specific logging requirements vs. other categories).\n\n**Concerns:**\n- The proposal is somewhat generic. It mentions \"pre-built hooks\" but doesn't address the heterogeneity problem - Article 12(3) has very specific minimum requirements for biometric identification systems that differ from general high-risk system requirements in 12(2). A single framework needs to handle this variance.\n- In an R&D lab, prototyping speed is paramount. If the framework becomes a bottleneck (e.g., you can't prototype because the logging interface doesn't fit your novel architecture), it could actually slow things down.\n- The proposal says \"wrapper around existing logging utilities\" which is good for speed, but the \"enforcement through shared API\" part needs to be carefully designed so it's not overly prescriptive.\n\n**Overall Assessment:**\nThis is a technically viable and sensible approach. It addresses a real need (reducing redundant compliance work across teams) while leveraging existing infrastructure. The main risk in an R&D context is rigidity vs. flexibility, but the \"wrapper\" framing suggests pragmatism. From my vantage point of technical viability and prototyping speed, this clears the bar - it's a well-scoped, implementable solution that would genuinely help teams move faster on compliance while maintaining quality. It's not revolutionary, but it's solid engineering practice applied to a regulatory requirement.\n\nI'd say this is approvable. The promise score should reflect that it's solidly useful but not groundbreaking - maybe around 72-78. It addresses the core need well, the approach is technically sound, and it directly supports prototyping speed by reducing boilerplate. I'll go with 75.\n",
