        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze proposal P2702 from the vantage point of a Compliance Officer at an R&D institution subject to the EU AI Act, with domain concerns being institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nThe proposal is: A Regulatory Risk Tiering Framework — an internal classification system to determine which R&D projects fall under the high-risk category of the AI Act. It proposes creating a review board that maps project goals against Annex III criteria to designate required logging levels, establishing a clear threshold for when Article 12 protocols must be activated, and preventing wasteful application of expensive record-keeping infrastructure to low-risk research.\n\nLet me assess this from my compliance vantage point:\n\n**Strengths:**\n1. This is fundamentally sound. The EU AI Act's obligations under Article 12 apply specifically to \"high-risk AI systems.\" If we don't know which projects are high-risk, we either over-apply (wasting resources) or under-apply (non-compliance, regulatory risk). A classification/tiering system is a basic compliance control.\n2. Mapping against Annex III criteria is exactly what the regulation requires — you need to determine whether your system falls within the high-risk categories before you can know which obligations apply.\n3. The \"review board\" concept provides governance and accountability for these determinations, which is important for demonstrating due diligence to regulators.\n4. Establishing a \"clear threshold for when Article 12 protocols must be activated\" directly addresses the compliance question of when logging infrastructure must be in place.\n\n**Concerns/Risks:**\n1. The classification itself carries risk — if we misclassify a project as low-risk when it's actually high-risk, we'd be non-compliant. The proposal needs to err on the side of caution (i.e., when in doubt, treat as high-risk). The proposal doesn't explicitly address this conservatism principle.\n2. \"Prevents wasteful application\" — from my vantage point, I'm cautious about language that prioritizes cost savings over compliance. However, the underlying logic is valid: we can't implement Article 12 logging on every R&D experiment, and we need a principled way to scope our obligations.\n3. The proposal says \"maps current project goals against Annex III criteria\" — this is good but Annex I (product safety) also creates high-risk systems. A complete framework would need to address both Annexes. However, for an R&D institution developing AI systems, Annex III is the more relevant one, and Annex I would apply when embedded in regulated products.\n4. The \"review board\" needs clear governance — who chairs it, what's the quorum, how often does it review (projects evolve), what's the escalation path. But these are implementation details that can be worked out.\n\n**From institutional survival perspective:** Without a tiering framework, we face two existential risks:\n- We fail to identify high-risk systems → regulatory penalties (up to 7% of global turnover for prohibited practices, 3% for other violations) → market reputation damage → potential loss of operating licenses in EU markets.\n- We over-invest in logging infrastructure for every project → resource drain → inability to fund actual compliance where it matters → still end up non-compliant on the systems that actually matter.\n\n**From regulatory compliance perspective:** This is essentially a prerequisite control. You cannot comply with Article 12 if you don't first determine which systems are high-risk. The tiering framework IS the compliance mechanism that triggers Article 12 obligations. It's not optional — it's foundational.\n\n**From market reputation perspective:** Demonstrating to clients, partners, and regulators that we have a structured governance process for determining AI Act applicability is a strong signal of institutional maturity and regulatory readiness. It shows we take our obligations seriously rather than winging it.\n\nOverall assessment: This proposal is fundamentally necessary for compliance. It's not optional \"nice-to-have\" — it's the gating mechanism that determines when Article 12 applies. Without it, we're either flying blind (risk of non-compliance) or over-applying resources indiscriminately. The core concept is correct and essential.\n\nMy concerns are mostly about implementation details (conservatism principle, covering both Annexes, review frequency), not about the fundamental approach. The proposal as stated captures the right idea.\n\nDecision: APPROVE. This is a foundational compliance control that my function depends on. Without it, I cannot assure the institution of Article 12 compliance because I can't even identify which systems need to comply.\n\nPromise score: This is highly relevant and necessary from my vantage point. It's not a perfect proposal (lacks conservatism principle, doesn't mention Annex I, governance details are thin), but the core concept is essential. I'd say around 82-85. The idea is clearly right and needed; the gaps are fixable in implementation rather than requiring rejection.\n",
