ation is a known failure point for compliance.\n2. Structured regulatory reports align with the need for \"clear and comprehensive form\" mentioned in Recital 71.\n3. Captures training, testing, validation data which are explicitly called out in Recital 71.\n4. Automation reduces human error and ensures consistency — critical for audit readiness.\n5. Model lineage (version tracking) supports traceability requirements.\n6. This is foundational infrastructure that enables compliance with multiple other articles (technical documentation under Article 11, post-market monitoring under Article 72).\n\n**Weaknesses/Concerns from compliance perspective:**\n1. The proposal focuses on model-level documentation but doesn't explicitly address the automatic logging of events required by Article 12(1) — start/end times, reference databases, input data matching, natural person identification (Article 12(3)). It's more about technical documentation than operational logging.\n2. \"Custom reporting engine\" introduces a single point of failure and adds complexity that itself needs to be validated/maintained for compliance.\n3. The scope seems limited to training/development artifacts — doesn't clearly address runtime event logging, which is the core of Article 12(1).\n4. However, it does contribute meaningfully to the \"technical documentation\" aspect which Recital 71 emphasizes as essential.\n\n**Institutional survival / market reputation angle:**\n- Having automated, current technical documentation is a significant competitive and regulatory advantage. Auditors will want to see this.\n- If the system fails or produces incomplete reports, that's a compliance gap. But the proposal is about building the capability, which is necessary.\n- Market reputation: being able to demonstrate robust record-keeping and documentation infrastructure signals maturity to regulators and business partners.\n\n**Decision assessment:**\nThis is a solid compliance-relevant proposal. It addresses a real a