ng record.\" Regulators and notified bodies will want documentation in specific formats for conformity assessment (Annex IV technical documentation). A purely dynamic web-based document might not satisfy format requirements unless it can also produce fixed snapshots. However, the proposal says \"formatted reports,\" which suggests output is still structured.\n- \"Maximizes scientific reproducibility\" is a nice framing but slightly misaligns with the compliance purpose — the regulation isn't primarily about reproducibility (that's more of a research ethics concern), it's about traceability and demonstrating compliance. However, reproducibility does support traceability, so this isn't a dealbreaker.\n- The proposal focuses on training logs, validation reports, hyperparameters, dataset versions — these are relevant to Annex IV technical documentation requirements (description of development process, training data details, etc.). It doesn't explicitly mention risk management system documentation or the logging capabilities required by Article 12 itself, but that's a separate concern.\n- Implementation resource commitment: building a CI/CD pipeline for doc generation is a moderate engineering effort. For an R&D institution, this is feasible and addresses a real gap.\n\n**From my vantage point (institutional survival, regulatory compliance, market reputation):**\nThis is a strong proposal. It directly addresses a specific regulatory requirement (up-to-date technical documentation) in a way that reduces ongoing compliance burden. The automation means the lab doesn't have to remember to update docs before every audit or deployment — it's built into the workflow. This protects against:\n- Regulatory penalties for outdated documentation\n- Reputational damage if an incident occurs and the tech file is stale\n- Audit failures where documentation doesn't match deployed system\n\nThe risk is that \"automatically generates\" might produce documentation that, while accurate to the data, lacks the narrative context (risk management rationale, intended purpose statements, etc.) that Annex IV requires. But the proposal says it extracts from \"training logs and validation reports,\" which covers a significant portion. The human review layer would still be needed for the interpretive/narrative parts, but the factual/technical backbone would be automated.\n\nI think this clears my bar. It's directly responsive to Recital 71, reduces compliance risk, is proportionate in scope, and addresses a real operational gap (documentation drift). I'd approve it.\n\nPromise score: This is very well-aligned with my domain. The \"up-to-date throughout lifetime\" requirement is one of the hardest ongoing obligations because it's continuous rather than one-time. Automating it from source data is the right architectural choice. I'd give this a high score — maybe 82-85. It's not a perfect solution (doesn't cover all of Annex IV, doesn't address Article 12 logging per se), but for what it addresses, it's well-conceived and directly protective of institutional compliance posture.\n\nI'll go with 83.\n",
