t risk significantly.\n- From market reputation: Demonstrating to clients and regulators that you've stress-tested your compliance mechanisms builds trust and competitive advantage.\n- From a resource commitment standpoint: This is a validation framework - it requires researchers, time, and computational resources. But the scope seems reasonably bounded (testing logging specifically under adversarial conditions).\n\n**Assessment:**\nThis proposal directly supports Article 12 compliance by verifying that the technical capability for automatic recording actually functions as required under stress. It addresses the risk of \"compliant on paper but not in practice.\" For a compliance officer, the concern is: if there's an incident and our logs don't capture what they should, we face enforcement action, fines (up to €35M or 7% for serious violations), and reputational destruction. This stress test mitigates that specific risk.\n\nHowever, I should consider: Is this within scope of compliance? Yes - verifying that the logging system meets the requirements of Article 12(2)(a) \"identifying situations that may result in the high-risk AI system presenting a risk\" is exactly what adversarial testing does. It's not just nice-to-have; it's a way to demonstrate due diligence.\n\nThe promise score: This is strongly aligned with my compliance function. It directly validates a specific regulatory requirement under adverse conditions. It protects against enforcement action and reputational damage. I'd rate this fairly high - maybe 82-85. It's not a 100 because it's somewhat of an engineering/QA activity rather than purely a governance/compliance process, but from my vantage point, verifying that the technical compliance mechanism actually works is critical to institutional survival.\n\nLet me settle on 84. It's strongly aligned but there are implementation considerations (who owns this? does it duplicate existing test suites?) and the \"scientifically reliable under extreme conditi