m my vantage point on resource efficiency and using existing infrastructure:\n- The core requirements are straightforward engineering, not R&D. They don't push scientific ambition forward.\n- Building a \"specialized system\" when the lab likely already has logging, identity management, and versioning tools would be inefficient.\n- But if we interpret this as \"ensure our existing systems can capture these specific data points for Annex III 1(a) compliance,\" it's a bounded, necessary task.\n\nI think the proposal is on the right track but over-scoped in its framing. The actual work required is modest integration/configuration rather than building a new module. From a resource-allocation perspective, I'd approve it with the understanding that it should be done as lightweight integration into existing infrastructure rather than as a standalone \"system.\" But I'm not sure if the lab actually has Annex III 1(a) systems in its portfolio — I don't know that for certain.\n\nGiven my role focuses on resource efficiency and using existing infrastructure:\n- The proposal is narrowly targeted (good for resource allocation)\n- It addresses non-negotiable compliance (must-do, not optional)\n- But the framing as building a \"specialized system\" suggests potential over-investment\n- The actual technical requirements are modest and could leverage existing tools\n\nI'll lean toward approve with moderate promise score. It's necessary compliance work if the lab has relevant systems, but the implementation should be efficient and leverage what exists. I'd give it maybe 55-65 — it's clearly needed (if applicable) but the framing suggests potential inefficiency in how resources would be allocated to build a \"system\" when the actual requirements are more about configuration and integration.\n\nLet me settle on: approve, promise 58. It's bounded compliance work that addresses real requirements, but from my resource-efficiency vantage point, building a \"specialized system\" is heavier