fic about what must be logged, so the core schema is well-defined.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- The proposal explicitly says \"automated infrastructure layer\" and \"modular set of API wrappers\" - this suggests it integrates with what we already have rather than requiring a complete rip-and-replace.\n- It positions itself as an addition to the research stack, not a replacement. Good.\n- The database schemas would need to be maintained, but that's operational overhead I can budget for.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- \"All the lab's diverse R&D models\" - scope creep risk. Do we need this for every model or just high-risk ones? Article 12 applies specifically to high-risk AI systems. Building it for ALL models might be over-engineering if many of our models aren't high-risk. However, the modular design could mean you opt-in per system, so maybe that's addressed.\n- Development timeline: building a robust unified framework takes time and engineering resources. We'd need dedicated engineers pulling them from research projects.\n- The proposal says \"ensures legal compliance without requiring each individual researcher to manually reinvent\" - this is the key value prop for me as Lab Director. Compliance is a floor, not a ceiling, but getting it done once centrally is far more efficient than N times per team.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a strong proposal from my vantage point. It directly addresses resource efficiency (one build serves many), reduces per-researcher compliance burden, and uses modular design that can grow with our needs. The main risk is scope (do we need it for non-high-risk models?) and upfront engineering investment. But given that Article 12 is a hard legal requirement for high-risk systems and we likely have multiple such systems in the pipeline, this is clearly more cost-effective than distributed compliance efforts.\n\nI would approve this. The promise score should be high - maybe 82-85. It's not a perfect proposa