r, say, 2-3 projects might not justify the upfront engineering investment.\n- However, Article 12 is mandatory for ALL high-risk AI systems, so if we're in this space at all, we'll need logging infrastructure regardless. The question is whether building it once vs. per-project makes sense. Given that Article 12(1) mandates automatic logging and 12(2)-(3) specify what must be captured, having a shared framework that maps to these requirements seems sensible for any lab with more than one or two high-risk systems.\n- \"Universal\" might be too rigid — different high-risk categories (Annex III points) have different logging requirements per Article 12(3). A truly universal schema might need significant customization per category. But an API/library can still provide the backbone while allowing project-specific extensions.\n- The upfront engineering cost is real and competes with other R&D priorities. But it's a one-time investment that amortizes across projects.\n\nOverall from my vantage point: This is a strong resource-efficiency play. It converts a recurring per-project compliance cost into a one-time infrastructure investment. It leverages the fact that Article 12 requirements are largely common across high-risk systems (timestamps, event recording, specific data points). The \"during development phase\" timing is smart — it's cheaper to build logging in from the start than to retrofit. The main risk is over-investment if we have very few high-risk projects, but even then, building a basic shared logging layer is far cheaper than per-project custom solutions.\n\nI'd rate this fairly highly. It's exactly the kind of infrastructure investment that makes sense for an R&D lab trying to do more with limited resources. The efficiency gain is real and the regulatory driver makes it non-optional (you need logging anyway; the question is how you implement it). I'll approve it with a strong promise score, maybe 82-85. It's not a perfect proposal (the \"universal\" framing co