e between scientific ambition and funding constraints:**\n- This is a pragmatic approach to compliance. Rather than hiring a team of compliance officers or creating heavy administrative processes, it automates what can be automated using tools we already have.\n- The cost is primarily engineering time to build the bridge/integration layer, which is one-time investment with ongoing low maintenance.\n- It doesn't require significant new funding - it's an integration project on existing infrastructure.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- This is the strongest argument FOR the proposal. We already have Git repos (version control for code), MLflow (experiment tracking, model versions, metrics). The proposal essentially says \"let's make our existing research tooling do double duty as compliance infrastructure.\" That's extremely resource-efficient.\n- It avoids the anti-pattern of building a separate, siloed compliance system that researchers would need to manually feed data into.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- \"Replaces static documents\" - regulators may expect certain formal documentation artifacts. A dashboard might not be acceptable for all purposes (e.g., regulatory audits might want a specific format). However, the proposal says it \"generates living technical documentation\" which suggests it does produce documents, just that they're auto-generated and kept current.\n- The scope is clear: Article 12 criteria specifically. It's not trying to solve all compliance problems at once.\n- \"Flag missing required fields based on Article 12 criteria\" - this is a practical, bounded feature set.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThis is a well-scoped, resource-efficient proposal that leverages existing infrastructure. From my vantage point of resource allocation and effective use of what we already have, this is very attractive. The engineering investment is moderate (building integration bridges between systems we already maintain), the ongoing maintenance is low