        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze proposal P2173 from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal is: \"Immutable Compliance Data Lake\" - a centralized, write-once storage architecture for all high-risk system logs and versioned reference databases, using cloud-native object store with WORM (Write Once Read Many) policies and strict access controls.\n\nLet me assess this from my technical viability and prototyping speed/quality perspective:\n\n**Technical Viability:**\n- Cloud-native object stores with WORM compliance (like AWS S3 Object Lock, Azure Blob Storage immutability policies, GCS retention policies) are mature, well-documented technologies. This is technically straightforward to implement.\n- The architecture is centralized rather than embedded in each system, which simplifies the per-system implementation burden but introduces a dependency on this central infrastructure being available and properly integrated.\n- \"Write-once\" semantics align well with Article 12's requirement for logs over the lifetime of the system - once written, they can't be tampered with or deleted prematurely.\n- The proposal mentions storing both logs AND versioned reference databases, which is a reasonable scope given Article 12(3)(b) requires recording \"the reference database against which input data has been checked.\"\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- A centralized infrastructure like this is a platform-level investment. From my perspective as someone focused on prototyping speed, I need to consider: does this block or enable rapid iteration?\n- On one hand, if the logging infrastructure exists and teams just \"point their systems at it,\" that reduces per-project overhead and speeds up prototyping of new high-risk AI systems. You don't have to rebuild logging from scratch each time.\n- On the other hand, a centralized system introduces integration complexity. Every new prototype