ol amortizes across multiple projects.\n\n**Concerns:**\n1. \"Dynamically generates\" and \"human-readable compliance reports\" — there's a risk here that auto-generated documents may not meet the quality bar of \"clear and comprehensive form\" as Recital 71 requires. The regulation asks for documentation drawn in a way that enables traceability and compliance assessment. A purely automated pipeline might produce technically accurate but insufficiently contextual documents. This could create a false sense of compliance.\n2. The scope mentions hyperparameters, dataset versions, validation results — these are the easier parts. Recital 71 also requires documentation on \"relevant risk-management system\" and \"general characteristics, capabilities and limitations of the system.\" Those are harder to auto-generate from MLflow logs alone. The proposal may understate the scope needed for full compliance.\n3. \"Single-click export\" is a nice pitch but might oversimplify what auditors/regulators actually need. Still, this doesn't make it a bad idea — just needs refinement.\n4. Building API connectors and template mapping is a well-understood engineering task with moderate effort. Not a huge resource commitment relative to the ongoing savings.\n\n**Resource-allocation assessment:**\n- This is a high-leverage use of existing infrastructure (MLflow) for a recurring compliance need.\n- The one-time build cost is modest relative to the continuous labor savings across all high-risk systems.\n- It doesn't require new funding categories or new headcount — it's an integration task on existing tools.\n- The risk of scope creep exists (trying to cover ALL of Recital 71 with auto-generation), but even a partial solution covering the data/training/validation documentation portion saves significant effort.\n- It aligns well with \"effective use of existing R&D infrastructure\" — literally repurposing MLflow's data for compliance output.\n\n**Scientific ambition vs. funding constraints:*