gging for biometric identification systems in Annex III point 1(a)). However, this proposal is scoped to a specific audit protocol and may not need to cover everything.\n- \"Quarterly\" frequency – is that sufficient? The regulation says logs should be over the \"lifetime of the system.\" Quarterly stress tests seem reasonable but one might argue for more frequent checks or event-triggered reviews as well. However, quarterly is a defensible baseline.\n- It doesn't address the technical documentation aspect from Recital 71 (keeping docs up to date throughout lifetime). But again, this proposal is specifically about logging/traceability audit.\n- The scope seems reasonable and focused – it's not trying to do everything at once.\n\nFrom my compliance vantage point:\n- This directly addresses a mandatory obligation (Article 12(2)) for high-risk AI systems\n- It creates an internal control mechanism that demonstrates regulatory diligence\n- It reduces the risk of non-compliance findings during regulatory inspections\n- It's proportionate in scope – not overreaching, just doing what's required plus a verification layer\n- The \"stress test\" language implies testing edge cases and failure scenarios, which is exactly what a compliance officer would want to verify\n\nThis is a solid, focused compliance proposal. It doesn't cost as much as building new technical systems (it's an audit/verification process on existing capabilities), but it creates a critical assurance layer. From my perspective of institutional survival and regulatory compliance, this is important because without such verification, we might not know if our logging actually meets the \"appropriate to intended purpose\" standard until a regulator tells us it doesn't.\n\nI'll approve this with a high promise score. It's well-scoped, directly addresses a mandatory provision, creates demonstrable internal controls, and protects against both regulatory sanctions and reputational damage from being caught out by regulators.\n\nPromise: I'd say around 82-85. It's strong but it's a verification/audit layer rather than the actual implementation of logging capabilities (which would presumably be handled elsewhere). It also only covers Article 12(2) not the full scope of Article 12 including paragraph 3. But as a standalone compliance protocol, it's very well-targeted and actionable.\n",
