t alongside or after the core logging implementation.\n- The \"suite of synthetic failure scenarios\" could scope-creep if not bounded well. How many scenarios? What level of fault injection? This needs scoping discipline.\n- From a prototyping speed perspective, this is a medium-complexity task: writing test harnesses, fault injection code, assertions against log format/content. Not trivial but very tractable for an experienced team.\n\n**Quality:**\n- Good engineering practice: testing your logging infrastructure before relying on it in production.\n- Running every deployment cycle ensures regression protection.\n- The \"synthetic failure scenarios\" aspect is particularly valuable because the most important time for logging to work is exactly when things go wrong (risk events, substantial modifications). Testing under normal conditions doesn't verify that logs capture these critical events reliably.\n\n**Concerns from my vantage:**\n- Scope ambiguity: \"a set of automated audit scripts\" and \"a suite of synthetic failure scenarios\" - how many? What's the coverage target? Without clear scoping, this could balloon.\n- Dependency on the logging implementation being in place first (or at least defined). If the architecture isn't settled, writing tests against a moving target wastes effort.\n- The proposal says \"prevents costly post-market failures\" - while true, this is more of a QA/reliability engineering task than a pure development/prototyping task. But it does sit within the technical implementation scope.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is technically viable, well-motivated by the regulation (Article 12 requires logging to actually work for specific event types), and represents sound engineering practice. The main risk is scope creep on \"synthetic failure scenarios\" without clear boundaries. It's a contained, well-understood engineering task that would be quick to prototype. I'd lean toward approving this - it's good engineering hygiene that directly serv