at reads logs doesn't fulfill that obligation - it's a downstream consumer of logs. The core compliance requirement (the system must record) is not addressed here.\n- However, as a supplementary tool, it enhances traceability and audit readiness significantly.\n- \"Map them directly to the specific requirements of Article 12\" - this is valuable for demonstrating compliance to regulators.\n- ELK stack is a reasonable technology choice for log management at scale.\n- The proposal doesn't address who is responsible for ensuring logs are complete, unalterable, and retained for the full lifetime - critical compliance concerns.\n- It says \"real-time monitoring\" which supports ongoing compliance but the regulation emphasizes lifetime recording.\n- From a market reputation standpoint, having a polished audit trail presentation tool helps when dealing with regulators and clients who ask about compliance posture.\n\n**From my vantage point (institutional survival, regulatory compliance, market reputation):**\n\nThis is a useful supplementary tool but it doesn't address the core obligation. The core obligation is that the system records. This dashboard is what you'd want AFTER the system records - for presenting that record to regulators and for internal monitoring. \n\nHowever, from a pure compliance perspective:\n- It supports demonstrating traceability (Art 12 purpose)\n- It supports post-market monitoring visibility (Art 72)\n- It helps with audit readiness (regulators will ask for this kind of documentation)\n- The \"separation of recording from reporting\" principle is architecturally sound and reduces risk of tampering with logs during the reporting process\n\nBut it's not sufficient on its own. It doesn't create the logging capability. It assumes logs exist. For an institution, building this without having the underlying logging infrastructure in place would be premature. However, as a standalone proposal to BUILD this dashboard (assuming logs already exist or will 