ication of natural persons involved in verification (Article 14(5)) - user IDs are captured\n\n**Recital 71:** Emphasizes comprehensible information on how systems were developed and perform throughout lifetime. Technical documentation including algorithms, data, training, testing, validation processes. The SDK addresses the logging aspect but doesn't seem to address the broader technical documentation requirement (though that's more Article 11 territory).\n\n**Compliance Officer Assessment:**\n\nStrengths:\n- Directly implements the \"automatic recording\" mandate of Art. 12(1) - this is the core obligation\n- Standardized approach across all high-risk pipelines ensures consistency (important for audit readiness and demonstrating systematic compliance)\n- Immutable database supports traceability and non-tampering (regulatory bodies will want evidence integrity)\n- Asynchronous queue means it won't compromise system performance (practical deployment consideration - if logging slows the system, engineers may route around it, creating gaps)\n- Reduces manual coding effort = reduces human error in implementing logs = more consistent compliance\n- \"Legal consistency across diverse portfolio\" speaks directly to my concern about uniform compliance\n\nConcerns:\n- The SDK as described captures \"timestamps, input hashes, and user IDs\" - this is a baseline but may not fully satisfy Art. 12(3) for Annex III 1(a) systems (e.g., the reference database field, the actual matched input data rather than just a hash). However, the proposal says it provides \"standardized hooks and API endpoints\" which implies extensibility - engineers could add system-specific fields through these hooks.\n- The SDK is an internal tool - it's infrastructure that enables compliance but isn't itself the compliance documentation. It doesn't address the broader technical documentation (Recital 71) or post-market monitoring workflows (Art. 72). But that's not what it claims to be.\n- \"Immutable database\" - good for integrity, but need to ensure retention periods comply with applicable law and that access controls are appropriate.\n\nOverall: This is a directly relevant, high-value compliance infrastructure investment. It addresses the core mechanical requirement of Article 12(1) in a scalable, consistent way. From my vantage point (regulatory compliance, institutional survival), having a unified logging layer across all high-risk systems is critical for:\n1. Demonstrating to regulators that we systematically comply with Art. 12\n2. Being audit-ready without scrambling per-project\n3. Reducing the risk of gaps in logging (which would be a compliance violation)\n4. Supporting post-market monitoring and incident investigation\n\nThe main risk is scope - will it actually capture all required fields for each system category? But as an SDK with \"hooks and API endpoints,\" it's designed to be extensible. The core architecture is sound from a compliance perspective.\n\nI would approve this. It's directly on-mission for my function. The promise score should be high - maybe 82-85. I'd deduct some because:\n- It doesn't explicitly address the specific Annex III 1(a) minimum fields (reference database, matched input data) in its description, though the \"hooks\" architecture implies extensibility\n- It's an enabling tool, not a complete compliance solution on its own\n- I'd want to see that retention policies, access controls, and data minimization principles are baked in\n\nLet me settle on 83.\n",
