ing infrastructure I care about.\n\nThe proposal maps well to Article 12 requirements:\n- Art 12(1): automatic recording of events — the pipeline leverages existing logging\n- Recital 71: technical documentation including \"general characteristics, capabilities and limitations... algorithms, data, training, testing and validation processes\" — these are all in ML tracking tools already\n\nPotential concerns:\n- Is this a complete solution? The proposal focuses on generating documentation from metadata. Article 12(3) has specific requirements for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometrics-related) that require recording specific things like reference databases, input data for matches, and identification of natural persons in verification. These are more operational/logging requirements than experiment metadata. So the pipeline might cover the technical documentation aspect well but not fully address all Article 12 logging requirements for specific high-risk categories.\n- However, from a resource-allocation perspective, this is still a strong proposal because it addresses the biggest chunk of compliance burden (technical documentation) using existing infrastructure.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a very strong proposal from my vantage point. It's cost-effective (leverages existing tools), addresses a real productivity problem, requires relatively modest engineering investment, and directly serves regulatory compliance without requiring new data collection infrastructure. The main risk is that it might not cover 100% of Article 12 requirements (especially the specific logging in paragraph 3 for certain high-risk systems), but as a component of a broader compliance strategy, it's highly efficient.\n\nI'd rate this quite highly — maybe 82-85. It's very well-aligned with my concerns. The slight deduction is because:\n- It addresses documentation generation more than the \"automatic recording of events\" logging requirement (Art 12(1) and (3))\n- There might be gaps for spe