ster on model development while compliance is handled automatically.\n- The \"standardized software layer\" aspect means once built, it's reusable across projects - reducing per-project setup time.\n- However, building this infrastructure itself takes significant upfront engineering effort before any AI prototyping benefits are realized. The first few weeks/months would be pure infrastructure work with no AI model output.\n- \"Removes manual record-keeping burdens from researchers\" - yes, this directly improves research velocity once deployed.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- The proposal is somewhat generic - it doesn't specify which specific logging events map to Article 12(3) requirements (start/end times of use, reference database identity, input data for matches, verifier identification). It mentions \"input-output pair\" but the Annex III biometric-specific requirements are more nuanced.\n- \"Immutable logs\" adds engineering complexity - once written, you can't correct a bad log entry without creating a new entry (audit trail pattern). This is doable but adds some friction to debugging/development.\n- The proposal doesn't address retention periods or the \"lifetime of the system\" requirement explicitly in terms of storage architecture.\n\n**Overall Assessment:**\nThis is technically viable, well-motivated by the regulation, and would genuinely improve prototyping speed once built. The architecture described (decoupled sidecar, metadata DB) is standard and proven. The main risk is scope - building a comprehensive \"framework\" for ALL high-risk experiments could be a large project. But from my vantage point, this is exactly the kind of infrastructure that makes compliant AI development faster rather than slower. It's not speculative; it's building on well-known patterns (experiment tracking + audit logging).\n\nI'd approve this. The promise score: it's solid, technically achievable, and directly serves both compliance and research velocity. Not a 100 because the scope is