s where the proposal shines most. If the lab already has CI/CD pipelines, version control (Git), training logs, and stores hyperparameters/dataset metadata, then this is largely a matter of writing extraction scripts and template mapping. It leverages what's already there.\n- The \"during the CI/CD process\" framing means it hooks into existing workflows rather than creating parallel processes. This is efficient.\n- The risk: if the lab's current infrastructure doesn't cleanly store all required data (e.g., dataset hashes, specific validation metrics in a structured form), then building that capture layer first could be more work than described.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThis is a pragmatic, well-scoped proposal. It addresses a real recurring obligation under Article 12 and Recital 71. The approach of hooking into CI/CD is the right architectural choice — it means documentation generation becomes a byproduct of normal development workflow rather than a separate manual task. The cost is moderate (scripting + template work), the benefit is ongoing (every release, every update), and it leverages existing infrastructure rather than requiring new systems.\n\nThe main risk I see: \"standardized template\" implies someone has to define what \"compliant\" looks like structurally, and that definition may shift as guidance evolves. But that's a maintenance cost, not a blocking concern.\n\nFrom my vantage point, this is a high-value, moderate-cost proposal that directly improves resource efficiency (researcher time saved) and leverages existing infrastructure well. It's not ambitious science, but it protects the conditions under which ambitious science can happen by removing administrative drag. I'd approve this.\n\nPromise score: This is very well-aligned with my concerns. The main uncertainty is whether \"existing R&D infrastructure\" truly has all the data points needed (dataset hashes specifically, structured validation metrics) or whether additional capture work is needed up