ty, I'd be more cautious, but if it's just standard engineering dressed up as research, it's a waste of dedicated resources that could go to prototyping actual logging infrastructure.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- A \"targeted research initiative\" is inherently slower than building the logging system with sensible defaults and optimizing later. From my perspective, I'd want to prototype the full logging pipeline first (what events are captured, how they're stored, how retrieval works for compliance), THEN optimize storage if it becomes a bottleneck.\n- The proposal addresses cost concerns (\"storage costs\", \"crippled by massive log archives\") which may be premature optimization. We don't know yet what volume we'll actually generate until we build the system and see real workloads.\n- However, if this is framed as building out efficient storage layers for our logging infrastructure, there's value. But calling it \"research\" rather than \"implementation\" suggests we're not committing to a deliverable timeline.\n\n**Key concerns from my vantage point:**\n1. The cryptographic hashing approach has a fundamental tension with regulatory auditability requirements - you need to be able to show what was logged, not just prove it wasn't tampered with. This is a technical viability issue: the proposed solution may not actually satisfy Article 12's traceability requirements.\n2. \"Research initiative\" framing means no concrete deliverable, no prototype, no working system - from my prototyping perspective this is low-value commitment of resources.\n3. The problem it solves (storage cost) is real but premature - we haven't built the logging system yet to know what our actual volumes are.\n4. Standard solutions exist and can be implemented quickly without a \"research\" phase.\n\n**What's good:**\n- It correctly identifies that storage for long-term logs is a real concern, especially over the lifetime of the system (Article 12(1) says \"over the lifetime\").\n- The