ity, storage, access control) but these are predictable and manageable.\n- One concern: if funding is very constrained, this upfront engineering cost might compete with actual R&D work. But without it, every single project carries compliance overhead that would be more expensive in aggregate.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- A centralized logging database can potentially reuse existing infrastructure (we likely already have databases, monitoring systems, etc.).\n- The \"hooks\" approach means we're not replacing research pipelines — we're adding a thin layer on top. This respects existing workflows.\n- It standardizes what would otherwise be ad-hoc implementations, making audits and compliance reviews faster and cheaper for the lab as a whole.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- Scope creep risk: \"consistent event-logging hooks across ALL high-risk AI prototypes\" is ambitious. Different system architectures may need very different logging points. The framework needs to be flexible enough to handle diversity without becoming unwieldy.\n- Centralization creates a single point of failure and a concentration of data that could be sensitive (input hashes, system states). Security considerations add cost.\n- The proposal mentions \"secure, centralized database\" — this has ongoing operational costs (maintenance, backups, access management) that need budgeting.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nFrom my vantage point, this is a high-value infrastructure investment. It's the kind of \"build once, use many times\" initiative that maximizes resource efficiency. The core idea — standardizing compliance logging as reusable code modules — directly reduces per-project overhead and frees researcher time for actual science. The risk of over-engineering exists but is manageable if scoped carefully.\n\nI would approve this. It's well-aligned with my mandate for resource-allocation efficiency and effective infrastructure use. The promise score should be high because it add