than a direct engineering deliverable. From my perspective, this is more exploratory research with uncertain timelines and outcomes.\n- The scope is somewhat vague: \"explore the use of cryptographic hashing and differential privacy\" — these are well-known techniques but applying them to this specific regulatory context requires significant experimentation.\n- The phrase \"integrated into our standard logging framework\" at the end suggests an eventual integration point, but the R&D phase itself has uncertain duration and deliverables.\n- However, the problem is real and will need solving eventually. If we don't prototype solutions now, we'll be scrambling later when the regulation takes effect.\n\n**Key concerns from my vantage:**\n1. The technical tension between Article 12(3)(c) requiring actual input data recording vs. privacy-preserving transforms that might make the data unrecoverable is a genuine engineering challenge that needs prototyping to validate feasibility.\n2. However, calling it \"research\" with open-ended exploration (\"will explore\") makes it less of a clear prototyping deliverable and more of an R&D gamble.\n3. The scope could be narrower and more actionable: e.g., \"prototype a hashing-based logging mechanism that satisfies Article 12(3)(c) while minimizing raw data exposure\" would be more concretely evaluable.\n4. Differential privacy applied to discrete log entries is technically tricky — the noise parameters need calibration, and getting them wrong either destroys utility or doesn't provide meaningful protection. This is a research problem with uncertain resolution timeline.\n\n**Decision考量:**\n- Is this technically viable? Partially yes, but the full scope (combining crypto hashing AND differential privacy for multiple data types in Article 12(3)) has significant uncertainty. The core idea of using hashes instead of raw data for match records is more straightforward and prototypable. Differential privacy on logs is harder to get right.\n