g of experiment data into documentation is technically sound and achievable.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- This could genuinely speed up prototyping by reducing the manual documentation overhead that researchers face when moving from prototype to production.\n- The \"hooks into existing pipelines\" approach means it doesn't require a complete rearchitecture - it's an add-on layer.\n- Templates make it fast to implement initially, though they'll need updating as regulatory guidance evolves.\n- It addresses a real pain point: researchers don't want to spend time formatting documentation, and manual documentation is error-prone and slow.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- The proposal conflates \"logs\" (Article 12's focus on automatic event recording) with \"technical documentation\" (which is more about Annex IV content). Article 12 is specifically about the system technically allowing for automatic logging of events. The technical documentation is a separate requirement (Articles 11, 18). This proposal seems to blur these two things.\n- The \"mandated documentation format\" doesn't exist yet in a rigid sense - the Act requires certain information but the exact format will be specified through harmonized standards and guidance. Building templates now risks obsolescence.\n- However, from a prototyping speed perspective, even if formats evolve, the infrastructure for auto-extraction is valuable and reusable.\n- The \"real-time\" aspect adds engineering complexity that may not be strictly necessary - Article 12 says logs should be recorded over the lifetime, but doesn't mandate real-time export to documentation format.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThe core idea has technical merit - automating data extraction from experiment pipelines into structured reports is achievable and useful. It addresses a genuine workflow bottleneck for prototyping teams. However, there's a conceptual muddle: it conflates Article 12 logging requirements with broader technical documentation requireme