itute risks for each particular high-risk AI system.\n- For Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometrics), the logging requirements are very specific: start/end times, reference database, input data matching, identification of natural persons in verification. These can be tested mechanically. But for broader categories, \"events relevant for identifying situations that may result in the system presenting a risk\" is more open-ended and harder to reduce to automated test cases.\n- CI/CD integration is good engineering practice - catching compliance issues early in the development cycle rather than at audit time.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping Assessment:**\n- From a prototyping speed perspective, this proposal adds overhead before you even have a working prototype of the logging system itself. If you're still in early prototyping phases, building a comprehensive test suite for logging events that may not be fully defined yet is premature.\n- However, if the lab has already built (or is close to building) the logging infrastructure, then this validator accelerates iteration by giving rapid feedback on whether your changes break compliance-relevant logging.\n- The \"guarantees technical viability\" claim is overstated - a test suite can verify that specific known events are logged, but it cannot guarantee that you've identified ALL events that would constitute a risk situation. This is fundamentally an incomplete problem for fully automated testing.\n- From prototyping quality: having these tests in CI/CD does improve the quality bar of your prototypes by ensuring they maintain compliance-relevant logging through iterations.\n\n**Concerns:**\n1. Premature optimization risk: Building this validator before the core logging system is stable wastes engineering resources on a test harness for a moving target.\n2. Scope ambiguity: \"Simulates system failures and substantial modifications\" - how do you automate this? A substantial modification might be adding a new model comp