ure is efficient. If we don't yet have the logging (Article 12(1) requirement), then this proposal presupposes a prerequisite that may not be met.\n- The \"competitive advantage\" framing is a bit of sales language for an internal compliance-driven initiative. That doesn't necessarily make it bad, but it might indicate scope creep — trying to justify investment by promising more than the regulatory floor requires.\n- \"Applied specifically to system logs\" — this is good scoping. It's not proposing to rebuild logging infrastructure; it's adding a detection layer on top.\n\n**Resource efficiency assessment:**\nThe core idea (anomaly detection on existing logs to flag drift/risk events) is operationally sound and relatively contained in scope if properly bounded. However, the \"research project\" framing worries me — we should be engineering this, not researching it. Anomaly detection for log streams is mature enough that a focused engineering sprint should deliver value without requiring a prolonged R&D cycle. If this gets framed as a multi-year research program with publications and novel contributions to the literature, it will consume resources far beyond what's needed to meet Article 12(2) requirements and get operational value.\n\nThe effective use of existing R&D infrastructure point: if our team already does ML/systems work, spinning up anomaly detection on logs is a natural extension that uses existing skills and tools. We don't need new hires or major infrastructure investments for this. It's an incremental engineering effort layered on top of what we're already building (the logging required by Art 12(1)).\n\n**Balancing scientific ambition vs funding constraints:**\nThe proposal as written is reasonably bounded. \"Anomaly detection algorithms applied specifically to system logs\" is a clear scope. The risk is the word \"research\" and the aspirational language (\"competitive advantage,\" \"transforms\"). If I approve this, I need it scoped as an engineerin