        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2907\",\n      \"rationale\": \"This proposal directly operationalises the three traceability objectives in Article 12(2)(a)–(c)—detecting substantial modifications, enabling post-market monitoring under Article 72, and supporting operator oversight under Article 26(5)—by layering active log analysis on top of the passive recording that Article 12(1) mandates. The design choices (separate monitoring agent for independence, human-in-the-loop alerting to the compliance team rather than autonomous intervention, real-time threshold checks) map cleanly onto what a regulator would expect to see as evidence of a functioning risk-management and post-market surveillance regime, and the early-warning posture materially reduces our exposure to enforcement action or forced market withdrawal.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 87\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "The user asks me to screen proposal P2907 from the vantage point of a Compliance Officer at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain of concern is institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nLet me analyze this proposal:\n\n**Proposal P2907: Automated Post-Market Risk Monitor**\n- An AI-driven auditing tool that scans system logs for indicators of substantial modification or emerging operational risks\n- Acts as an early warning system\n- A separate monitoring agent analyzes log data in real-time against predefined safety and performance thresholds\n- Triggers alerts for the compliance team\n- Protects market reputation by identifying/mitigating failures before they become public regulatory breaches or liability issues\n\n**Assessment from Compliance Officer's vantage point:**\n\nStrengths:\n1. Directly addresses Article 12(2)(a) - \"identifying situations that may result in the high-risk AI system presenting a risk within the meaning of Article 79(1) or in a substantial modification\" - This is explicitly what the regulation requires logging capabilities to enable.\n2. Addresses Article 12(2)(b) - facilitating post-market monitoring referred to in Article 72.\n3. Addresses Article 12(2)(c) - monitoring operation of high-risk AI systems referred to in Article 26(5).\n4. The \"early warning\" framing aligns perfectly with the compliance function's mandate: catching issues before they become regulatory breaches.\n5. It directly supports Recital 71's emphasis on traceability, verifying compliance, and monitoring operations throughout lifetime.\n6. From a market reputation perspective, being proactive rather than reactive in compliance is critical.\n\nWeaknesses/Concerns:\n1. The proposal says \"AI-driven auditing tool\" and \"monitoring agent\" - this means we'd be building ANOTHER AI system to monitor our high-risk systems. This introduces its own regulatory complexity (though likely not high-risk itself, it still needs governance). However, from a compliance perspective, having an automated monitoring layer is actually a strength for demonstrating robust oversight.\n2. \"Predefined safety and performance thresholds\" - the question is whether these can be kept current as regulations evolve. The EU AI Act is relatively new and guidance will develop. This is manageable but requires ongoing maintenance.\n3. The proposal is somewhat high-level - it describes a concept rather than specifying implementation details. But for screening purposes, the concept itself is sound.\n4. Real-time monitoring of logs directly operationalizes Article 12(1) requirement that systems \"technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system\" - and goes further by actually analyzing those logs.\n\nFrom my compliance vantage point:\n- This is a strong alignment with the regulatory text. The EU AI Act explicitly requires logging capabilities to enable traceability, risk identification, and post-market monitoring. Having an automated system that actively monitors these logs transforms compliance from passive record-keeping to active surveillance.\n- For institutional survival: catching issues early prevents enforcement actions, fines (up to 7% of global turnover for serious violations), and mandatory withdrawal of systems from market.\n- For regulatory compliance: this directly operationalizes Articles 12, 72, 79, and 26(5). It also supports the broader conformity assessment requirements.\n- For market reputation: proactive monitoring demonstrates to regulators, clients, and partners that we take our obligations seriously.\n\nThe \"separate monitoring agent\" aspect is a good design choice from a compliance perspective - it creates separation between the system being monitored and the entity doing monitoring (analogous to internal audit independence). This strengthens our position in any regulatory inquiry.\n\nOne concern: building an AI-based monitor introduces its own risk surface. If the monitor itself fails or misses something, that could be seen as inadequate oversight. But this is a standard engineering risk, not a fundamental flaw in the proposal. The compliance benefit clearly outweighs.\n\nAnother thought: the proposal says \"triggers alerts for the compliance team\" - this is appropriate. It doesn't propose autonomous corrective action (which would raise its own governance questions) but rather flags issues to humans who make decisions. This is the right level of automation from a compliance perspective.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a highly relevant, well-aligned proposal that directly addresses multiple Article 12 requirements and supports broader compliance obligations. It's strategically important for institutional survival (avoiding fines/enforcement), regulatory compliance (operationalizing specific article requirements), and market reputation (demonstrating proactive oversight). I would approve this with high confidence.\n\nPromise score: This is squarely in my domain of concern. It operationalizes the exact requirements I'm responsible for ensuring compliance with. The early-warning framing addresses institutional survival directly. I'd give this a high score - maybe 85-90. It's not a perfect 100 because it's still at concept level and introduces an additional AI system to govern, but the core idea is exactly what my function needs.\n\nLet me settle on 87. Strong alignment, clear regulatory mapping, good design principle (separate monitoring agent, human-in-the-loop for alerts). Slight deduction for: (a) it's a new AI system that will itself need governance/compliance oversight, adding complexity; (b) \"predefined thresholds\" need ongoing maintenance to track evolving regulatory guidance.\n",
