? The proposal doesn't commit to a specific technical approach, which is fine at ideation level but suggests some uncertainty in the implementation path.\n\n2. **Speed of prototyping:**\n   - Building on existing logging infra is a plus — we don't need to build the pipeline from scratch.\n   - A \"lightweight analysis layer\" suggests it's meant to be quick to implement.\n   - However, making anomaly detection meaningful for *regulatory* risk patterns (not just technical anomalies) requires domain knowledge integration that may slow prototyping. The gap between \"detect anomalous log entries\" and \"identify situations that may result in the system presenting a risk within the meaning of Article 79(1)\" is significant.\n   - That said, as a prototype, we could start with basic statistical anomaly detection on key metrics (latency, error rates, model output distributions) and iterate. This is achievable in weeks, not months.\n\n3. **Quality:**\n   - The proposal frames this well: it transforms \"passive record-keeping into an active tool.\" From a prototyping quality standpoint, having feedback loops during development (not just after deployment) improves iteration speed and catches issues early.\n   - It directly supports Article 12(2)(b) post-market monitoring and (c) operational monitoring.\n   - The \"alerting\" aspect is valuable for the development team — catching drift during prototyping means faster correction cycles.\n\n4. **Concerns from my vantage:**\n   - The scope is somewhat broad: \"detect patterns indicative of the risks mentioned in Article 12(2)\" covers (a) risk situations/substantial modifications, (b) post-market monitoring events, and (c) operational monitoring. That's three distinct use cases that may need different detection logic.\n   - \"Real-time\" adds infrastructure complexity (stream processing vs. batch). For a prototype, near-real-time might suffice, but the proposal commits to real-time which could slow initial delivery.\n   - The connection between anomaly detection and *regulatory* risk identification is the weak link technically. Anomaly ≠ regulatory risk. We'd need a mapping layer or rules that connect detected anomalies to specific Article 12(2) categories.\n\n**Overall assessment:** This is technically viable, builds on existing infrastructure (good for speed), and has clear prototyping value for improving development iteration quality. The main technical challenge is the gap between statistical anomaly detection and regulatory risk identification, but this can be addressed incrementally in a prototype. The proposal is well-scoped enough to start building something useful quickly while leaving room to refine the detection logic. From a development/prototyping standpoint, I'd say this is solid — it's buildable, leverages existing infrastructure, and has clear near-term value for our prototyping workflow even before full regulatory compliance is achieved.\n\nI'll approve this. Promise score: I'm leaning toward 72-78. It's technically sound, builds on existing infra, has clear prototype value, but the \"regulatory risk pattern\" mapping adds some uncertainty to the technical approach that keeps it from being a slam-dunk 90+. The real-time requirement also adds complexity that could slow initial delivery. I'll go with 75.\n",
