        "reasoning_content": "The user asks me to screen a single proposal (P1348) from the vantage point of a Lab Director at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain of concern is: resource-allocation efficiency, the balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints, and effective use of existing R&D infrastructure.\n\nLet me analyze this proposal through that lens:\n\n**Proposal P1348: \"Unified Traceability Framework for High-Risk Prototypes\"**\n\nThe proposal describes building an integrated middleware layer that automatically logs model inputs, versions, and execution timestamps across all high-risk AI projects. Key elements:\n- Standardized API wrapper capturing event metadata in real time\n- No need for researchers to write custom logging code per experiment\n- Interfaces directly with existing compute clusters\n- Ensures regulatory compliance \"by default\"\n- Reduces administrative burden on scientists\n\n**From my vantage point (resource-allocation efficiency, scientific ambition vs. funding constraints, effective use of existing R&D infrastructure):**\n\nPros:\n1. **Effective use of existing infrastructure**: It explicitly interfaces with existing compute clusters rather than requiring new hardware or separate logging infrastructure. This is smart — it leverages what we already have.\n2. **Resource-allocation efficiency at scale**: A single middleware layer serving ALL high-risk projects is far more efficient than each research group building its own logging solution. The marginal cost of adding a new project to the framework should be near-zero, versus the per-project cost of custom logging.\n3. **Reduces researcher burden**: By making logging an architectural feature rather than a manual task, it frees scientists' time for actual R&D work. This directly supports the balance between scientific ambition and compliance overhead.\n4. **Scales with growth**: As we take on more high-risk AI projects (which is the direction of travel under the Act), this framework amortizes its cost across more uses. A one-time build that serves indefinitely is better than repeated per-project implementations.\n5. **Compliance by default**: Reduces the risk of a project slipping through without adequate logging, which would be costly to remediate later (regulatory penalties, rework).\n\nCons/Concerns:\n1. **Upfront resource commitment**: Building middleware is a significant engineering investment upfront. From a funding-constraint perspective, this requires dedicated engineer-hours that could go toward research deliverables. However, the \"one-time build, ongoing benefit\" profile makes it more defensible than recurring costs.\n2. **Scope risk**: \"All high-risk AI projects\" — if the lab's portfolio of high-risk systems is small (say 2-3 active projects), the overhead of building a full middleware layer might not be justified yet. But if the trajectory is growth, it's forward-looking investment.\n3. **Maintenance burden**: Middleware needs its own maintenance team. This is an ongoing cost that must be budgeted.\n4. **Technical complexity**: Building a standardized API wrapper that works across diverse model architectures and experimental setups is non-trivial. There's a risk of scope creep or the wrapper being too rigid/brittle for the diversity of research experiments.\n\nOverall assessment: This is well-aligned with my mandate. It directly addresses resource-allocation efficiency (amortized cost across projects), effective use of existing infrastructure (builds on compute clusters, not parallel systems), and supports scientific ambition by reducing compliance overhead on researchers. The main risk is upfront engineering cost and scope management, but the long-term economics are favorable, especially as the AI Act's requirements become more binding over time.\n\nI think this clears my bar. It's a high-leverage infrastructure investment that aligns well with my domain concerns. I'd give it a solid promise score — not 100 because there are execution risks and the upfront cost is real, but clearly above threshold. Maybe 78-82 range.\n\nLet me settle on:\n- Rationale: Focuses on amortizing compliance costs across all projects via shared infrastructure built atop existing compute clusters, directly reducing per-project overhead and freeing researcher time for actual science.\n- Decision: approve\n- Promise: 80 (strong alignment with my domain; main caveats are upfront engineering cost and the need to keep scope disciplined so it doesn't balloon into a multi-year platform project)\n",
