ogging down. The proposal needs to be very clear that this toggling only applies during the R&D phase where the system isn't yet a \"high-risk AI system\" under the Act.\n- The risk here is ambiguity: what if an internal pilot or limited deployment gets classified as \"put into service\" before the full logging infrastructure is in place? The \"dynamic toggle\" could create a gap where the system is technically high-risk but logging is still in \"minimal debug\" mode.\n- Article 12(3) has specific minimum requirements for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometrics). If any of our R&D touches those, even exploratory work might need to be more careful.\n- The phrase \"prevents overhead during early discovery\" — while true from a resource perspective, compliance needs to ensure that the \"early discovery\" boundary is well-defined and legally defensible. What if a regulator questions when exactly something became a high-risk system?\n- However, the mandatory audit check at transition is actually a strong compliance control. It creates accountability and documentation of the handoff point.\n\n**Overall assessment from Compliance Officer perspective:**\nThis is a reasonable governance framework that aligns with how I'd want to structure compliance obligations across project lifecycles. The key risk is the \"dynamic toggle\" language — it could be misinterpreted or misapplied if not carefully bounded. But as a framework concept, it's sound: you don't need full Article 12 infrastructure for a paper prototype, but you DO need clear triggers and documented gates before anything becomes subject to those obligations.\n\nThe mandatory audit check at transition is the critical compliance control here. Without it, this would be more risky. With it, it provides a defensible boundary.\n\nHowever, I'm somewhat cautious because:\n1. \"Dynamically toggled\" implies runtime switching which could create compliance gaps if not carefully engineered\n2. The proposal doesn't explicitly address what happens during the transition period itself — is there a buffer? What if the audit check fails?\n3. It doesn't mention retention periods, which are critical for record-keeping compliance\n\nBut overall, as a framework concept that creates clear governance gates and documented transitions, this aligns well with compliance objectives. It's not perfect but it's directionally correct and addresses a real operational concern (not building full regulatory infrastructure before you know the system will be high-risk).\n\nI'd lean toward approving this because:\n- It establishes a governance structure for when compliance obligations kick in\n- The mandatory audit check is a strong control\n- It prevents resource waste while maintaining a documented path to compliance\n- The \"documented transition\" language is exactly what regulators want to see\n\nBut I'm not at 100% because of the \"dynamic toggle\" risk and the lack of explicit handling for edge cases (what if you're in limbo between R&D and deployment? What about the retention clock?).\n\nI'll approve with a moderate-to-high promise score. Maybe 72-78 range. The concept is sound and the audit gate is good, but there are implementation risks around the \"dynamic\" nature and edge cases that need to be addressed before this is truly bulletproof from a compliance standpoint.\n\nLet me settle on: approve, 74.\n",
