e.\n5. From my perspective: A mandatory committee gate before every project could actually HURT speed of prototyping. Every new idea goes through a bureaucratic bottleneck before engineers can even start building. This adds latency to the development cycle.\n6. However, the counter-argument: if you don't know whether logging is required until after you've built the system, you'll have significant rework. The cost of a 1-week pre-screening vs. months of rearchitecting to add comprehensive logging retroactively.\n\nLet me weigh this:\n- It's not technically viable as an \"engineering solution\" per se — it's a process/governance mechanism.\n- From speed/quality perspective, adding a mandatory committee gate for EVERY project (not just potentially high-risk ones) creates friction. The proposal says \"every new AI project.\" That's broad.\n- It doesn't address the actual technical implementation of logging.\n- But it does have a legitimate upstream concern: scoping requirements before prototyping saves rework.\n\nHowever, my domain is specifically \"technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\" This proposal:\n- Is not a technical solution (it's an organizational process)\n- Could slow down prototyping by adding mandatory gates to every project\n- Doesn't provide any engineering substance for actually implementing Article 12 logging\n- The \"multidisciplinary committee\" is a governance mechanism, not something I'd typically evaluate on technical viability\n\nI think from my vantage point, this is largely out of scope. It's a legal/governance process, not a technical solution. While I can see the upstream scoping benefit, the proposal as written adds bureaucratic friction without providing any technical architecture or engineering approach to actually solving the logging problem. For every project (not just potentially high-risk ones), this gate would slow down prototyping cycles.\n\nMy assessment: This is more of a legal/compliance process than a