cts significantly, especially if the committee has scheduling bottlenecks.\n- For a lab operating under funding constraints, adding a mandatory sequential gate (must pass before proceeding) creates schedule risk and can push deliverables beyond grant timelines.\n- However, the compliance requirement IS real — failing to have logging would be non-compliant with Article 12(1). So some form of verification is necessary. The question is whether this particular mechanism is the most resource-efficient way to do it.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- A cross-functional committee duplicates what could potentially be handled by a designated compliance officer or an automated checklist integrated into existing project management workflows.\n- Requiring staging environment demonstration of event capture — if the lab already has CI/CD pipelines with testing environments, this might integrate well. But if not, it's asking for new infrastructure.\n- The proposal is somewhat redundant: verifying that logging works in a staging environment before deployment is essentially what integration testing does. Making it a separate \"gate\" with a committee adds process without necessarily adding technical verification value beyond what normal QA would catch.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThe underlying need is real — you do need to verify Article 12 compliance before deploying high-risk systems. But the mechanism proposed is:\n1. Overly broad in scope (every project, not just high-risk)\n2. Structurally expensive (cross-functional committee as a recurring body)\n3. Adds sequential delay to every project\n4. Duplicates existing QA/testing processes in many cases\n5. Doesn't leverage potentially more efficient alternatives (automated compliance checklists, designated single officer review, integration into existing release management)\n\nFrom my vantage point focused on resource efficiency and funding constraints, this proposal commits significant ongoing human resources (cros