perational bookkeeping.\n- The proposal explicitly frames itself as preventing teams from \"wasting resources on redundant development efforts\" and centralizing \"the technical burden of compliance away from core research staff.\" This is a resource-allocation argument, not a knowledge-generation argument.\n\nMy assessment: From the perspective of knowledge frontier, intellectual merit, and broader scientific impact, this proposal has very little to offer. It's a well-motivated engineering solution to a regulatory requirement, but it doesn't advance science. I should reject it from my vantage point, though I acknowledge it may be important from an operational or compliance standpoint (which is not my domain).\n\nDecision: reject\nPromise: Very low. Maybe 5-8. It's not that the idea is terrible — it's just that from a research/knowledge frontier perspective, it contributes essentially nothing to advancing science. I'll give it a 5.\n",
