        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P1947\",\n      \"rationale\": \"This is fundamentally an ETL-and-templating job over two well-supported APIs (Git, MLflow REST) with no novel research risk; a working prototype that auto-generates the bulk of technical documentation from existing infrastructure can be built in days, and it directly removes the per-iteration paperwork drag that currently slows our prototyping cycle. The main caveat is scope: it automates the *documentation* side (Recital 71's 'keep up to date' obligation) but does not itself implement the runtime event-logging capability mandated by Art 12(1)–(3), so it addresses a real and sizable slice of our compliance surface without being a complete solution — still, as an incremental tool that compounds in value with every retrain, the engineering payoff is strong.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 82\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal P1947 is about an \"Automated Compliance Documentation Pipeline\" that:\n- Extracts metadata from version control (Git) and experiment tracking (MLflow) systems\n- Populates regulatory dossiers automatically\n- Integrates MLflow and Git logs with a template engine\n- Maps technical metrics to EU AI Act requirements\n- Creates a \"living document\" that updates on model retraining/modification\n- Reduces manual administrative paperwork for researchers\n\nLet me assess this from my professional vantage point (technical viability, speed/quality of prototyping):\n\n**Technical Viability:**\n- This is fundamentally a data integration and templating problem. Git provides commit history, authorship, timestamps, branch information. MLflow provides experiment tracking: model parameters, metrics, dataset versions, run IDs, etc. These are well-established APIs with stable interfaces.\n- The \"template engine\" mapping technical metrics to EU AI Act requirements is the core challenge. This requires someone to define the mapping (which Git/MLflow fields correspond to which regulatory documentation requirements). This is a finite, enumerable task for a specific set of high-risk systems.\n- The \"living document that updates whenever a model is retrained or modified\" is technically straightforward: hook into CI/CD pipeline events (model push, experiment completion) and trigger regeneration of the relevant sections.\n- However, there's a gap: Article 12 requires *automatic recording of events (logs)* — this proposal seems more about documentation generation than about the logging capability itself. The logging (start/end times, reference databases, input data matches, natural person identification for Annex III point 1(a) systems) is a different technical problem that this pipeline would consume but not produce.\n- Still, from a prototyping perspective, the core idea — stitching together existing telemetry into a structured document — is very feasible. The hard part (defining the mapping between internal artifacts and regulatory fields) is a one-time domain knowledge task, then it's maintainable.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- This is a high-leverage tool for our team. If we build this once, every subsequent model iteration automatically updates its documentation. The prototyping speed benefit compounds: researchers don't lose time to paperwork between iterations.\n- A prototype could be built quickly: a script that reads MLflow run metadata + Git commit history and outputs a markdown/PDF document matching a template. That's days of work for an MVP, not months.\n- Quality risk: the mapping from internal metrics to \"EU AI Act requirements\" is somewhat fuzzy initially. The regulation says documentation should include \"general characteristics, capabilities and limitations of the system, algorithms, data, training, testing and validation processes used as well as documentation on the relevant risk-management system.\" Some of this (risk management system docs) won't live in Git/MLflow naturally. So the tool would be partial — it handles the technical/experimentation metadata well but not everything in Recital 71.\n- This is still a strong prototype candidate because the parts it CAN automate are exactly the parts that are most repetitive and time-consuming (version histories, training parameters, dataset versions, metric snapshots). The risk-management documentation would need to be supplemented manually or by other tools, but that's a smaller slice of the total documentation burden.\n\n**Overall assessment from my vantage point:**\n- Technically very viable: it's an integration/ETL problem with well-defined inputs (Git API, MLflow REST API) and structured outputs (templated document). No novel AI/ML research needed.\n- High prototyping speed payoff: directly reduces the administrative overhead that slows down our iteration cycles. The \"living document\" aspect means we don't have to re-do documentation each time we ship a new model version.\n- Moderate scope risk: it won't cover 100% of Article 12 requirements (especially the runtime logging of events for Annex III 1(a) systems, or the natural person identification), but as a starting pipeline that auto-populates the bulk of technical documentation from existing infrastructure, it's high-value.\n- The \"template engine mapping to EU AI Act requirements\" is the one part that needs domain expertise (legal/regulatory knowledge) to define correctly. But once defined, it's a static configuration, not an ongoing research problem.\n\nI'm leaning toward approve. It's technically feasible, directly addresses a pain point in our prototyping workflow, has a clear MVP path, and leverages infrastructure we already have. The promise score should be high but not 100 because of the partial-coverage concern (it handles documentation metadata well but doesn't solve the runtime logging requirement of Art 12(1) itself).\n\nI'll say approve with a promise of around 82.\n",
