data and hyperparameters\" which is a subset. Does it cover the full Recital 71 scope?\n- The proposal addresses Recital 71 (documentation up-to-dateness) well but Article 12's core requirement is about automatic recording of events (logs) over lifetime, with specific logging capabilities in paragraph 3 for Annex III point 1(a) systems. The proposal seems to conflate technical documentation with logging capability. These are related but distinct requirements under the Act.\n- However, the proposal as stated is specifically about the documentation pipeline, which IS a valid and important component of compliance. It's not trying to solve Article 12(1)-(3) logging per se, but rather the Recital 71 documentation maintenance requirement that supports traceability.\n\n**Resource efficiency assessment:**\n- The core mechanism (Git hooks + extraction scripts + portal sync) is relatively straightforward engineering work if we already have Git-based workflows (which an R&D lab developing AI systems almost certainly does)\n- This converts a recurring manual compliance cost into a one-time automation investment - excellent ROI profile\n- It reduces ongoing staff time that would otherwise be spent updating documentation manually\n- The \"versioned, machine-readable files\" approach is more scalable than maintaining static PDFs or Word docs\n\n**Ambition vs. funding balance:**\n- This is appropriately scoped - it's not trying to reinvent our ML infrastructure, just adding an automation layer on top of existing Git workflows\n- It's a compliance necessity, not optional ambition\n- The implementation cost seems moderate relative to the ongoing manual effort it replaces\n\n**Infrastructure use:**\n- Leverages existing Git repos (high efficiency)\n- The \"centralized documentation portal\" could be new or existing - slight ambiguity but likely can map to something we have\n- Automated scripts are low-cost to develop and maintain\n\nOverall: This is a well-scoped, infrastructure-efficient proposal that addresses a real regulatory requirement using automation to reduce ongoing manual overhead. It leverages what we already have (Git) and adds a thin automation layer. From my resource-allocation perspective, this is high-value-per-effort. The main caveat is scope - \"model metadata and hyperparameters\" might not capture the full Recital 71 documentation requirements (risk management system documentation, training/testing/validation process details, etc.), but as a pipeline framework, it can be extended.\n\nI'll approve this with a solid promise score. It's efficient, leverages existing infrastructure, converts recurring cost to one-time investment, and addresses a genuine regulatory obligation. I'd say around 82-85.\n",
