c contribution.\n\n4. **Is it well-reasoned from a research standpoint?** \n   - The 5% figure appears arbitrary. There's no justification for why 5% specifically (vs. 3%, vs. 10%, vs. project-specific allocation).\n   - \"Mandatory\" across ALL project grants is a blunt instrument. Some projects may need more, some less. A one-size-fits-all percentage doesn't reflect the varying logging complexity of different AI systems.\n   - The framing of \"compliance creep\" as something to be prevented by a fixed buffer assumes that without this mechanism, research would suffer. But well-managed labs already account for operational overhead in project planning.\n   - It treats Article 12 compliance as purely a performance/latency concern (\"performance cost of continuous logging,\" \"degrade system latency or throughput\"). From a research perspective, the more important aspects of Article 12 are the *content* and *quality* of what's logged (recital 71 mentions comprehensibility, technical documentation up to date, risk management documentation) — not just that logging doesn't slow things down.\n\n5. **Does it help or hinder the knowledge frontier?** \n   - It's neutral at best for scientific progress\n   - A mandatory fixed percentage could actually be slightly negative: if a project is at the cutting edge with novel architectures where logging requirements are genuinely unclear, a rigid 5% buffer might underfund (or overfund) the compliance work needed\n   - It doesn't address the *research* challenges of designing logging systems that capture scientifically meaningful events while being technically feasible\n\n6. **Intellectual merit:** The proposal has low intellectual content. It's a budgeting policy dressed in regulatory language. The \"promise\" framing (\"maintains scientific ambition by explicitly funding the operational costs of transparency\") is rhetorical — allocating 5% of compute to logging doesn't inherently maintain or enhance scientific ambition.\n\n**My ass