        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as Compliance Officer of an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain of concern is: institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nThe proposal P1990 proposes a \"Telemetry Research for High-Risk Event Detection\" initiative focused on:\n- Defining what constitutes a recordable event in complex neural networks to satisfy Article 12(2)\n- Mapping internal state changes to external risk triggers\n- Developing new probe-based logging techniques that trigger high-resolution data captures when the system enters an unstable latent space\n- Transforming logs into diagnostic scientific data\n- Advancing intellectual merit in AI safety and interpretability\n\nLet me assess this from a compliance perspective:\n\n**Strengths for compliance:**\n- It directly addresses Article 12(2), which requires logging capabilities to record events relevant to identifying situations that may result in the system presenting a risk, facilitating post-market monitoring, and monitoring operation.\n- The concept of mapping \"internal state changes to external risk triggers\" aligns with Article 12(2)(a) - identifying situations that may result in the high-risk AI system presenting a risk within the meaning of Article 79(1).\n- It could support post-market monitoring (Article 72) and operation monitoring (Article 26(5)).\n\n**Concerns from a compliance perspective:**\n- This is framed as a \"research initiative\" - it's exploratory, not an implementation plan. The regulation requires that high-risk AI systems SHALL technically allow for automatic recording of events. This isn't optional or aspirational; it's a mandatory technical requirement. A research project doesn't satisfy the obligation to have logging capabilities in place by the applicable deadline.\n- The proposal focuses on \"defining what constitutes a recordable event\" - this is somewhat problematic because Article 12(2) already defines what must be recorded (events relevant to risk identification, post-market monitoring, and operation monitoring). For Annex III point 1(a) systems, Article 12(3) provides specific minimum requirements. The regulation doesn't need us to \"define\" these - they're defined. What we need is implementation.\n- \"Probe-based logging techniques that trigger high-resolution data captures when the system enters an unstable latent space\" - this is a very specific technical approach that may or may not be appropriate. Compliance requires reliable, predictable logging. Triggering on \"unstable latent space\" detection introduces complexity and potential for missing events if the instability detection itself fails. The regulation requires automatic recording of events over the lifetime - it doesn't specify that we need to detect internal instability as a trigger mechanism.\n- The framing around \"advancing intellectual merit\" and \"transforming logs into diagnostic scientific data\" suggests this is motivated more by research prestige than compliance obligation. From my vantage point, I care about whether we meet the regulatory requirement reliably and defensibly before regulators, not about generating publishable research.\n- There's a risk that committing resources to this exploratory research initiative delays or substitutes for straightforward implementation of the mandatory logging requirements. The regulation is clear: systems SHALL technically allow for automatic recording. We need a compliance-ready solution, not a research project.\n\n**However**, there are some elements worth noting:\n- Understanding what constitutes a \"risk\" event (Article 79(1) reference) does require some interpretation and technical understanding. A structured effort to define risk triggers could have compliance value.\n- But the proposal as written is too speculative, too research-oriented, and doesn't clearly commit to delivering a compliance-ready logging system within required timelines.\n\n**My assessment:** This proposal, while touching on relevant compliance territory, is framed as exploratory research rather than a compliance implementation plan. The regulation demands specific technical capabilities in deployed systems - it doesn't fund or encourage research into how best to achieve them (though that research may be necessary as part of development). The \"unstable latent space\" trigger mechanism introduces unnecessary complexity and potential failure modes for a mandatory compliance requirement. The intellectual merit framing is not relevant to my compliance assessment. I need solutions that are defensible, reliable, and meet the specific textual requirements of Article 12(2) and (3).\n\nI'll reject this but give it a moderate promise score since some of the underlying thinking about mapping internal states to external risk triggers could inform a proper compliance implementation if restructured.\n",
