g R&D infrastructure**: A \"secure, immutable central database\" might require new hardware/storage, but a modular API layer can be integrated into existing CI/CD pipelines and model serving infrastructure. The \"standardized software layer integrated into all R&D pipelines\" suggests it's being built INTO what already exists rather than creating parallel systems. However, I should note: the proposal says \"Our engineering team will develop\" — this is an internal build, which means committing scarce engineering talent away from other tasks. But for a lab that develops multiple high-risk AI systems, this is clearly the most efficient path versus each team building their own logging.\n\n**Potential concerns from my vantage:**\n- The upfront cost of building and maintaining this infrastructure is non-trivial. Engineering time diverted to build it is engineering time not spent on model development or other projects.\n- \"Immutable central database\" — storage costs scale with number of models, log volume, retention periods. This could become an ongoing operational expense that strains budget.\n- The proposal says \"all R&D pipelines\" and \"every high-risk model\" — this is a broad commitment. If the lab's portfolio shifts, some of this infrastructure may be underutilized.\n- However, Article 12 is mandatory for high-risk systems. You HAVE to do this. The question is whether you do it once centrally or many times per-project. Centralized is clearly more efficient.\n\n**Overall assessment**: This proposal directly addresses the regulatory requirement in the most resource-efficient way possible for a multi-project lab. It converts a per-project recurring cost into a one-time infrastructure investment with low marginal cost per additional model. It protects researcher time (scientific ambition) by offloading compliance mechanics to an automated layer. The main risk is upfront engineering commitment and ongoing storage/maintenance costs, but these are bounded and amortizable. From a resource-allocation perspective, this is the right call — it's the highest-leverage single investment for regulatory compliance across the lab's portfolio.\n\nI'd rate this fairly high. It's well-aligned with my domain concerns. The main reservation is the upfront cost and the assumption that \"all R&D pipelines\" can be standardized without too much friction, but the modular API approach mitigates that. I'll give it a solid 82-85 range. Let me settle on 83.\n\nDecision: approve.\n",
