ion\" — this means it scales with our R&D output rather than requiring proportional human effort. Good marginal cost structure.\n\nConcerns/risks:\n- The template engine needs to be maintained as the EU AI Act evolves, as our systems evolve, and as interpretation shifts. This is an ongoing maintenance cost that must be budgeted.\n- \"Custom\" template engine — how custom? If it's overly tailored to one specific model architecture or experiment type, it could become a bottleneck rather than an enabler. But if it's truly mapping from standardized metadata fields, it should generalize well.\n- Integration complexity: connecting existing ML tracking tools to a documentation pipeline requires understanding both systems' data models. This is real engineering work upfront.\n- However, the upfront cost is bounded and one-time (or near-one-time), while the recurring benefit is continuous savings on every model iteration we produce.\n\nAssessment from my domain:\nThis proposal is very well-aligned with my concerns. It:\n1. Uses existing infrastructure (ML experiment tracking) rather than building new — efficient resource use\n2. Reduces ongoing compliance overhead that would otherwise eat into research time — protects scientific ambition\n3. Has bounded upfront investment for unbounded recurring benefit — good ROI structure\n4. Is a focused engineering task, not an open-ended research bet — predictable cost\n\nThe main risk is scope creep in the \"template engine\" — if it tries to be too clever or cover every possible edge case of the regulation from day one, costs balloon. But as stated, mapping metadata fields to Recital 71 requirements is a well-defined problem.\n\nI would approve this. It's a high-leverage use of existing infrastructure that directly protects research throughput against compliance drag. The promise score should be high — maybe 82-88 range. I'll say 85. It's not a 100 because the \"custom template engine\" has some maintenance risk and the integration wor