cation suite before you have a working prototype to test against. It's more of a mid-to-late pipeline tool.\n- However, from a TDD/spec-driven development angle, having the test suite defined early could help shape the logging architecture. But in practice, at prototyping speed, this might add overhead before the core system is even functional.\n- For quality: yes, it catches broken/incomplete logging before deployment. This is valuable but it's a QA gate, not a development accelerator. It prevents bugs from reaching production.\n- The risk: if the synthetic failure scenarios don't actually trigger all code paths that would generate logs in production, you get false confidence. Building realistic \"synthetic failure scenarios\" for AI systems can be non-trivial — what does a \"failure scenario\" look like for an LLM-based biometric verification system? This could become a significant engineering effort with diminishing returns if the scenarios are too simplistic.\n\n**Assessment:**\n- Technically viable: Yes, this is buildable with standard testing frameworks.\n- Useful for prototyping speed: Marginally. It's more of a gate/verification tool than an enabler of faster development. During rapid prototyping, you might skip this and add it later. But having it ready when the prototype matures is valuable.\n- Quality improvement: Yes, meaningful quality gate for compliance-critical logging paths.\n- Resource commitment: Moderate. You need someone to define test scenarios, build the harness, maintain it as the system evolves. Not enormous but not trivial either.\n\nI think this clears my bar. It's a technically sound, well-scoped engineering artifact that addresses a real gap (verifying that your logging actually works as the regulation requires before you ship). The main concern is whether \"synthetic failure scenarios\" will be meaningful or just checkbox exercises, but the overall direction is right. From a prototyping speed perspective, I'd want it available in the mid-pipeline rather than blocking early exploration, but as a suite that gets applied when prototypes mature into deployment candidates, it's a good investment.\n\nI'll approve with a moderate-high promise score. It's useful and technically sound, but it's not the highest-leverage thing I could be working on during rapid prototyping phases. It's more of a quality gate. I'd say around 72-78. Let me settle on 75.\n",
