ild your own logging\" task from every project's critical path. This is significant - researchers don't need to think about compliance logging during prototyping, they just wire in the library.\n- PRO: Consistency means when one team learns the pattern, it transfers. Reduces onboarding friction for new projects.\n- CON: There's a bootstrap cost. The infrastructure team needs to build and stabilize this framework BEFORE any project can use it. This is a lead-time risk - if the framework isn't ready when Project X starts, either they wait or they build ad-hoc solutions (defeating the purpose).\n- CON: \"Mandated as dependencies\" creates coupling. If the framework has bugs, poor API design, or performance issues, ALL projects are affected. This is a single point of failure for prototyping speed.\n- CON: During early prototyping, requirements are still fluid. A rigid mandated framework might slow down experimentation if the logging interface doesn't match how the researcher is actually thinking about their system at that stage. The tension between \"compliance-ready from day one\" and \"rapid iteration during prototyping\" is real.\n- However, the modular design mitigates some of this - you can start with basic timestamp/user-ID capture and add reference database tracking as the system stabilizes.\n\n**Overall Assessment:**\nFrom a dev engineering perspective, this is a solid, well-motivated infrastructure investment. The technical approach (shared library with modular APIs) is standard practice and low-risk. It directly addresses a recurring pain point (every project rebuilding logging). The main risk is the initial development lead time and the potential rigidity during early prototyping phases, but these are manageable with good API design and a phased adoption strategy.\n\nThe proposal is technically sound, well-scoped, and addresses a real efficiency gain. I'd approve it. It's not a moonshot - it's pragmatic infrastructure work that pays dividends across multiple 