egression testing — every code change gets validated against logging requirements. This is good engineering hygiene.\n\n**Weaknesses / Concerns:**\n- The phrase \"technical guarantee\" is slightly overstated. A test suite can verify current behavior but doesn't guarantee future compliance if requirements change or if novel failure modes emerge that aren't in the test cases. However, this is a minor semantic issue.\n- \"Simulate high-risk events\" — this could be complex depending on the system. If you're dealing with facial recognition (Annex III 1(a)), simulating what constitutes a \"high-risk event\" requires domain-specific understanding and might require synthetic data generation that's non-trivial. But for the logging layer specifically, you can simulate at the input level without needing full end-to-end high-risk scenarios.\n- The scope is clear and bounded — it's about the logging subsystem, not about building the AI system itself. This makes it technically tractable.\n- From a prototyping speed perspective: this is medium complexity. Not trivial (you need to understand the Article 12 requirements in detail, map them to test assertions, build fault injection), but not a research problem either. It's applied engineering with clear acceptance criteria.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThis is a technically viable, well-scoped engineering proposal. It's essentially \"write integration tests for your logging infrastructure and add fault-injection scenarios.\" The technical challenges are well-understood. It directly serves the regulatory requirement. From my vantage point of prototyping speed and quality, this is achievable with a small team in a reasonable timeframe (weeks to a couple months depending on system complexity). It's not a moonshot — it's solid engineering work that has clear success criteria.\n\nThe main risk is scope creep: \"simulating high-risk events\" could balloon if interpreted too broadly. But the core ask — verify logs are produced and contain required fields under various conditions — is very doable.\n\nI'd rate this as a solid, approvable proposal from my technical viability standpoint. It's not groundbreaking but it's necessary, well-defined, and technically sound. I'd give it a high promise score because it's exactly the kind of engineering work that makes prototyping faster (you know your logging works before you ship) and ensures quality (automated regression against compliance requirements).\n\nPromise: I'd say around 82-85. It's clearly viable, well-scoped, and useful. The minor caveat is that \"simulating high-risk events\" could be more complex than stated depending on the system, but for the logging layer specifically, it's manageable.\n",
