        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal is: \"Research into Semantic Traceability for AI Forensics\" - essentially an academic research project to log latent space activations and attention maps during critical decision-making nodes, with automated anomaly detection analysis.\n\nLet me assess this from my professional perspective:\n\n**Technical Viability:**\n- Logging latent space activations and attention maps is technically feasible in principle, but it's an open research problem. There's no established methodology for \"logging internal state transitions\" in a way that reliably captures \"why a system reached a specific conclusion.\" This is essentially asking us to solve one of the hardest problems in AI interpretability as part of a compliance engineering task.\n- The proposal frames this as \"research\" and \"new methodologies\" - which signals it's exploratory, not implementable. From my vantage point, I care about prototyping speed and technical viability. This is neither quickly prototypeable nor technically settled.\n- Attention maps are not fully interpretable themselves. They're a proxy that may or may not correlate with the actual reasoning pathway. Logging them gives you data but doesn't necessarily give you \"semantic traceability\" in any meaningful, auditable sense.\n- The scale problem: logging latent space activations across all critical decision nodes for production high-risk systems would create enormous data volumes and computational overhead. This isn't a trivial engineering task.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- This is explicitly a \"scientific project\" with researchers designing \"new methodologies.\" That's not prototyping - that's R&D exploration. My mandate is about technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping for the lab's implementation pipeline.\n- There's no clear prototype here. What would a working prototype look like? The proposal doesn't define a deliverable. It says \"researchers will design new methodologies\" - that could take years with uncertain outcomes.\n- Article 12 requires \"automatic recording of events (logs)\" - it doesn't require interpretability research. A straightforward event log system with timestamps, input/output records, and metadata would satisfy the letter of the regulation. This proposal is solving a problem far beyond what's required.\n\n**Regulatory fit:**\n- The EU AI Act Article 12 asks for logs that enable traceability, post-market monitoring, and identifying risk situations. It does not require logging latent space activations or attention maps. That's an interpretability concern, not a record-keeping concern per se.\n- The proposal conflates \"traceability of functioning\" (which the regulation means) with \"semantic traceability\" / model interpretability (which is a much harder and different problem).\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom my vantage point as a Development Engineer concerned with technical viability and prototyping speed/quality:\n1. This is not technically viable as a near-term deliverable. It's an open research question.\n2. It cannot be quickly prototyped because the methodology doesn't exist yet.\n3. It over-engineers relative to what Article 12 actually requires. The regulation asks for event logs, not interpretability logging.\n4. The \"research\" framing means it has no clear engineering deliverable or prototype milestone.\n5. Committing implementation resources to this would be committing them to an open-ended research project with uncertain technical outcomes, which contradicts my mandate of building viable, prototypeable solutions.\n\nThis is a reject from my vantage point. It's not that the research isn't interesting - it clearly has intellectual value for interpretability. But my job is to screen for technical viability and prototyping readiness. This fails both tests. I'd give it a low promise score because while the underlying question is relevant to AI systems, as an implementation commitment it would consume resources with no clear path to a working prototype or deliverable that satisfies Article 12.\n\nPromise: Maybe 15-20. The intellectual direction isn't wrong - understanding why systems make decisions does help with post-market monitoring and risk identification. But from a pure prototyping/technical viability standpoint, it's not something I can build a timeline for, prototype quickly, or guarantee works. It's research, not engineering. I'll go with 15.\n",
