ototyping within a broader logging architecture build-out.\n- However, there IS value in getting this right early: if you architect your AI system's logging layer from scratch without considering performance implications at high frequency (especially for biometric matching systems that log every query), you might end up with an unworkable prototype that logs terabytes per day. So there is a legitimate prototyping concern here.\n- The framing \"ensures our limited infrastructure remains dedicated to AI development rather than regulatory telemetry\" suggests this is about protecting compute resources, which is a valid engineering constraint but feels somewhat like rationalizing underinvestment in compliance infrastructure.\n\n**Key concerns from my vantage point:**\n1. This is not really a \"research\" problem - it's an engineering optimization with well-known solutions (async logging, compression, sampling, tiered storage). Calling it a \"targeted research project\" overstates the uncertainty and justifies dedicating R&D resources to what should be a standard engineering task.\n2. The focus on \"lowest cost point that meets legal requirements\" is technically risky - you need headroom for interpretation changes, regulator requests, and edge cases in Article 12(2)(a) about identifying risk situations. Over-optimizing logging could mean missing events that matter for traceability.\n3. For prototyping speed: actually, a reasonable default logging architecture (structured JSON logs to a time-series database with compression) is straightforward to prototype quickly. You don't need a dedicated study to get 80% of the way there. The last 20% optimization can happen during production hardening.\n4. However, for high-frequency biometric systems specifically (Annex III 1a), the logging volume IS genuinely challenging - you're recording every match/non-match against a reference database at potentially very high throughput. Getting the architecture right early does save rework in prototyping.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThe proposal has some legitimate technical merit for the specific case of high-frequency biometric logging, but it's over-scoped as a \"research project\" when it's really an engineering optimization task with well-known solutions. The framing around \"lowest cost point that meets legal requirements\" introduces compliance risk. For prototyping speed, you'd be better off building a reasonable default logging architecture quickly and optimizing later rather than spending time on a dedicated benchmarking study first. It would slow down our ability to get working prototypes of the actual AI systems in front of stakeholders.\n\nI'll lean toward reject with a moderate promise score - there's some validity but it's misframed and would consume prototyping bandwidth that could be better spent on the actual AI system development.\n",
