        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2278\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Article 12's obligations attach only to 'high-risk AI systems,' yet the Act provides no automatic classification mechanism — that determination is the provider's responsibility. Without a formal, documented, and repeatable categorization gate at design-phase entry, the lab cannot even begin to scope its logging duties under Article 12(1)–(3), exposes itself to enforcement risk (misclassified high-risk system operating without mandated logs → up to €35 M / 7 % turnover fines), and forfeits a defensible institutional record should a market-surveillance authority later challenge our classification. The proposal's design-phase timing is critical because retrofitting automatic event-recording into an already-architected system is orders of magnitude more costly and technically disruptive than specifying it from the outset; the pre-funding gate also creates an audit trail that directly supports our position in any regulatory inquiry.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 93\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "The user asks me to screen proposal P2278 from the vantage point of a Compliance Officer at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain of concern is institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nLet me analyze this proposal:\n\n\"AI Risk Categorization Protocol. A formal institutional process to determine if a research project falls under the High-Risk classification defined in the Act. This prevents the lab from over-engineering low-risk systems while ensuring high-risk ones are captured. We will establish a compliance review board that evaluates every new project against Annex III criteria during the initial design phase. Each project will receive a formal risk certification before receiving internal funding. It optimizes R&D efficiency by focusing regulatory resources only where they are legally necessary.\"\n\nFrom my Compliance Officer vantage point:\n\n**Rationale:**\nThis proposal is fundamentally about ensuring the lab doesn't miss its obligations under the Act. Article 12 applies to \"high-risk AI systems\" — but determining whether a system IS high-risk is a prerequisite to knowing whether Article 12 (and all other substantive requirements) even apply. Without a formal categorization process, the lab risks:\n- Operating a high-risk system without implementing required logging capabilities → direct violation of Article 12(1), potential fines up to €35M or 7% of global turnover\n- Inconsistent application of compliance obligations across projects\n- No institutional memory of why certain systems were classified as non-high-risk (critical if the classification is later challenged by a market surveillance authority)\n\nThe proposal establishes:\n1. A formal process (not ad-hoc) — good for defensibility\n2. A compliance review board — multi-perspective, reduces single-point-of-failure in judgment\n3. Evaluation against Annex III criteria during initial design phase — early intervention is critical because retrofitting logging into a system post-design is far more expensive and technically challenging\n4. Formal risk certification before internal funding — creates an audit trail of the classification decision\n\nThe \"over-engineering low-risk systems\" framing is from an efficiency perspective, but from compliance's view, the key value is: (a) ensuring nothing high-risk slips through unregulated, and (b) creating a documented institutional record that supports our position if a regulator questions why we did or didn't implement certain controls.\n\nPotential concerns:\n- The proposal says \"Annex III criteria\" but Annex I covers products regulated under other EU harmonization legislation where the AI system is a safety component. A thorough process would also consider Annex I. However, for an R&D institution (not a product manufacturer integrating into machinery/medical devices), Annex III is likely the more relevant one, so this isn't necessarily a flaw.\n- The proposal doesn't explicitly mention that re-assessment should happen if a system's intended purpose changes (which could shift it from non-high-risk to high-risk). But this is an implementation detail, not a fundamental flaw in the proposal itself.\n- \"Before receiving internal funding\" — this creates a gate. From compliance perspective, this is excellent because it means you can't deploy without classification.\n\nOverall: This is strongly aligned with my professional mandate. It's a foundational prerequisite to ANY compliance work under Article 12 (or any other article). Without knowing whether your system is high-risk, you cannot even begin to comply. The institutional survival angle is clear — a misclassification that goes undetected until a market surveillance audit could be catastrophic for the institution.\n\nI'd rate this highly. It's not just \"nice to have\" — it's a prerequisite for everything else in my compliance function. I'll approve with a high promise score, maybe 92-95. The slight deduction is because it focuses on initial classification but doesn't explicitly address re-classification triggers (purpose changes, substantial modifications under Article 12(2)(a)), and the Annex III-only framing might miss edge cases. But these are implementation refinements, not structural problems with the proposal.\n\nLet me settle on: approve, promise 93.\n",
