hers get better reproducibility records for free as a side effect.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- The key insight here is \"link our version control systems to an automated reporting tool.\" If the lab already has VCS (which any serious R&D institution would), this proposal builds on that rather than creating parallel systems.\n- However, \"an integrated system\" and \"automated reporting tool\" could range from a lightweight script wrapping existing tools to a heavy bespoke platform. The proposal is somewhat vague on implementation scope.\n\n**Regulatory fit (Article 12 / Recital 71):**\n- Recital 71 specifically calls for: general characteristics, capabilities and limitations, algorithms, data, training, testing and validation processes, risk-management documentation.\n- Article 12(1) requires automatic recording of events/logs over the lifetime.\n- The proposal addresses the \"technical documentation\" aspect (Recital 71's second paragraph) well - capturing training data provenance, validation metrics, hyperparameters, dataset versions.\n- However, it doesn't explicitly address the \"automatic recording of events (logs)\" requirement in Article 12(1) as distinct from the technical documentation. The logging capability for identifying risk situations (Art 12(2)(a)), facilitating post-market monitoring (Art 12(2)(b)), and monitoring operations (Art 12(2)(c)) is a different technical requirement than just capturing experiment metadata.\n- That said, the \"living record of system evolution\" framing suggests it's meant to be ongoing, not just a one-time documentation exercise.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThis is a well-targeted proposal that addresses a real resource efficiency problem (manual documentation) by leveraging existing infrastructure (version control). It aligns with Recital 71's requirements for technical documentation comprehensibility and up-to-date maintenance. The approach of automating at the point of experimentation rather than 