        "reasoning_content": "The user asks me to screen a single research proposal (P2703) from my vantage point as a Researcher at an R&D institution whose domain of concern is \"the knowledge frontier, intellectual merit of research, and broader scientific impact.\"\n\nLet me analyze this proposal carefully:\n\n**Proposal P2703: Selective Event Sampling for High-Fidelity Audit Trails**\n\nThe core idea: Treat logging as an optimization problem (signal vs. noise), use information theory and anomaly detection to determine which system states are most predictive of risk/failure, then develop adaptive logging triggers.\n\nLet me evaluate this from my professional vantage point (knowledge frontier, intellectual merit, broader scientific impact):\n\n**Strengths:**\n- The framing of logging as an optimization problem is genuinely interesting from a research perspective. Most approaches to compliance logging are brute-force: log everything. Treating it as a signal-detection/information-theoretic problem is a legitimate and non-trivial research question.\n- Information theory applied to system observability has real intellectual depth — you're essentially asking \"what is the minimum information content needed to reconstruct a causal narrative of system behavior?\" This connects to active learning, optimal experimental design, and sensor placement literature.\n- Anomaly detection for adaptive triggers is a well-studied area but applying it specifically to regulatory compliance logging (where the \"anomaly\" definition is tied to legal risk categories in Articles 79(1), 72, 26(5)) gives it a novel constraint structure.\n- The broader scientific impact is reasonable: if you can develop principled methods for adaptive observability in AI systems, this has applications beyond EU AI Act compliance — it could inform how any complex system (autonomous vehicles, medical devices) should be monitored.\n\n**Weaknesses/Concerns:**\n- There's a tension between the research framing and