rch project.\"\n- The scope is relatively narrow and well-understood in general systems engineering. Logging overhead (disk I/O, CPU for serialization, memory buffering) is not novel territory. There are established patterns (async logging, sampling, tiered storage). Is this really a \"research\" problem or an engineering tuning problem?\n- However, in the specific context of AI/ML systems — where inference pipelines have their own latency constraints and where the \"events\" to log might include model inputs/outputs that could be large tensors — there IS genuine uncertainty about what the overhead looks like at scale. This is more novel than just \"how much does a log statement cost.\"\n- The proposal says \"find the optimal balance between regulatory requirements and runtime efficiency.\" This is useful but also somewhat vague as an outcome. What would success look like? A set of benchmarks? Design guidelines? An architecture recommendation?\n\n**From speed/quality of prototyping perspective:**\n- If we're building prototypes quickly, having a separate research project that takes weeks/months before we get answers could slow us down. We'd rather integrate lightweight benchmarking into our prototype iterations.\n- BUT: if we don't understand the overhead profile early, our first prototypes might be architecturally wrong (e.g., synchronous logging in the inference path vs. async), and we'd waste prototyping cycles reworking them.\n- So there's a timing question. A quick, focused study (say 2-4 weeks) before major architecture decisions would save time. An open-ended \"research project\" that drags on would not.\n\n**Technical viability:**\n- The research is technically sound and feasible. Benchmarking logging overhead is straightforward engineering work. No fundamental technical barriers.\n- The question of what constitutes \"optimal balance\" involves some judgment calls about acceptable latency degradation for different use cases, which is a design decision more t