tructure that enables future research? Possibly in a very indirect way — if everyone logs consistently, meta-analysis becomes possible. But the proposal as stated doesn't aim at any scientific output; it aims at compliance (\"seamless compliance without adding manual administrative burdens\").\n- It doesn't generate new knowledge, new methods, new understanding of how AI systems work, or new capabilities.\n\n**However**, let me steelman: \n- Provenance tracking is actually a growing research area (reproducibility crisis in ML, model cards, etc.). But this proposal frames it purely as compliance infrastructure, not as a research contribution to reproducibility science.\n- An immutable database with synchronized timestamps for every execution could theoretically create a corpus useful for studying system behavior over time. But again, that's not the stated aim or design intent.\n\n**My assessment:** From my specific vantage point (knowledge frontier, intellectual merit, broader scientific impact), this proposal is essentially an engineering compliance task dressed up as a \"framework.\" It has minimal to no research content. It doesn't advance AI science, it doesn't generate new knowledge about how these systems work, and its primary stated purpose is regulatory compliance with reduced administrative burden. While I understand why the lab needs to do this (it's legally required), my job here is to screen from MY domain of concern. This proposal does not clear that bar in terms of intellectual merit or contribution to the knowledge frontier.\n\nThat said — is it so far below the bar that it gets a 0? No. There's a small sliver: if done well, a provenance infrastructure could become a platform for studying system behavior (drift detection, failure mode taxonomy). But as written, it's compliance plumbing. I'd give it maybe 15-20 from my vantage point. It's not something I would \"back\" as a researcher because it doesn't advance knowledge, but it's not absurd either — it