oidance rather than regulatory accuracy. However, efficient allocation doesn't conflict with compliance if the classification is done correctly.\n- The proposal says \"an internal auditing system to determine which specific projects actually fall under Article 12 requirements.\" This is essentially a governance/classification function. It's not itself an implementation of logging — it's a meta-process that determines WHERE logging must be implemented.\n- Risk: If the classification protocol is too aggressive in flagging things as \"not high-risk\" to save resources, we could miss systems that require Article 12 compliance. The rubric needs to be conservative/err on the side of classifying as high-risk when uncertain.\n- Another risk: Classification isn't static. A system's classification can change over time (e.g., if its intended purpose changes, or it's integrated into a larger system that makes it high-risk). The proposal mentions \"project proposal stage\" but doesn't explicitly address re-classification triggers during the system's lifecycle.\n- The proposal is essentially a governance process rather than a technical implementation. From my compliance perspective, this is necessary infrastructure — you can't comply with Article 12 if you don't know which systems are in scope.\n\n**Assessment from compliance vantage point:**\nThis is a foundational prerequisite for any Article 12 compliance program. Without knowing which systems are high-risk, we cannot determine where to implement logging capabilities, what level of traceability is needed (Article 12(2)), or whether the minimum logging requirements of Article 12(3) apply. It's not optional — it's a prerequisite for all downstream compliance activities.\n\nHowever, I have some reservations:\n- The proposal as stated focuses on \"preventing waste\" rather than ensuring no high-risk system slips through. A compliance officer would want the rubric to be explicitly conservative (when in doubt, classify as high-risk 