        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2754\",\n      \"rationale\": \"From a prototyping standpoint, this proposal misidentifies the core engineering deliverable: Article 12(1)-(3) requires the high-risk system itself to automatically record specific event fields (timestamps, reference database, matched inputs, verifier IDs), not an external AI model that later analyzes those logs. Building a 'separate supervisory model trained specifically' on anomaly patterns in our own deployment logs introduces a heavy ML pipeline—labeled training data for rare risk/substantial-modification events, streaming inference infrastructure, and validation against ground truth we likely don't yet possess—making it far slower to prototype than the comparatively straightforward logging-architecture work the regulation actually mandates. The concept of log-based monitoring has merit as a complementary Article 72/79 tool, but as framed here it commits significant engineering resources to an ML system while leaving the actual Article 12 technical requirements (automatic event recording with the specified fields) unaddressed.\",\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"promise\": 35\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "The user wants me to evaluate proposal P2754 from the vantage point of a Development Engineer at an R&D institution that develops AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain of concern is: technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nLet me analyze this proposal through my lens as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability and prototyping speed/quality.\n\nThe proposal: \"Post-Market Risk Detection Engine\" - An AI-driven monitoring tool that scans system logs in real-time to identify patterns indicating substantial modifications or emerging operational risks. It's a separate supervisory model trained specifically to detect anomalies and risk indicators within event logs of deployed high-risk systems.\n\nLet me think about this from my vantage point:\n\n**Technical Viability:**\n- The core idea is: build an AI model that monitors logs for anomalies/risk patterns. This is technically feasible - anomaly detection on structured/semi-structured log data is a well-trodden engineering problem (log parsing, sequence modeling, statistical baselines, etc.).\n- However, the proposal says \"trained specifically to detect anomalies and risk indicators.\" The critical question is: what training data do we have for \"substantial modifications\" or \"emerging operational risks\"? These are relatively rare events. We'd need labeled examples of when a system was in a risky state vs. normal operation. This is a classic cold-start / scarce-label problem for the specific use case.\n- \"Real-time scanning\" adds engineering complexity - streaming log processing, low-latency inference, alerting pipelines. Feasible but non-trivial infrastructure work.\n- The proposal frames this as satisfying Article 12(2), which requires logging capabilities to enable recording of events relevant for identifying risk situations and facilitating post-market monitoring. But wait - Article 12 is about the SYSTEM's ability to LOG events, not about building a separate AI that analyzes those logs. The regulation says high-risk systems shall \"technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs).\" It doesn't mandate an AI-based log analyzer. So there's a scope mismatch here - this proposal goes well beyond what Article 12 requires.\n- Actually, re-reading: Article 12(2) says logging capabilities \"shall enable the recording of events relevant for\" identifying risks, facilitating post-market monitoring, and monitoring operations. The proposal is about an AI that analyzes logs AFTER they're recorded. This is more aligned with Article 72 (post-market monitoring) or even Article 79 (risk management) rather than strictly Article 12's record-keeping requirement.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- A prototype for anomaly detection on log streams could be built relatively quickly if we already have logging infrastructure in place (basic statistical methods, autoencoders, etc.).\n- However, a \"separate supervisory model trained specifically\" implies a significant ML pipeline: data collection from deployed systems, labeling or semi-supervised approaches, training, validation, deployment. This is not a quick prototype - it's a multi-month project at minimum.\n- The quality of the prototype depends heavily on whether we have access to real deployment logs and examples of problematic states. If our high-risk systems are already in production, this is more feasible. If they're still in development, we lack the operational data.\n- \"Real-time\" adds complexity to prototyping - you need streaming infrastructure, which is harder to prototype than batch processing.\n\n**Key concerns from my vantage point:**\n1. Scope creep relative to Article 12: This isn't really a record-keeping solution. It's an analytics/monitoring solution layered ON TOP of logging. The actual Article 12 requirement (automatic recording of events) is a simpler engineering task - implement proper logging infrastructure with the right fields.\n2. Training data problem: What are we training the supervisory model on? We need labeled examples of \"substantial modifications\" and \"emerging risks\" in our specific systems. This may not exist yet, especially if we're early in deployment.\n3. Real-time requirement makes prototyping harder - streaming ML pipelines are more complex to prototype than batch ones.\n4. The proposal conflates compliance (Article 12) with risk management (Articles 79, 72). As a development engineer, I need to know: what's the actual technical deliverable? Is it just \"make sure logs capture the right fields\" (simpler) or is it \"build an AI anomaly detection system\" (much more complex)?\n5. However, if we're building high-risk systems anyway and will have logging infrastructure, adding a monitoring/analytics layer is technically reasonable and could be prototyped incrementally.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThe idea has technical merit - log-based anomaly detection is feasible engineering. But from my vantage point:\n- It overreaches what Article 12 actually requires (it's more of an Article 72/79 solution)\n- The \"separate supervisory model trained specifically\" framing suggests a heavy ML project where simpler approaches might suffice initially\n- Real-time streaming adds prototyping complexity\n- Training data availability is uncertain and could block the whole thing\n- It's not impossible, but it's a significant engineering commitment for what may be overkill relative to the specific Article 12 requirement\n\nI think this is a borderline case. The technical approach is sound in principle (anomaly detection on logs), but the framing as an \"AI-driven\" solution with a \"separate supervisory model\" makes it heavier than necessary for a prototype. A simpler rule-based or statistical monitoring layer could be prototyped faster and would still address the spirit of the requirement. The proposal as stated commits to building an ML system, which is a bigger engineering commitment.\n\nI'll lean toward reject with a moderate promise score - maybe 40-50. The underlying idea has merit but the specific framing (separate AI model, real-time, \"trained specifically\") makes it harder to prototype quickly and raises training data concerns that could block progress. It also somewhat mischaracterizes what Article 12 requires versus what Articles 72/79 require.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The proposal says \"This proactively satisfies Article 12(2).\" But Article 12(2) is about the LOGGING CAPABILITIES of the system enabling recording of certain events. It's not about an external AI analyzing those logs. So there's a fundamental misunderstanding of what needs to be built. The actual technical requirement for Article 12 is:\n- Implement automatic logging in the high-risk systems themselves\n- Ensure logs capture: start/end times, reference database, input data matched, identification of natural persons verifying results\n\nThe proposed solution doesn't address any of these specific technical requirements. It builds something adjacent (log analysis) without addressing the core requirement (the system's ability to record events automatically). This is a significant technical gap in my view.\n\nI'll go with reject, promise around 35-40. The underlying monitoring concept has value but as framed it doesn't actually solve the Article 12 technical problem, and the \"separate AI model\" approach makes prototyping slower than necessary for what's needed.\n",
