g a new system. If the lab already has authentication infrastructure (which most R&D institutions do), this could be redundant or at least should integrate with existing systems rather than creating a parallel portal\n- The scope is narrow and specific to Annex III 1(a) systems. Is that a large portion of what we're developing? If not, the ROI on building a dedicated system might be low\n- It's essentially an IT/security project dressed up as R&D - it doesn't advance scientific ambition at all\n- However, from a resource allocation perspective: this is a bounded, well-defined task with clear requirements. It's not a research bet. It's compliance engineering. The question is whether the lab should be spending its resources on this vs. other things.\n- Actually, as Lab Director concerned with \"effective use of existing R&D infrastructure,\" I should ask: do we already have audit trails and authentication in place? Most serious labs do. The question is whether a NEW portal is needed or whether existing systems can be configured to meet this requirement.\n\nThe key tension for me: This is a compliance necessity (for Annex III 1(a) systems), but the proposal frames it as \"building\" something new rather than potentially configuring/adapting existing infrastructure. From my resource-efficiency standpoint, I'd want to see whether this can be achieved by extending existing authentication and logging systems before committing resources to building a dedicated portal.\n\nHowever, the regulatory requirement is clear and specific. If we have Annex III 1(a) systems in our portfolio (or are developing them), we MUST have this capability. The question is the implementation path.\n\nLet me weigh:\n- Is it necessary? For Annex III 1(a) high-risk systems, yes - Article 12(3)(d) explicitly requires \"identification of the natural persons involved in the verification of the results.\" A simple log entry isn't sufficient; you need authenticated identity.\n- Is it efficient? If we build a new portal from scratch, probably not. If we extend existing auth infrastructure with a sign-off module and structured logging, that's much more efficient.\n- Does it use existing R&D infrastructure well? The proposal doesn't specify integration with existing systems - it says \"build a digital sign-off interface\" which sounds like a standalone system.\n\nMy assessment: The underlying need is legitimate and non-negotiable for compliance. However, the framing as building a new dedicated portal rather than extending existing authentication/logging infrastructure is concerning from an efficiency standpoint. That said, even if the implementation approach needs refinement, the core capability (authenticated human verification logging) is essential.\n\nI think this clears my bar because:\n1. It addresses a hard regulatory requirement (not optional, not speculative)\n2. The scope is bounded and well-defined\n3. The resource commitment, while real, is proportionate to the compliance obligation\n4. Even if implemented as an extension of existing infrastructure rather than a new portal, the functional outcome is the same\n\nBut my promise score should reflect that I'd want this integrated with existing systems rather than built as a standalone \"portal.\" It's necessary but the implementation approach needs cost-consciousness. I'll approve it because the compliance need is real and specific, but give it a moderate-to-good promise score reflecting that it's a compliance necessity (not a scientific ambition) and the build-vs-reuse question matters for resource efficiency.\n\nI'll say approve with a promise around 62-70. It's clearly needed if we have Annex III 1(a) systems, it's bounded in scope, but it's pure compliance overhead that doesn't advance science, and I'd want to ensure it leverages existing auth infrastructure rather than building parallel systems.\n\nLet me settle on 65.\n",
