ts to individual projects. That's a reasonable division of labor.\n\nI think this clears my bar. The resource leverage is high, it uses existing infrastructure (CI/CD), it protects researcher time, and the scope is bounded enough that we're not committing to an open-ended platform build. The core engineering team investment is front-loaded but amortizes across all projects.\n\nOne reservation: the proposal doesn't fully address Article 12(2) logging requirements (risk events, post-market monitoring events). But as a \"framework\" and \"modular software library,\" it could be extended. And my role isn't to scope the perfect solution — it's to judge whether committing resources to this direction is efficient. I think it is, with the understanding that the framework will need iterative refinement.\n\nPromise score: This is genuinely good from a resource-efficiency standpoint. It's not a 100 because of the gaps in addressing all Article 12(2) requirements and the risk of over-engineering before full requirements clarity, but it's clearly high-value. I'd say around 78-82. Let me settle on 80 — strong efficiency play with some scope gaps that will need iteration.\n\nDecision: approve.\n",
