n to non-engineering staff.\n  - For prototyping speed: adding a documentation-generation step to every major commit could slow down iteration cycles if not well-isolated. However, if it's async/non-blocking in CI, it shouldn't impede prototyping significantly.\n\n**Speed/quality of prototyping perspective:**\n- The automated generation part is a net positive for prototyping speed — engineers don't have to manually write/update docs after each experiment.\n- The \"monthly audit\" requirement adds a compliance overhead that doesn't directly affect engineering work but creates a dependency: if the auto-generated docs are incomplete, engineers might need to backfill information, slowing things down.\n- The proposal is reasonable in scope — it's not trying to build an entirely new system, just integrate existing VCS with doc generation. This is technically viable within a typical R&D lab.\n\n**Concerns from my vantage:**\n- \"Every major commit\" — what counts as major? If this triggers full documentation regeneration including data lineage re-computation, it could be expensive computationally and slow down CI pipelines.\n- The proposal doesn't address how to handle the gap between automated capture (model architecture params, training hyperparameters) and the qualitative assessments required by Recital 71 (\"general characteristics, capabilities and limitations\"). These latter items are not easily auto-generated from a commit.\n- Data lineage: this is actually quite hard to do fully automatically. Tools like MLflow, DVC help, but full lineage across preprocessing, augmentation, train/test splits, etc., requires deliberate instrumentation in the codebase.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThe proposal is technically sound in its core mechanism (VCS + auto-doc generation) and addresses a real requirement from Recital 71. It's feasible to prototype within reasonable timeframes. The main risks are: (1) scope creep on \"comprehensiveness\" vs. what can actually be automated, (2)