d what goes — a recurring cost that eats into R&D budgets. Automating it once means minimal marginal cost thereafter.\n\n4. **Addresses Article 12 directly**: The EU AI Act requires logs over the \"lifetime of the system\" (Art. 12(1)) and technical documentation kept up to date throughout the lifetime (Recital 71). This is not a one-time snapshot — it's a perpetual obligation. A lifecycle management approach is the natural engineering response to a perpetual data retention obligation.\n\n5. **Prevents crowding out**: The explicit framing that compliance data shouldn't consume high-performance R&D storage is exactly the kind of resource-allocation thinking I'm responsible for. Without this separation, compliance logs (which grow unboundedly over system lifetime) would gradually eat into GPU clusters, NVMe arrays, etc., that researchers actually need.\n\nPotential concerns:\n- Is this a new build or can it be implemented on existing infrastructure? The proposal says \"dedicated\" architecture, which might imply additional cost. However, tiered storage using commodity object storage is relatively inexpensive and well-understood. It doesn't require exotic hardware.\n- \"Cost-efficient\" is stated but not quantified. Still, the architectural pattern (hot → warm → cold) is standard and well-costed in cloud environments.\n- The proposal is scoped appropriately — it's a data management concern, not a research question. It has clear deliverables and bounded scope.\n\nFrom my vantage point, this is squarely within my domain. It's about efficient resource allocation: using the right (cheap) storage for compliance obligations so that expensive resources remain available for scientific ambition. The tiered approach is cost-effective, the automation reduces ongoing operational cost, and the separation protects R&D infrastructure.\n\nIs there anything I'd push back on? Maybe the \"dedicated\" language — one might argue you don't need a fully separate system; you could use tagging