        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2927\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The strategic core—front-loading Annex III classification at prototype stage to avoid expensive retroactive instrumentation (especially the granular Article 12(3) logging for biometric-matching systems)—is sound resource-efficiency logic that directly protects our engineering hours from rework. However, a standing quarterly governance board is heavier than the problem likely requires in an R&D setting where intended purpose rarely shifts mid-project; a one-time classification gate at project kickoff plus a scope-change trigger would capture most of the benefit with far less recurring overhead, and the proposal does not specify whether it layers onto our existing milestone-review infrastructure or spawns a parallel structure.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 74\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze proposal P2927 from my vantage point as Lab Director concerned with resource-allocation efficiency, balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints, and effective use of existing R&D infrastructure.\n\nThe proposal is: A formal governance protocol for early-stage classification of research projects as high-risk according to Annex III, with a quarterly review board where researchers map project goals against EU AI Act categories to determine necessary instrumentation, including updating technical documentation throughout the lifecycle. The stated benefit is preventing costly retroactive modifications by aligning development with regulatory requirements from the first prototype.\n\nLet me assess this from my perspective:\n\n**Resource-allocation efficiency:**\n- A quarterly review board adds a recurring governance overhead. Researchers must spend time mapping projects against Annex III categories each quarter. This is a fixed cost regardless of how many projects actually end up being high-risk.\n- However, the proposal's core argument is that catching misclassification early prevents \"costly retroactive modifications.\" If a team builds a system for 12 months without realizing it falls under Annex III and then has to retrofit logging capabilities (Article 12(3) specifically - start/end times, reference database tracking, input data matching records, natural person identification), that is genuinely expensive in engineering time.\n- The question is: how many projects in this R&D lab are actually likely to be high-risk under Annex III? If most projects are research prototypes that never deploy as high-risk systems, the quarterly board is overhead with low hit rate. But if a meaningful fraction of our work does feed into Annex III applications (employment, credit scoring, biometrics, etc.), early classification saves significant rework cost.\n\n**Balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints:**\n- The proposal frames regulatory compliance as enabling rather than constraining - \"aligning development with regulatory requirements from the very first prototype\" suggests that if you know your logging needs upfront, you can design systems more efficiently rather than bolting on compliance later.\n- However, a quarterly review board could create friction in an R&D environment where projects pivot rapidly. If a project's scope shifts between quarters (which is common in research), the classification might need to be revisited mid-cycle, reducing the effectiveness of the quarterly cadence.\n- The \"formal governance protocol\" language suggests bureaucratic structure that could slow down agile research workflows.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- A review board requires staffing - who chairs it? Who has the regulatory expertise? This may require hiring a compliance officer or diverting an existing person's time.\n- The \"mapping project goals against EU AI Act categories\" step requires researchers to understand Annex III, which is non-trivial. Training costs are implied but not stated.\n- On the other hand, if we already have project review cycles (which most R&D labs do for funding milestones or safety reviews), this could be layered onto existing infrastructure rather than creating a new parallel structure. The proposal doesn't explicitly say it leverages existing review mechanisms, which is a gap.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThe core idea - early classification to avoid costly retrofits - is sound and aligns with efficient resource use. Retrofitting Article 12(3) logging (especially the specific biometric matching data requirements in point 3(a)-(d)) into an already-deployed system is genuinely expensive and disruptive. Catching this at prototype stage is smart engineering practice, not just compliance theater.\n\nHowever, the \"quarterly review board\" framing is somewhat heavy. A lighter mechanism - perhaps a one-time classification gate at project kickoff plus a trigger-based re-assessment if scope changes materially - might achieve the same goal with less recurring overhead. The quarterly cadence implies that projects' risk classifications are expected to change regularly, which may not be accurate for most R&D work where the intended purpose is set early.\n\nThe proposal also conflates two things: (1) classification (which Annex III category applies) and (2) ongoing technical documentation updates throughout lifecycle. The second is a continuous obligation regardless of when you classify - it doesn't really benefit from quarterly review; it's an operational discipline that needs to be baked into project management processes, not gated by a board meeting.\n\nOverall: The strategic insight is correct and valuable for resource efficiency (avoiding rework). But the specific mechanism proposed (quarterly formal governance board) may be heavier than necessary and could add process overhead that doesn't scale well with R&D agility. I lean toward approving because the core value proposition - front-loading regulatory awareness to prevent expensive retrofits - is exactly the kind of resource-efficiency thinking my function should champion. The implementation details can be refined. But I'm not at 100 because the quarterly board structure feels like it could become a bureaucratic bottleneck, and the proposal doesn't address how it integrates with existing project review infrastructure (which would reduce marginal cost significantly).\n\nI'll approve but with a moderate-high promise score reflecting that the strategic direction is right but the specific mechanism needs refinement to be truly resource-efficient. Maybe 72-78 range. I'll go with 74.\n",
