op: human corrections feed back into training/validation datasets. This could reduce the need for separate annotation pipelines later. However, this secondary benefit is somewhat speculative at the ideation stage — whether the data actually proves useful depends on the nature of the tasks and volume.\n\n4. **Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure**: If we already have some form of review workflow (which many labs do for quality control), extending it to capture identity + sign-off + link to log entries is incremental. If we don't, building from scratch is more costly but still within normal software engineering scope rather than requiring new lab equipment or novel research.\n\n5. **Is this really a \"proposal\" that commits scarce implementation resources?** It's relatively narrow in scope — one dashboard with specific fields. Compared to, say, building the full automatic logging infrastructure required by Article 12(1), this is a smaller slice. But it does commit engineers for a period of time to design, build, test, and maintain this interface.\n\n6. **Risk of misalignment**: The proposal frames itself as \"fulfilling Article 12(3)(d).\" If the lab's high-risk systems don't fall under Annex III 1(a) (biometrics), then this specific clause doesn't apply to them at all, and the investment is misdirected. However, the general concept of a review/sign-off interface has value beyond that one clause — it supports Article 14(5) human oversight more broadly, and creates traceability records.\n\n7. **Independence from other proposals**: I'm judging this on its own merits. Is a lightweight sign-off dashboard worth building? From my resource-efficiency perspective:\n   - It's a bounded, well-defined software project (not open-ended research).\n   - It leverages existing infrastructure (web servers, databases, auth systems).\n   - The compliance value is real but narrow (specifically Annex III 1(a) systems).\n   - The secondary R&D benefit (ground-truth data) is plausible but uncertain.\n   - Maintenance burden: once built, it needs to be kept up-to-date as system logs evolve.\n\nMy assessment: This is a reasonable, bounded engineering project with clear compliance utility for the specific biometric use case and a plausible secondary research benefit. It's not scientifically ambitious in the sense of pushing R&D frontiers, but from my vantage point of resource efficiency and infrastructure utilization, it's a low-risk, moderate-return investment that fits within normal software development capacity without requiring exotic new capabilities. The \"lightweight\" framing is doing some work — I'd want to see scope discipline to keep it truly lightweight rather than scope-creeping into a full case-management system.\n\nI'll approve it with a moderate-to-good promise score. It's not transformative but it's efficient, uses existing infrastructure well, and has dual purpose (compliance + data capture). Maybe 62-70 range. I'll go with 65 — it's solidly useful but the secondary R&D benefit is speculative enough that I wouldn't be \"backing it without hesitation,\" and the compliance value is narrow to one Annex III category.\n",
