eems narrowly scoped to 12(3) minimums only.\n\n**Compliance Officer assessment:**\n\nStrengths:\n- Directly targets a specific regulatory obligation with clear mandatory language (\"shall provide, at a minimum\")\n- Addresses the biometric-specific requirements which are the most prescriptive part of Article 12\n- Security (encrypted database) is appropriate for sensitive biometric data\n- Integration with identity management addresses the human verification tracking requirement\n- Scoped and implementable - it's a focused system\n\nConcerns:\n- It only addresses Article 12(3) minimums, not the broader obligations in Articles 12(1) and 12(2). However, this is a scoped proposal for one specific piece.\n- The lifetime aspect (over the \"lifetime of the system\") isn't explicitly addressed in retention policies.\n- It's narrowly focused on Annex III point 1(a) systems only - but that's appropriate scoping if the lab develops biometric ID systems.\n- Doesn't mention the technical documentation requirements from Recital 71, but those might be handled elsewhere.\n\nFrom my compliance vantage point: This is a well-scoped proposal that directly addresses a specific, mandatory regulatory requirement. If the lab develops any Annex III(1)(a) biometric identification systems, this is non-negotiable - failure to implement would mean direct non-compliance with a \"shall\" provision. The risk of not having this is regulatory sanction, market exclusion, and reputational damage. The proposal correctly identifies the specific minimums and proposes a concrete implementation path.\n\nHowever, I note it's somewhat incomplete as stated (lifetime retention, full 12(3)(c) explicitness), but these are implementation details that can be addressed during development. The core architecture is sound and directly responsive to regulatory text.\n\nMy decision: This clears my bar. It addresses a mandatory \"shall\" requirement for biometric systems. If the lab has any such systems in its portfoli