ublic format. My job is to ensure our systems meet those functional requirements within deadlines and budget. Spending scarce implementation resources on building an external open standard, when I don't yet know if it will align with final CEN/CENELEC harmonized standards, is a significant resource risk to institutional survival.\n\nHowever, there IS a compliance value: having a well-thought-out data schema internally (even if not publicly released) would help us implement Article 12 correctly. And engaging with other institutions could provide useful input on what regulators expect. The privacy/transparency balance is genuinely important for compliance.\n\nBut the proposal as framed — \"open-source,\" \"global scientific community,\" \"reproducibility,\" \"positions our lab as a leader\" — reads more like a thought leadership / academic positioning project than a compliance implementation project. It's not directly ensuring WE are compliant; it's trying to shape an external standard.\n\nMy decision: This is borderline. There's some tangential value for compliance (thinking through data formats, privacy balance), but the core framing and resource commitment are misaligned with my function's priorities. The actual Article 12 implementation in our own systems could be a small component within this larger project, or it could get buried under standardization governance overhead. I'm inclined to reject because:\n1. It doesn't directly implement Article 12 for OUR systems\n2. It commits scarce resources to an external-facing standardization effort with uncertain regulatory alignment\n3. The primary stated benefits (reproducibility, global positioning) are outside my domain of concern\n4. There's a risk it distracts from the actual compliance implementation timeline\n\nBut I'll give it a moderate promise score because there IS some value in thinking through data formats and privacy balance that could inform our internal implementation. Maybe 30-35? It's not zero — if done