sting R&D infrastructure:**\n- Integrating into \"the base image of all R&D environments\" is smart — it leverages existing containerized/cloud infrastructure rather than building new systems.\n- It uses the natural deployment pipeline (base images) as the distribution mechanism, which is how things already work in most modern ML/AI labs.\n- The secure database schemas could potentially leverage existing data infrastructure rather than requiring entirely new storage.\n\n**Potential concerns from my vantage:**\n- Scope creep risk: \"all institutional high-risk AI projects\" — if this library becomes a single point of failure or requires frequent updates, maintenance burden could be significant. But this is manageable with dedicated engineering ownership.\n- The proposal says \"mandatory API hooks\" — this could impose friction on research workflows if not designed well. But from an efficiency standpoint, standardization reduces long-term costs versus bespoke solutions.\n- One risk: building a generic library that needs to handle all possible high-risk system types (Annex III point 1(a) biometric systems have very specific logging requirements per Article 12(3)) might be complex. But the proposal seems focused on the general case (Article 12(1)-(2)), which is the core requirement for most projects.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a textbook example of efficient resource allocation in an R&D lab. Instead of N project teams each spending engineering time on logging compliance, one team builds it once and everyone inherits it. It leverages existing infrastructure (base images, deployment pipelines). It reduces the risk of non-compliance that could lead to regulatory penalties or forced rework. The cost is bounded and predictable. From a resource-allocation efficiency standpoint, this is exactly the kind of \"shared infrastructure\" investment that scales well.\n\nI would approve this with high confidence. It's one of the most efficient ways to address Article 12 comp