ing development or producing reports mid-sprint.\n- The proposal is somewhat vague on the technical implementation. It describes a \"gating process\" and \"checklists\" but doesn't specify what the actual logging architecture would look like, how it integrates with our existing codebase/infrastructure, or what tools we'd use. From a prototyping speed perspective, I need concrete technical direction, not just a process framework.\n- The proposal focuses on identification/classification rather than the actual engineering of the logging solution. It's more of a project management/process proposal than a technical one. It tells me WHEN to start thinking about compliance but not HOW to build the logging system in a way that doesn't compromise prototyping velocity.\n- However, the core insight — early classification prevents retrofit costs — is genuinely important for engineering. If I'm building a prototype and at week 8 of a 10-week sprint someone says \"oh, this needs full Article 12 compliance with automatic event recording over system lifetime,\" that's a nightmare. Designing for observability from day one is standard good engineering practice anyway (logging, metrics, tracing).\n\n**Net assessment:**\nThe proposal has a valid core insight relevant to my work (early classification helps architecture), but it's framed as a process/administrative solution rather than a technical one. The quarterly audit component could add overhead to prototyping cycles. It doesn't provide concrete technical guidance for HOW to implement logging in a way that balances compliance with development velocity. It's more of a PM/governance proposal. From my narrow vantage (technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping), it's partially relevant but not directly actionable as an engineering solution. The \"checklist\" approach is somewhat antithetical to the iterative, fast prototyping culture I operate in — though early identification IS valuable.\n\nI'd lean toward a moderate approval