        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2560\",\n      \"rationale\": \"This proposal directly operationalises the Recital 71 obligation that technical documentation 'should be kept up to date, appropriately throughout the lifetime of the AI system,' and its Git-based version-controlled workflow creates an auditable trail supporting Article 12(2) traceability (particularly identifying substantial modifications under 12(2)(a)). The trigger-on-model-update mechanism is well-matched to the most common audit failure I see in practice — deployed model versions whose compliance dossiers still describe a prior iteration — and the 'mandatory review' language, while ideally implemented as a hard release gate rather than merely an alert, provides a defensible accountability loop. Minor residual concern: Recital 71 requires documentation 'in a clear and comprehensive form,' so raw git commits or diff logs will not satisfy a regulator; the protocol must include a formatting/publishing layer, but that is an implementation detail within this proposal's scope.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 84\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "The user asks me to act as a Compliance Officer at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. I need to screen proposal P2560 from my professional vantage point focused on institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nLet me analyze the proposal:\n\n**P2560: \"Living Technical Documentation Protocol\"**\n- A dynamic documentation system that synchronizes technical specifications, capabilities, and risk assessments with the current model version.\n- Git-based documentation workflow where every model update triggers an automated alert for a mandatory compliance dossier review.\n- Purpose: prevent documentation gaps that lead to regulatory failure during official post-market audits.\n\nNow let me assess this from my Compliance Officer perspective:\n\n**Strengths:**\n1. Directly addresses the requirement in Recital 71 that \"The technical documentation should be kept up to date, appropriately throughout the lifetime of the AI system.\" This is a core compliance obligation.\n2. The synchronization of docs with model versions is smart — it ties documentation updates directly to the artifact being regulated, reducing the risk of stale documentation.\n3. Git-based workflow provides version control and audit trail — this supports traceability requirements under Article 12(2)(a) (identifying situations presenting risk or substantial modification).\n4. The automated alert for compliance dossier review on every model update is a practical mechanism to ensure no model ships without updated documentation.\n5. This directly protects against the most common audit failure: documentation that doesn't match what's actually deployed.\n\n**Weaknesses/Concerns:**\n1. \"Automated alert for a mandatory compliance dossier review\" — an alert alone isn't sufficient. The obligation is that documentation IS kept up to date, not that someone gets reminded to check it. This needs to be a gate/blocker, not just an alert. However, the proposal says \"mandatory\" which implies enforcement mechanism.\n2. Git-based workflow for compliance documentation: while version control is good, the EU AI Act requires documentation to be in \"clear and comprehensive form\" (Recital 71). Raw git diffs or commit messages may not meet that standard. But this is an implementation detail — the proposal is about the protocol/workflow, not the format.\n3. The scope mentions \"technical specifications, capabilities, and risk assessments\" — this aligns well with Recital 71's list: \"general characteristics, capabilities and limitations of the system, algorithms, data, training, testing and validation processes used as well as documentation on the relevant risk-management system.\"\n4. It doesn't explicitly address Article 12(1) (automatic logging of events) — but it's not trying to; it's about technical documentation, which is a separate but related obligation under the Act (Article 11/Annex IV). The proposal correctly focuses on its domain.\n\n**From my Compliance Officer vantage point:**\n- Institutional survival: Documentation gaps are one of the top reasons providers fail audits and face enforcement actions. This directly mitigates that risk. HIGH VALUE.\n- Regulatory compliance: Directly addresses the \"kept up to date\" requirement and supports traceability. STRONG ALIGNMENT.\n- Market reputation: Being caught with stale documentation during an audit is a public trust killer. This protects reputation. GOOD.\n\nThe proposal is well-scoped, directly relevant to my domain, and addresses a real, common compliance failure mode. The Git-based approach provides audit trail (who changed what, when), which supports Article 12(2) traceability requirements. The \"mandatory\" review trigger on model updates creates accountability.\n\nOne concern: it says \"automated alert\" rather than a hard gate. But in practice, a mandatory review process triggered by alerts is a reasonable compliance mechanism — you can't fully automate judgment calls about whether documentation accurately reflects new capabilities/risks. And the word \"mandatory\" implies enforcement.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a strong proposal that directly serves my function's core mandate. I would approve it with high confidence. It's not a moonshot; it's operationalizing an existing obligation in a practical way. The promise score should be high — maybe 82-88. It's very aligned but has minor implementation concerns (alert vs. gate, format compliance) that mean it won't be a perfect 95+.\n\nI'll go with approve and a promise of around 85.\n",
