peline). But the proposal specifically says it integrates with existing version control and experiment tracking tools, suggesting minimal disruption.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:** This is a strong point. The proposal explicitly leverages \"version control and experiment tracking tools\" — these are almost certainly already in place at any serious AI lab. We're not building new data collection infrastructure; we're adding an extraction/reporting layer on top of what exists. This is high-leverage: the input data (metadata, hyperparameters, dataset info) is already being generated as a byproduct of research activity. The proposal essentially says \"we're already capturing this data, let's just format it into compliance documents.\" That's efficient use of existing infrastructure.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- Scope creep risk: \"auto-generate reports on datasets and validation processes\" — how complex is the validation process documentation really? If our validation processes are highly bespoke per project, the auto-generation might need significant customization. But even then, it's better than starting from scratch each time.\n- The proposal addresses Recital 71 (technical documentation) but Article 12(1)-(3) also requires automatic logging of events during operation. This pipeline seems focused on development-time documentation rather than operational logging. However, the proposal doesn't claim to solve all of Article 12 — it's specifically about the technical documentation component mentioned in Recital 71. That's a reasonable scope.\n- Maintenance: any automation pipeline needs maintenance as tools change, new models are added, etc. But this is far less resource-intensive than manual documentation.\n\n**Overall assessment:** This is a high-value, low-friction proposal from my vantage point. It leverages existing infrastructure (version control, experiment tracking) to reduce recurring compliance costs. It frees researcher time for actual R