fically address the reference database, specific match data, or verifier IDs as distinct categories. However, it's a general framework that could accommodate these.\n\n**Recital 71:** Emphasizes comprehensible information, traceability, technical documentation kept up to date, automatic recording of events over lifetime.\n- The proposal supports traceability and automated recording. Good alignment.\n\nFrom my Compliance Officer vantage point:\n\n**Strengths:**\n- Directly addresses the core mandate of Article 12(1) — automatic logging over the system's lifetime.\n- A unified, institutional asset means consistent compliance across all projects, reducing the risk that one team implements logging poorly or inconsistently. This is a major compliance advantage — regulators can see uniform practices.\n- \"Plug-and-play\" reduces the chance of human error in implementing logging per-project, which is a significant compliance risk. If every project must build its own logging, some will get it wrong. A shared library enforces consistency.\n- Reduces per-project overhead means more projects can be brought into compliance within resource constraints — this supports institutional survival (we can deploy more systems without proportionally scaling the compliance burden).\n- Market reputation: demonstrating a robust, standardized traceability infrastructure is a strong signal to regulators and market participants that the institution takes compliance seriously.\n\n**Weaknesses/Concerns:**\n- The description is somewhat high-level. \"Timestamps, input hashes, and user IDs\" may not fully cover all Article 12(2) and 12(3) requirements without additional work. But as a foundational architecture, it's the right direction.\n- There's a risk that a single unified API becomes a bottleneck — if it has a vulnerability or needs updating, all projects are affected. But from a compliance standpoint, this is actually an advantage (single point of control).\n- The proposal focuses on \"automatically capture\" which aligns with the \"automatic recording\" requirement in Art 12(1). Good.\n- One concern: \"without requiring custom code for every model\" — some high-risk systems may need very specific logging fields that a generic wrapper can't anticipate. But this is an implementation detail; the architecture should allow for configurable extensions.\n\n**Overall assessment from Compliance perspective:**\nThis is strongly aligned with my function's priorities. It directly implements Article 12(1), supports traceability (Recital 71), creates institutional consistency that reduces compliance risk, and signals regulatory seriousness to external stakeholders. The unified approach means I can audit one system rather than N different implementations. This is exactly the kind of structural compliance investment that protects institutional survival.\n\nI would approve this. It's a foundational compliance infrastructure piece. My promise score should be high — maybe 82-88. It's very well-aligned, but the description is somewhat generic and I'd want to see it explicitly mapped to all sub-requirements of Art 12(2) and (3), particularly for Annex III 1(a) systems. But as a directional investment, it's strongly in my favor.\n\nLet me settle on a score: 85. It's highly promising from my vantage point — directly addresses the regulatory mandate, creates consistency that reduces compliance risk across the institution, and supports market reputation. The slight deduction is for the generality of the description (doesn't explicitly address all Art 12(3) fields) and the need to ensure it can be extended for specific high-risk categories.\n",
