s wrong for compliance purposes and the scope is too exploratory.\n\nLet me settle on:\n- Rationale: The proposal misidentifies the nature of Article 12(3)(d) as a UX/HCI problem when it is fundamentally an engineering requirement for automatic identity capture in logs. A user study does not deliver the compliance artifact (automatic recording capability), and the exploratory framing (\"investigation,\" \"study\") commits resources to exploration rather than implementation.\n- Decision: reject\n- Promise: 30 (there's a kernel of legitimate concern about ensuring verification steps are actually completed, but it doesn't justify this particular investment from my vantage point)\n",
