ucture:**\n- This directly uses MLflow registries and experiment trackers that we presumably already deployed for MLOps purposes. It extends their utility without duplicating functionality.\n- The version-controlled documentation wiki — if we already have one (e.g., Git-based docs, Confluence with versioning), this is a natural integration point. If not, there's a small additional cost.\n- It creates a single source of truth that reduces the gap between what the model actually does and what the documentation says it does — critical for compliance AND for internal scientific integrity.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- \"Custom scripts\" are inherently fragile. Without proper engineering investment, they break, create technical debt, and eventually consume MORE time than manual documentation would have.\n- The scope is somewhat broad: \"algorithms, data versions, and validation metrics\" — getting all of this to flow automatically from MLflow into a wiki is non-trivial. The initial build could take longer than estimated.\n- It addresses Recital 71 (documentation updates) but doesn't directly address Article 12(1)'s automatic logging requirement or the specific logging in Art 12(3). However, as a documentation pipeline, it complements those requirements rather than conflicting with them.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nFrom my resource-allocation vantage point, this is a strong proposal because:\n1. It uses existing infrastructure (MLflow) — no new capital expenditure\n2. It frees researcher time (the scarcest resource) for actual science\n3. The initial investment is primarily engineering effort, not funding\n4. It has clear, bounded scope that delivers regulatory compliance value\n5. It reduces ongoing administrative overhead\n\nThe main risk is the \"custom scripts\" maintenance burden, but even accounting for that, the net efficiency gain from reducing manual documentation work likely outweighs the maintenance cost. The proposal is pragmatic and resource-conscious.\n\nI'd give this