ing a proposal means committing the lab's scarce implementation resources to pursue it.\" This is a pure research project — developing new algorithms, publishing in journals. It doesn't directly implement Article 12 requirements. The logging capability itself (the actual compliance obligation) would need separate engineering work. This proposal commits resources to research that, while potentially interesting, doesn't directly satisfy any regulatory requirement.\n\n3. **Market reputation**: Publishing in peer-reviewed journals could have some reputational benefit — showing the institution is at the forefront of AI safety and compliance innovation. However, it's a stretch. The EU AI Act doesn't reward you for publishing papers about your logging; it requires you to actually implement logging. There's also a risk: if you publish research that identifies \"patterns preceding system instability or bias,\" those publications could be used in litigation or regulatory proceedings against the institution if those patterns are found in your own systems. This is a real reputational and legal risk.\n\n4. **Scope creep / misalignment**: The proposal reframes compliance as \"a regulatory burden\" to be transformed into something else. From my perspective, Article 12 compliance is not a burden to be \"transformed\" — it's a hard requirement with specific technical specifications (especially for Annex III point 1(a) systems). The risk is that leadership sees this as \"addressing\" the logging requirement when in fact the actual engineering work of implementing compliant logging (ensuring start/end times are recorded, reference databases are logged, input data matches are captured, verification personnel are identified) still needs to happen separately.\n\n5. **No direct compliance output**: The proposal doesn't produce a compliant logging system. It produces research papers and algorithms for analyzing logs. The actual Article 12 obligation — that the system \"shall technically al