ta, identification of natural persons involved in verification. The proposal doesn't explicitly address all these specific requirements.\n- However, it says \"modular API\" which suggests extensibility - teams could add project-specific logging fields while the core framework handles the universal requirements.\n- Risk: A single unified library creates a single point of failure for compliance. If the framework has a bug or gap, ALL projects are affected. But this is mitigated by the fact that it's one codebase to audit and fix vs. N separate implementations.\n- The proposal focuses on \"timestamps and input data\" which covers some but not all of Article 12(3) requirements (like reference database identification, verification personnel IDs). But as a foundational framework, this is reasonable - the modular design allows project-specific extensions.\n\n**Compliance Officer assessment:**\nThis is directly on-mission for my function. Article 12 is one of the most operationally demanding articles because it requires technical implementation across every high-risk system. The risk of non-compliance here is high because it's easy for a research team to neglect logging in favor of model development, and the consequences of non-compliant logging are severe (inability to demonstrate traceability during an investigation). A unified framework:\n1. Ensures minimum compliance requirements are met everywhere (institutional survival)\n2. Reduces regulatory risk by enforcing consistent standards (regulatory compliance)\n3. Demonstrates organizational maturity to regulators and clients (market reputation)\n\nThe fact that it's described as a \"modular API\" that \"integrates with existing inference pipelines\" is pragmatic - it doesn't require ripping out existing systems, which makes adoption more likely. The compliance benefit of preventing \"every separate research team from wasting expensive engineering hours building their own bespoke logging solutions\" is real because bespoke