r Annex III point 1(a) systems (start/end times, reference databases, input data matching, identification of verification persons). However, as an institutional protocol framework, it could serve as the architecture into which those specifics are embedded.\n- It focuses heavily on the \"technical documentation\" side (hyperparameters, dataset versions, validation logs) but doesn't explicitly address the event logging / automatic recording requirement of Article 12(1)-(2), particularly the traceability requirements for risk situations and post-market monitoring events. That said, \"validation logs\" could encompass some of this.\n- The proposal frames it as a \"research lifecycle protocol\" — I need to consider whether this adequately covers the full lifetime including deployment and post-market phases. Recital 71 explicitly states documentation should be kept up to date \"throughout the lifetime of the AI system,\" not just during R&D.\n\n**From institutional survival / market reputation perspective:**\n- This is genuinely strong because it embeds compliance into the research workflow rather than treating it as an external audit burden. This is the most sustainable approach for an R&D institution.\n- The \"scientific asset\" reframing protects market reputation — it positions the lab as methodologically rigorous, not just compliance-driven.\n- For institutional survival, having records auto-generated at milestones dramatically reduces the risk of non-compliance that could trigger enforcement actions under the Act (which carry fines up to 7% of global turnover or €35M for prohibited practices, and up to 3.5%/€15M for other violations).\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a well-conceived institutional protocol that directly serves Article 12 requirements while also strengthening the lab's research culture. It addresses the core compliance need (automatic record-keeping tied to system development) in a way that's operationally sustainable for an R&D institution. The m