ective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- The explicit mention of wrapping \"existing telemetry tools\" is good - it's not asking for greenfield development but augmentation of what already exists.\n- A unified SDK reduces fragmentation and redundant implementation across teams.\n- However, the risk is over-engineering: if only 2-3 projects need high-risk compliance, a full SDK might be overkill compared to a simpler shared library or even documented patterns.\n\n**Professional assessment:**\nThis is a well-conceived proposal that directly addresses Article 12's requirements (automatic recording, event logging, metadata). The architectural choice to wrap existing tools rather than replace them shows resource sensitivity. The decoupling of compliance from model logic is strategically important for an R&D lab - it protects research velocity while meeting regulatory obligations.\n\nThe main concern: the scope says \"across all projects\" and \"all high-risk AI systems.\" If the lab has only a handful of such systems, the ROI on building a full SDK (with its maintenance burden, version management, documentation) may not justify itself vs. a lighter-weight shared module or documented conventions. But if there's a pipeline of high-risk systems coming, this amortizes well.\n\nGiven my mandate around resource-allocation efficiency and effective use of existing infrastructure, I lean toward approving this because:\n1. It explicitly builds on existing telemetry (not replacing)\n2. The decoupling protects research velocity (scientific ambition)\n3. A unified approach is more efficient than N separate implementations\n4. It directly maps to Article 12 requirements\n\nMy concern would be if the lab only has 1-2 high-risk systems, in which case a simpler approach might suffice. But the proposal as stated doesn't overreach - it's a \"wrapper\" not a new platform. I'll approve with moderate-high confidence.\n\nPromise score: This is solid infrastructure thinking that aligns w