des system-level events, not just model outputs) as a rich signal for failure prediction is more novel.\n- However, the proposal as stated is quite broad and doesn't specify what makes this research intellectually distinct from existing model monitoring/drift detection literature. It reads more like an engineering application than a research contribution with clear scientific questions.\n- The \"predict high-risk failure modes\" claim is ambitious but underspecified — what are the failure modes? What's the signal-to-noise ratio in these logs?\n\n**Broader Scientific Impact:**\n- If successful, this could create a new subfield at the intersection of regulatory compliance data and AI safety monitoring. The idea that mandatory logging creates a corpus for safety research is genuinely novel as a framing.\n- Could inform not just EU-regulated systems but broader AI safety practices globally.\n- However, the impact is somewhat bounded — it's primarily applicable to high-risk AI systems in regulated contexts.\n\n**Assessment:**\nThis is a reasonable research direction with genuine novelty at the intersection of regulatory compliance and AI safety monitoring. The framing of using mandated logs as both compliance artifacts AND research substrates for predictive safety is intellectually interesting. However, the proposal is somewhat generic in its methodological description (\"advanced anomaly detection models\") and doesn't clearly articulate what specific scientific questions it will address that haven't been addressed by existing drift-detection or model-monitoring literature. It has potential but as stated reads more like a project brief than a research proposal with clear intellectual contributions.\n\nI'd say this is moderately promising from my vantage point — it touches the frontier but in an incremental way, and the intellectual merit is there but not clearly articulated. I'd give it maybe a 55-60 range. It's above reject because the intersection of regulatory log da