c.\n\n3. **Broader scientific impact:** This has essentially zero broader scientific impact. It's an internal governance mechanism. It doesn't publish anything, doesn't open new research directions, doesn't contribute to the understanding of AI systems or their risks in any scholarly sense. At best, it ensures institutional continuity so that research can continue undisturbed by regulatory surprises.\n\n**Counter-arguments (steel-man):**\n- Without this screening, researchers might waste months/years on projects that can't be deployed due to non-compliance, which is a resource misallocation that ultimately harms the lab's capacity for frontier research.\n- The \"multidisciplinary committee\" could theoretically become a forum where legal constraints inform technical design choices, potentially leading to more innovative compliance-by-design approaches. But this is reading a lot into what is essentially described as a sign-off process.\n- It \"secures institutional survival\" — if the lab doesn't exist, no research happens. This is a necessary condition but not a sufficient one for my vantage point.\n\n**My assessment:** From my specific vantage point (knowledge frontier, intellectual merit, broader scientific impact), this proposal is essentially neutral-to-negative. It's a necessary institutional hygiene measure that any competent lab would have regardless of AI Act concerns. But it doesn't advance research, doesn't generate new knowledge, and has no broader scientific impact. It's an administrative process dressed in regulatory language. The \"intellectual merit\" of a sign-off committee reviewing risk categories is minimal.\n\nHowever, I should note: this is clearly a proposal that OTHER functions (legal, operations, compliance) would strongly approve. From MY vantage point, it's not something I'd champion or resource toward. It doesn't clear my bar for \"intellectual merit of research\" or \"knowledge frontier advancement.\" It's a procedural safeguard, not a re