ree\" - this is efficient use of infrastructure investment over time.\n5. The scope seems well-bounded: a middleware API, not a massive re-architecture. It's an integration layer, not a rebuild.\n\n**Arguments for concern/rejection:**\n1. \"Develop a middleware API that intercepts system calls and data inputs\" - this could be resource-intensive to build and maintain, especially if our existing infrastructure uses heterogeneous systems. The cost of building and maintaining this middleware could be significant.\n2. \"Secure, immutable ledger\" - the term \"immutable ledger\" could imply blockchain or similar technology, which might be overkill or introduce complexity/cost that isn't strictly necessary for compliance. However, it could also just mean write-once storage (like WORM drives), which is reasonable.\n3. The proposal says \"embedded in all high-risk project templates\" - this assumes a degree of standardization across projects that may not exist in an R&D lab where experiments are highly varied. Forcing a uniform middleware on diverse experimental setups could be expensive to implement and maintain.\n4. It's described as something we \"will develop\" - so it's net-new development cost. In a resource-constrained environment, is there an off-the-shelf solution or existing infrastructure component that can be adapted rather than building from scratch?\n\n**Net assessment:**\nFrom my vantage point, this proposal is strong because:\n- It addresses a mandatory requirement (not optional)\n- It's a one-time investment that amortizes across all high-risk projects\n- It protects researcher time (our most scarce resource) from compliance overhead\n- The scope seems appropriately bounded (a middleware layer, not a full re-architecture)\n- It improves effective use of R&D infrastructure by making compliance a property of the template rather than a per-project burden\n\nThe main risk is execution cost and whether \"intercepting system calls\" across heterogeneous research 