ve for compliance, immutability can be problematic if it means records cannot be corrected when errors are found. Recital 71 says documentation should be \"kept up to date,\" which implies amendments/corrections need to be possible (with version control), not that they must be truly immutable.\n3. Monthly audits might be insufficient for high-risk systems with rapid deployment cycles, but it's a reasonable starting point.\n4. The proposal is somewhat high-level/operational - it's an internal process change rather than a technical implementation of automatic logging (which Article 12(1) specifically requires: \"shall technically allow for the automatic recording\"). The manual/gate approach addresses governance but doesn't directly address the TECHNICAL requirement of automatic event recording.\n5. However, as a compliance officer, this is about institutional process and accountability - making sure the organization has the governance structure to ensure compliance. It's a necessary (but not sufficient) piece.\n\n**From my vantage point (institutional survival, regulatory compliance, market reputation):**\n- This directly supports institutional survival by creating clear accountability structures and audit trails that regulators expect.\n- It addresses a key risk: documentation being treated as an afterthought rather than integrated into development.\n- Monthly audits create a recurring compliance check-in.\n- However, it doesn't fully address the technical logging requirement (automatic recording) - that's more of an engineering concern. But from my perspective, mandating that validated logs exist before phase progression is exactly what I'd want as a gate.\n- Market reputation: having a formalized, audited compliance process signals to market participants and regulators that the institution takes EU AI Act seriously.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a strong governance proposal that directly supports Article 12 compliance through process. It's not the technical implementation itself but creates the organizational accountability structure needed. As a compliance officer, I would want this in place. The \"immutable\" language is slightly problematic (should be version-controlled rather than truly immutable), but the spirit is correct.\n\nI'd rate this fairly high - it addresses core compliance concerns directly. Maybe 78-82 range. It's not perfect (doesn't address post-market logging specifically, immutability issue) but the core concept of gating R&D progression on validated documentation is exactly right from a compliance perspective.\n\nLet me settle on: approve, promise ~80.\n",
