able margin of safety.\n- \"Immutable ledger\" — this is a strong technical choice but the regulation doesn't specifically mandate immutability; it mandates recording. However, from a compliance defense standpoint, immutability strengthens the institution's position in case of audits or litigation. This is good for institutional survival and reputation.\n- The proposal mentions \"avoid litigation\" which shows awareness of risk but frames it somewhat narrowly. From a compliance officer's view, this is about meeting regulatory requirements, not just avoiding lawsuits (though both matter).\n- It doesn't explicitly address Article 12(3)(b) (reference database) or 3(c) (input data for matches), but those might be handled by other proposals. This one focuses specifically on the human verification aspect.\n\n**My assessment as Compliance Officer:**\nThis is a well-targeted, legally-grounded proposal that addresses a specific and explicit requirement in Article 12(3)(d). The mandatory authentication ensures the logged identity is genuinely verifiable (not just self-declared), which is important for regulatory audits. The immutable ledger provides strong evidentiary protection. Time-stamping covers part of 3(a) as well.\n\nThe scope to Annex III point 1(a) is appropriate and shows understanding that this specific obligation only applies there. The \"secure credentials\" requirement ensures the identification is robust enough to satisfy a regulator's traceability expectations.\n\nFrom institutional survival: if a regulator audits us and finds we cannot identify who approved biometric matches, that's a direct compliance failure with potential fines (up to €35M or 7% of global turnover under Article 99). This proposal directly mitigates that risk.\n\nFrom market reputation: having a robust, auditable human verification trail for our highest-risk systems signals serious regulatory commitment to clients and partners.\n\nThe one concern is whether \"immutable ledger\" might be over-engineering (blockchain, for example) when the regulation just requires recording. But from a compliance defense perspective, I'd rather have immutability than not. It's a reasonable implementation choice that strengthens our position.\n\nI'll approve this with a high promise score. It's directly on-point with an explicit legal requirement, well-scoped, and addresses a critical traceability gap for our highest-risk systems. The main reservation is slight over-engineering (immutability) and the mention of \"avoid litigation\" framing, but these are minor.\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 82-85. It's very strong on compliance grounds, directly maps to a specific legal text requirement, well-scoped. Slight deductions for the \"litigation avoidance\" framing (which is not really our primary concern — regulatory compliance is) and the immutability choice being potentially over-specified. But overall this clears my bar decisively.\n",
