        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal P2266 is about a \"Dynamic Technical Documentation Pipeline\" that auto-generates living technical documentation from code comments and experiment logs (Git repos + MLflow tracks) to satisfy Recital 71's requirement for up-to-date technical documentation.\n\nLet me assess this from my perspective:\n\n**Technical Viability:**\n- Auto-generating \"regulatory-compliant document templates\" from code comments and MLflow tracks is a significant challenge. Code comments are notoriously incomplete, inconsistent, and not written with regulatory compliance in mind. They describe local implementation details, not system-level characteristics, capabilities, limitations, or risk management.\n- MLflow tracks capture experiment parameters, metrics, and artifacts — useful for training/testing/validation process documentation, but they don't capture the \"general characteristics, capabilities and limitations of the system\" or \"documentation on the relevant risk-management system\" that Recital 71 explicitly requires.\n- The idea of linking Git repos to a structured template is technically feasible as a pipeline, but the semantic gap between raw code/experiment metadata and what a regulator expects in technical documentation (clear, comprehensive narrative about system purpose, data provenance, algorithmic choices, risk assessments) is enormous.\n- \"Auto-generates\" is doing a lot of heavy lifting here. Current NLP/code-understanding tools cannot reliably produce the kind of coherent, accurate regulatory narrative needed. You'd still need significant human review and curation.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- The promise to \"eliminate manual reports\" is unrealistic in the near term. Even with a great pipeline, you'd need humans to validate that the auto-generated documentation actually 