lling engineers from other projects?\n- The proposal says it captures \"required timestamps and input data automatically.\" But Article 12(3) for Annex III point 1(a) systems also requires recording the reference database, specific input data matched, and identification of natural persons in verification. Does this middleware handle ALL of that, or just the basic logging? If it only handles part, you still need per-project work.\n- \"Integrated into our core AI development stack\" — assumes there IS a unified core stack. In many R&D labs, researchers use heterogeneous toolchains (PyTorch, JAX, custom frameworks, different infrastructure). A single middleware that hooks into all of these could be complex and fragile.\n- The proposal is somewhat high-level. \"Build a shared library\" is a lot of work glossed over in one sentence. What's the actual engineering scope? How many person-months?\n- From a funding constraint perspective: this is an upfront capital investment with ongoing maintenance costs. But it pays back if there are multiple high-risk projects (which, being subject to the EU AI Act, we presumably have).\n\n**Resource-allocation efficiency assessment:**\nThe core logic is sound — build once, use everywhere. This is a classic platform-engineering play that reduces per-project marginal cost of compliance. For a lab with multiple high-risk AI systems, this is clearly more efficient than ad-hoc per-project logging implementations. The risk is the upfront cost and the assumption of a unified stack.\n\n**Scientific ambition vs funding constraints:**\nThe proposal frames compliance as something that should NOT slow down scientific work — it automates the boilerplate so researchers can focus on science. This aligns well with balancing ambition and constraints. However, if the engineering team is pulled to build this for 6+ months, that's research capacity not spent on discovery.\n\n**Effective use of existing infrastructure:**\nThe proposal explicitly leverages the \"core AI development stack\" rather than building something new alongside it. This is good from an infrastructure-efficiency standpoint. The concern is whether the core stack is truly unified or whether this will require adapting to multiple heterogeneous environments.\n\nOverall: From my vantage point, this is a well-conceived proposal that addresses resource-allocation efficiency directly (build once vs. build N times), leverages existing infrastructure, and protects scientific velocity. The main risk is scope creep in implementation and the assumption of stack uniformity. But the fundamental logic — centralizing compliance logging to reduce per-project engineering burden — is exactly what a Lab Director focused on efficient resource use would want.\n\nI'd rate this fairly highly. It's not a perfect proposal (lacks specificity on scope, timeline, cost), but the strategic direction aligns strongly with my domain concerns. I'll approve it with a high promise score, maybe 78-82. Let me settle: The core idea is strong for efficiency, but there are implementation risks that could blow up the resource commitment. I'll say 80.\n\nDecision: approve\nPromise: 80\n",
