ficer's perspective:**\n\nStrengths:\n1. **Institutional-wide standardization**: This is exactly what a compliance officer wants — consistency across all projects eliminates gaps where one team might forget or inadequately implement logging. This reduces regulatory risk significantly.\n2. **CI/CD integration for mandatory adoption**: This is a strong compliance mechanism. Making it part of the pipeline means no project ships without logging capability. This addresses the \"technically allow\" requirement of Art 12(1) as a hard gate rather than a soft recommendation.\n3. **Automatic recording**: Directly maps to Art 12(1)'s requirement for \"automatic recording of events.\" Removes human error and manual implementation risk.\n4. **Centralized governance**: As compliance officer, having one framework means I can audit one system rather than N different implementations. Reduces compliance verification burden.\n5. **Timestamps and input hashes**: Good foundational data points that support traceability (Recital 71).\n\nConcerns:\n1. **Doesn't explicitly address Art 12(3) specifics** for biometric identification systems (Annex III 1(a)) — the reference database, match data, natural person verification. However, a well-designed unified framework could be extended to include these modules. The proposal is at the architecture level.\n2. **\"Input hashes\" vs \"input data\"**: Art 12(3)(c) requires recording of input data for which a search led to a match — not just hashes. This could be a gap if the implementation only stores hashes. But this might be an implementation detail not captured in the proposal's summary.\n3. **Scope limitation**: The proposal focuses on \"high-risk AI models we develop.\" Art 12 applies to high-risk systems specifically, so scoping is correct. However, it should also cover deployed systems over their lifetime (not just at development time).\n4. **\"Over the lifetime\"**: Art 12(1) says \"over the lifetime of the system.\" The CI/CD integration add