chnical gap exists that requires *new research* versus applying existing engineering solutions. From a prototyping standpoint, I could stand up a logging pipeline with compression and indexing in weeks using existing tools.\n\n4. **Speed/Quality of Prototyping**: A \"research project\" to explore novel techniques is inherently slower than an engineering approach that applies known best practices. If the lab needs to meet regulatory deadlines (the AI Act has staggered application dates), spending time on novel research when off-the-shelf solutions exist would slow down compliance. However, if the data volumes are truly unprecedented (petabyte-scale biometric matching logs), there might be a legitimate technical gap.\n\n5. **Practical concerns**: \n   - \"High-compression\" for log data: Logs tend to be already compressible text. The marginal gain from \"novel\" compression is likely small compared to engineering overhead.\n   - \"Searchable\": Standard database solutions handle this. What's novel?\n   - The framing as a \"scientific opportunity\" and \"publishable results\" suggests the motivation is partly academic rather than purely solving the compliance problem efficiently.\n   - There's no mention of integration with existing system architectures, which from an engineering standpoint is where most of the actual work lies.\n\n6. **What's missing for technical viability**:\n   - No specification of what data volumes we're talking about (when does compression actually matter?)\n   - No latency requirements (real-time vs. batch)\n   - No retention period details (Article 12 says \"lifetime\" but Article 18 specifies 6+ years minimum)\n   - No security/access control considerations for the logs themselves\n   - No mention of how this integrates with the actual AI system's runtime\n\n**Decision reasoning**: From my vantage point, this proposal is too vague and research-oriented to be a viable engineering solution. The problems it identifies (storage cost, searchability) are already solved by mature infrastructure. A \"new research project\" exploring \"novel techniques\" when standard solutions exist would delay prototyping rather than accelerate it. However, the underlying need (efficient logging at scale for compliance) is real and important. The issue is that this proposal doesn't demonstrate a technical gap that requires novel R&D versus engineering application of known solutions.\n\nI'd say this gets a moderate-low promise score. It identifies a real concern but proposes an overly research-heavy approach to what is largely an engineering problem with existing solutions. It's not useless, but it's not the right shape for moving our prototyping forward quickly. Maybe a 30-35? It has some merit in that if we truly are dealing with unprecedented data volumes, compression strategies matter. But as framed, it's too exploratory and doesn't connect to concrete engineering deliverables or timelines.\n\nI'll go with reject at around 28-32. Let me settle on 30.\n",
