entific understanding or producing higher-quality science per se.\n\n   However, I should consider: good traceability IS part of good research practice. Reproducibility, documentation of experimental conditions, versioning of datasets — these are all values in the scientific community. Article 12's requirements (recording start/end times, reference databases, input data, verification by natural persons) overlap with what good scientific practice already demands. So there IS some alignment with research integrity values.\n\n   But the proposal as stated is framed entirely around compliance gates and checklist updates. It doesn't propose any new methodology for logging that would improve our understanding of model behavior, it doesn't suggest innovative approaches to traceability that could inform how we study AI systems, and it doesn't extend into broader scientific questions.\n\n3. **Broader scientific impact:** Would adopting this protocol change what science we can do or contribute to the field? Marginally at best — ensuring our logs are complete means our results are more reproducible, which is a good thing for scientific credibility. But the proposal doesn't aim at broader impact; it aims at regulatory compliance. It's defensive (avoiding \"costly retrospective fixes\") rather than exploratory or contribution-oriented.\n\n**Assessment:** From my vantage point as a Researcher concerned with knowledge frontier, intellectual merit, and broader scientific impact, this proposal is primarily an administrative/compliance mechanism. While I acknowledge that good traceability supports research integrity and reproducibility (which I value), the proposal itself doesn't advance any scientific question, doesn't create new intellectual capacity, doesn't push the knowledge frontier, and doesn't aim at broader scientific contribution. It's a reasonable operational measure, but from MY specific lens — is this something I would champion as advancing our research mission? No, not 