        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2680\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The architectural core—decoupling an append-only, hash-verified audit store from the hot inference path—is technically sound and genuinely speeds prototyping because the compliance layer can be developed and tested independently of the AI model pipeline. However, the proposal stops at the storage abstraction: it does not specify the ingestion mechanism (synchronous write would add latency to every event despite the stated non-regression goal), the event schema required by Art. 12(3) (use-period timestamps, reference-database pointers, input-data captures, verifier IDs), or how 'kept up to date' per Recital 71 is reconciled with a strictly write-once model; and 'tamper-proof' overstates what SHA-chaining alone delivers without external anchoring. These omissions mean the prototype would still require substantial design work before it meets the letter of Article 12, but the pattern itself is buildable quickly with off-the-shelf WORM/append-only tooling.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 64\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "The user wants me to screen a single proposal (P2680) from the perspective of a Development Engineer at an R&D institution that develops AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain of concern is: technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nLet me analyze the proposal:\n\n**Proposal P2680: \"Immutable Regulatory Audit Vault\"**\n- Centralized, tamper-proof storage architecture for high-integrity records and event logs\n- Write-once-read-many database system\n- Stores hashed event logs separately from primary operational application data\n- Ensures traceability required by Article 12\n- Claims it won't compromise latency or performance of active production environments\n\nNow let me assess this from my vantage point: **technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping**.\n\n**Technical Viability Assessment:**\n- A write-once-read-many (WORM) database is a well-established pattern. Technologies exist (e.g., append-only databases, content-addressable storage, hash-chained logs). This is technically viable.\n- Separating audit logs from primary operational data is a sound architectural decision — it decouples the compliance logging path from the hot-path of inference/serving. This is good engineering practice.\n- Hashing event logs provides tamper-evidence but not tamper-prevention by itself (you need integrity verification at read time, and ideally external anchoring like timestamps or blockchain-like structures for true immutability). The proposal says \"tamper-proof\" which is a strong claim — hashing makes it tamper-*evident*, not inherently tamper-proof unless you add more mechanisms.\n- \"Centralized\" is a concern from an engineering standpoint: single point of failure, potential bottleneck for high-throughput logging. But for prototyping purposes, starting centralized is reasonable and can be distributed later.\n- The claim about not compromising latency is plausible IF the logging is asynchronous (fire-and-forget to a queue that feeds into the WORM store). If it's synchronous writes to the audit vault in the critical path, it WILL add latency. The proposal doesn't specify whether logging is sync or async.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping Assessment:**\n- A WORM store with hashed logs is relatively straightforward to prototype. You can stand up a basic version quickly: a message queue → append-only file/database → hash chain verification.\n- However, \"centralized\" and \"tamper-proof\" add complexity. True tamper-proofing (e.g., cryptographic anchoring, multi-party sign-off) is more complex than just hashing.\n- The separation from production data is actually good for prototyping — you can develop the audit layer independently without touching the core AI system. This modularity speeds up development.\n- One concern: \"specifically designed\" and \"centralized architecture\" suggests a bespoke build rather than leveraging existing tools (e.g., append-only databases like SQLite in WAL mode, or existing compliance logging frameworks). Building custom infrastructure for an R&D lab that needs to move fast on prototyping could be a resource drain.\n- Another concern: the proposal is somewhat high-level. It describes WHAT it wants to achieve but doesn't specify HOW — what WORM technology? What hashing scheme? How are logs ingested (sync/async)? What's the retention policy? This vagueness makes it harder to assess prototyping speed precisely, but the core concept is implementable.\n\n**Article 12 Alignment:**\n- Article 12(1) requires \"technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs).\" A separate WORM store supports this.\n- Article 12(2) requires traceability appropriate to intended purpose — the vault provides a place to store such logs.\n- Article 12(3) has specific requirements for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometrics): recording of use periods, reference databases, input data, and identification of verifying persons. The vault could store all of these, but the proposal doesn't address the *schema* or *structure* of what's being logged — just that there's a place to put it.\n- Recital 71 emphasizes \"clear and comprehensive\" documentation kept up to date throughout lifetime. A WORM (write-once) system has tension with \"kept up to date\" — if you need to correct or supplement records, WORM makes this harder. Though in practice, you can append corrections rather than modify originals.\n\n**My Verdict:**\nThe core idea is technically sound and aligns well with Article 12's requirements. Separating audit storage from production data is good engineering. The WORM pattern is viable and prototypable at reasonable speed. However:\n- It's somewhat generic — it describes a storage layer but doesn't address the *content* schema, the ingestion mechanism (sync vs async), or the specific event types required by Article 12(3).\n- \"Tamper-proof\" is an overclaim for just hashing; true immutability needs more.\n- For prototyping speed, building a bespoke centralized vault might be slower than leveraging existing tools initially.\n\nFrom my vantage point (technical viability + prototyping speed/quality): The concept is viable and the architectural separation is good for prototyping independence. But it's a bit vague on implementation details that would affect how fast we can get a working prototype. I'd lean toward approving because the core pattern is sound, modular (can be built independently of the AI system), and addresses a real regulatory need. The promise score should reflect that it's solid but not a turnkey solution — maybe 62-70 range.\n\nI'll go with approve at 65. It's technically viable, architecturally sensible for prototyping (decoupled from production), but the vagueness on implementation details and the \"tamper-proof\" overclaim keep it from being higher.\n",
