ing from limited regulatory logs) is scientifically interesting. The methodology of using synthetic failure modes to benchmark forensic capability is rigorous. It sits at an interesting intersection where compliance engineering meets scientific methodology. The broader impact is clear — any organization deploying high-risk AI would benefit from standardized forensic methods based on compliance data.\n\nThe main reservation is whether \"AI forensics\" as framed here is truly novel or a rebranding of existing work in model debugging/drift detection under a regulatory constraint. But even if it's an extension rather than a completely new field, the specific angle (regulatory-log-constrained diagnosis) does represent a meaningful contribution to the knowledge frontier.\n\nI'd lean toward approving this with a moderate-to-high promise score. It's well-scoped, methodologically sound, has clear scientific questions, and offers genuine contribution potential. I wouldn't give it 100 because \"pioneering the field\" is overclaiming and the core novelty depends on execution quality. But it clears my bar comfortably.\n\nI'll approve with a promise score around 72-78.\n",
