        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2491\",\n      \"rationale\": \"This proposal directly operationalises the core mandate of Article 12(1)—automatic event recording over system lifetime—as embedded, non-optional infrastructure rather than a per-project afterthought, which materially reduces the risk that any single team ships a high-risk system without compliant logging. The pre-configured Annex III templates and centralized immutable ledger also align with Recital 71's emphasis on clear, comprehensive, up-to-date documentation facilitating traceability, post-market monitoring (Art. 72), and regulator inspection; my residual caution is that 'immutable' must be reconciled with GDPR data-minimisation and retention limits, and the Annex III scope language should precisely distinguish Art. 12(3)'s minimums (Applicable only to Annex III point 1(a)) from the broader Art. 12(2) traceability duty.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 85\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "The user asks me to act as a Compliance Officer at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. I need to screen proposal P2491 from my professional vantage point: institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nLet me analyze the proposal:\n\n**Proposal P2491: Universal Compliance Logging Framework**\n- A standardized software library for automated event recording and telemetry across all high-risk AI prototypes\n- Serves as a shared dependency for every project\n- Modular middleware layer that automatically captures timestamps, input data, system states into a centralized, immutable ledger\n- Pre-configured templates for Annex III requirements\n- Eliminates need for manual logging in every new project\n- Preserves prototyping speed\n\n**My compliance assessment:**\n\nStrengths from a compliance perspective:\n1. Article 12(1) requires high-risk AI systems to \"technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\" A universal framework ensures this is baked in from day one across all projects, reducing the risk that a team forgets or deprioritizes logging.\n2. Article 12(2) requires logging capabilities appropriate to intended purpose for traceability, post-market monitoring, and operation monitoring. A centralized framework makes it easier to ensure consistency.\n3. Recital 71 emphasizes keeping records and technical documentation up-to-date throughout the lifetime of the system. An immutable ledger supports this.\n4. Pre-configured templates for Annex III requirements directly addresses Article 12(3) minimum logging requirements (timestamps, reference database, input data matches, identification of natural persons).\n5. A shared dependency ensures uniformity — one standard that's auditable by regulators rather than N different implementations that might each have gaps.\n\nConcerns from a compliance perspective:\n1. \"Prototypes\" — the proposal says it applies to \"all high-risk AI prototypes.\" Article 12 applies to high-risk AI systems, not necessarily all prototypes in early development. However, being proactive and building compliance into prototypes is good practice for institutional survival — you don't want to retrofit logging later.\n2. \"Centralized, immutable ledger\" — from a data protection angle (GDPR intersection), storing input data and system states in an immutable centralized ledger could create retention challenges. Article 12 requires logs over the lifetime of the system, but GDPR also has data minimization principles. However, for compliance purposes, having a single auditable source is actually valuable for demonstrating compliance to regulators.\n3. The \"pre-configured templates for Annex III requirements\" — this is good but I need to note that Annex III covers different categories (employment, credit, law enforcement, etc.) and the specific minimum logging in Article 12(3) applies only to point 1(a) of Annex III (biometric identification systems). The proposal says \"Annex III requirements\" broadly which could be fine as a general framework but needs to ensure it correctly handles the differential requirements.\n4. \"Eliminates the need for manual logging implementation in every new project, preserving our prototyping speed\" — from compliance, this is actually a positive because it reduces the risk of non-compliance due to engineering shortcuts or resource constraints.\n\nOverall: This is a strong proposal from my vantage point. It directly addresses the core requirement of Article 12(1) (automatic recording), supports 12(2) traceability requirements, and provides templates for 12(3). The centralized immutable approach makes regulatory audits cleaner. Building it as a shared dependency means compliance isn't an afterthought but embedded infrastructure.\n\nThe main risk I see is that \"immutable ledger\" might conflict with data minimization or the right to erasure under GDPR in edge cases, and there's a question about whether applying this to all \"prototypes\" (some of which may never become high-risk systems) is proportionate. But from a pure compliance-readiness standpoint, being ahead of the curve is better than retrofitting.\n\nI would approve this. The promise score should be high — it directly implements core Article 12 requirements in a systematic way that reduces institutional risk. I'd say around 85-90. It's not a 100 because:\n- The \"immutable\" aspect needs careful handling regarding data retention and GDPR intersection\n- \"Annex III requirements\" phrasing is slightly imprecise (Article 12(3) specifically applies to Annex III point 1(a), while 12(2) applies more broadly)\n- Applying to all prototypes including those that may never reach high-risk classification could be resource-misallocation, though from compliance it's defensible as a precaution\n\nI'll go with 85. Approve.\n",
