g (Article 12) and Recital 71's emphasis on keeping documentation up to date throughout the system's lifetime.\n6. \"Standardized API\" - this means it can be extended to new projects without re-engineering, which is efficient at scale.\n\n**Potential concerns from my vantage point:**\n1. Building a unified infrastructure layer requires significant upfront investment. Is this proportionate to our funding? However, if we're doing multiple high-risk AI systems (which the Act requires), building one unified system is far more cost-effective than ad-hoc solutions per project.\n2. \"Silent observer\" - need to ensure it doesn't degrade training performance or add significant compute overhead, which would waste resources.\n3. The scope is broad (\"across all high-risk projects\") - this is a good thing from an efficiency standpoint (one system serves all), but the initial build-out cost could be substantial.\n4. Does it overlap with existing MLOps tools? If we already have experiment tracking (MLflow, W&B, etc.), building another layer could be redundant unless this specifically targets the EU AI Act requirements that existing tools don't cover. The proposal says \"modular\" and \"standardized API integrated into our internal training pipelines,\" which suggests it's meant to complement rather than replace existing infrastructure.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a strong, well-thought-out proposal from my vantage point. It directly addresses:\n- Resource-allocation efficiency: one unified system vs. per-project solutions; automated vs. manual logging\n- Balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints: preserves researcher time for actual research while ensuring compliance\n- Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure: integrates into existing pipelines rather than creating parallel systems\n\nThe main risk is the upfront capital expenditure, but given that this is a regulatory requirement (not optional) and it's being built once to serve all high-risk pr