ntained (one compliance officer's time quarterly, plus stress-testing). It's not a massive capital expenditure.\n- The framing \"prevents expensive legal risks\" aligns with funding constraint awareness — non-compliance could mean fines or inability to market systems in the EU, which would be far more costly.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- This proposal doesn't require new infrastructure per se. It's a process/workflow addition. The compliance officer could potentially be an existing role or a fractional allocation.\n- Stress-testing retrieval of event records uses existing systems — it's testing what's already there, not building new things.\n- It leverages the logging that Article 12(1) already requires to be built into the system technically.\n\n**Assessment:**\nThis is a reasonable, proportionate compliance measure. From my resource-efficiency perspective:\n- It's not over-engineered — quarterly cycle is sensible (not daily/weekly which would burn resources)\n- The stress-testing of retrieval is practical and uses existing infrastructure\n- The gate before production is necessary but the proposal doesn't specify how heavy this gate would be\n- The \"designated compliance officer\" is a single point of resource commitment\n\nConcerns:\n- \"Stress-testing\" could become a resource black hole if not scoped tightly. How extensive? Every quarter? What constitutes sufficient stress-testing?\n- The proposal says \"quarterly audit cycle\" but also implies ongoing documentation upkeep (\"up to date throughout the lifetime\"). The quarterly review might be insufficient for truly keeping things current, or it might be overkill depending on system complexity.\n- It's somewhat generic — doesn't specify how this integrates with existing R&D workflows, which could lead to parallel processes that waste resources.\n\nOverall: This is a sensible, proportionate proposal. From my vantage point, it's a low-cost insurance policy against high-cost regulatory non-compliance. The resource commitment is modest and recurring but bounded. It doesn't advance scientific ambition but protects the commercial viability of R&D outputs. I'd lean toward approval with a moderate promise score — it's necessary and well-scoped enough, though I'd want to ensure the stress-testing component doesn't balloon.\n\nI'll approve it. Promise score: around 70-75. It's clearly needed, proportionate, and resource-efficient relative to the risk it mitigates. But it's not something I'm excited about from a research perspective — it's overhead. The quarterly cycle is reasonable. The main concern is scope creep on \"stress-testing.\"\n\nLet me settle on 72.\n",
