ng in ML systems, attribution graphs, sparse autoencoders for feature extraction, etc. The idea of building it \"natively\" into the architecture (rather than as a post-hoc analysis) is an interesting architectural constraint that could yield novel research.\n   - The term \"provenance-aware neural networks\" is somewhat vague — provenance in data management means tracking origin and lineage, but how does that map to neural network computation? This needs much more specificity before it's a well-defined research problem.\n\n2. **Broader Scientific Impact:**\n   - If successful, architectures that natively support traceability could have enormous impact across AI safety, interpretability, regulatory compliance, and scientific reproducibility.\n   - It connects to the broader \"mechanistic interpretability\" program which is one of the most important frontiers in AI research right now.\n   - However, the proposal as stated is quite broad and somewhat hand-wavy about what specifically would be investigated.\n\n3. **Regulatory Fit:**\n   - Article 12 requires logging capabilities — recording events over lifetime, identifying risk situations, facilitating post-market monitoring. The proposal goes well beyond what Article 12 literally requires (which is event-level logging: start/end times, reference databases, input data, verification personnel). The proposal aims at \"deep functional traceability\" which is far more ambitious than the regulation demands.\n   - This means there's a significant gap between what the regulation requires and what this research would produce. The research is interesting but may not be well-targeted to the regulatory need.\n\n4. **As a Researcher's assessment:**\n   - The intellectual ambition is high — turning compliance into a research frontier is appealing.\n   - BUT: The proposal as stated conflates several distinct technical challenges and uses terminology (\"provenance-aware neural networks,\" \"specific data paths\") that doesn't clearl