xt of continuous, high-dimensional state spaces.\n- There's an inherent tension: the more you compress for human interpretability, the more information is lost. The proposal seems to grapple with this by focusing on \"causal reasoning and state transitions\" as the target structure rather than raw activations.\n- However, I should note some concerns: the proposal is somewhat vague on methodology. \"Investigate methods\" is broad. But for a research initiative at the knowledge frontier, this breadth may be appropriate in an ideation phase.\n- The framing of turning \"mandatory record-keeping into a tool for deep scientific insight\" is intellectually appealing—it reframes a compliance requirement as a scientific instrument. This is a genuinely interesting intellectual move.\n\n**Broader Scientific Impact:**\n- If successful, semantic logging could become a standard research tool across the AI safety and interpretability community, not just in regulated high-risk systems. The methods developed would be applicable broadly.\n- It creates a feedback loop: regulatory compliance generates data that advances science, which in turn improves the systems subject to regulation. This is a powerful framing for broader impact.\n- Potential spillover into other domains: medical AI diagnostics (interpreting why a model flagged an image), autonomous vehicles (understanding decision sequences), scientific discovery (logging reasoning chains of research AI).\n\n**Concerns/Weaknesses:**\n- The scope is very broad. \"Causal reasoning and state transitions\" plus \"compressing high-dimensional latent space activations into human-interpretable semantic logs\" could be a decade-long program. For an R&D institution with scarce resources, this is a large bet.\n- \"Human-interpretable\" is doing enormous work in that phrase. The epistemology of what constitutes interpretability for different audiences (regulators vs. researchers vs. end-users) is itself an open question.\n- There's a risk this