sion.\n- A dedicated R&D team working on compressed log formats is overkill. This is an engineering task that can be handled within normal development sprints. You don't need months of research to decide between snappy and zstd for your logs.\n- \"Selective sampling techniques\" as a research area is where I'm most concerned. If the goal is to figure out how to sample logs while still meeting Article 12, that's more of a legal/compliance analysis question than an engineering one. You need to determine which events are mandatory (the Annex III minimums) and ensure those are always captured. The rest might be optimizable, but that's a configuration decision, not a research project.\n\n**Resource Commitment:**\n- This is framed as a \"research project\" - meaning it will tie up engineers for an extended period on something that doesn't require deep R&D. The actual work (choose compression format, implement log pipeline, configure sampling rules) could be done in weeks by the existing development team as part of system buildout.\n- The promise of \"transforming a mandatory compliance burden into a novel scientific contribution\" is marketing language that overstates the novelty. Compressed logging isn't novel. Selective event capture for traceability has been studied in distributed systems and auditing literature but doesn't require a dedicated AI-specific research project.\n\n**Where it could be useful:**\n- If we're dealing with truly massive models (hundreds of billions of parameters) where inference logs are genuinely enormous, there might be legitimate engineering challenges around real-time log ingestion without degrading serving latency. But even then, this is more of an infrastructure/SRE problem than an R&D problem.\n- The \"selective sampling\" angle could be interesting IF framed correctly: which events to capture in full fidelity vs. which can be summarized. But that's a design decision informed by the regulatory requirements, not open research.\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom my vantage point (technical viability + speed/quality of prototyping), this proposal has issues:\n1. It overstates the novelty - log compression is solved; selective logging based on regulatory minimums is an engineering configuration task, not research.\n2. It would slow down prototyping by creating a parallel R&D track for what should be part of normal system development. We'd need logging to work before we can ship anything anyway.\n3. The \"selective sampling\" language raises red flags - if it means not capturing all required events, that's a compliance risk. If it means optimizing which non-mandatory events to log in full detail, that's just good engineering practice done within normal development.\n4. It doesn't directly help us build high-risk AI systems faster or better. Logging is table stakes; you implement it as part of the system architecture.\n\nHowever, I should acknowledge: if we're genuinely dealing with edge cases where logging at full fidelity would cause unacceptable latency in serving (e.g., real-time biometric matching under Annex III 1(a)), there might be legitimate technical challenges around async logging pipelines, buffering strategies, etc. But that's an engineering problem solvable within a development sprint, not a multi-quarter R&D project.\n\nMy decision: Reject. This isn't technically novel enough to warrant dedicated R&D resources, it doesn't accelerate prototyping (it potentially delays it by creating a separate research track), and the core problems it addresses are standard infrastructure/engineering tasks rather than genuine technical challenges requiring investigation.\n\nPromise score: I'd give this maybe 25-30. There's a kernel of legitimate engineering concern (logging overhead in massive systems) but it's misframed as research when it's really an implementation task, and the \"selective sampling\" angle is potentially problematic from a compliance standpoint. It doesn't help us prototype faster or better.\n",
