ital 71 mentions \"comprehensible information\" - so making logs comprehensible aligns with the spirit of the regulation.\n- Publishing in top-tier venues could enhance market reputation as a thought leader in AI governance/compliance.\n\n**Against:**\n- This is explicitly a *research* project, not an implementation of compliance requirements. The lab already needs to implement Article 12's logging capabilities. A research project on \"novel methods\" for semantic log interpretation is not the same as building and deploying compliant logging systems.\n- The framing \"turning a regulatory burden into a scientific contribution\" is concerning from my vantage point. I don't want compliance treated as a \"burden\" to be converted into publications. My job is to ensure we MEET the regulation, not publish papers about it.\n- There's no clear timeline to deployment. Research projects on \"novel methods\" can drag on indefinitely while our systems need compliant logging NOW (the AI Act has specific timelines).\n- The proposal doesn't specify how it connects to actual implementation of Article 12 requirements for OUR systems. It's abstract research.\n- Publishing findings externally could create disclosure risks - sharing details about our high-risk system architectures, latent states, etc., in public papers could raise regulatory or competitive concerns.\n- From a pure compliance standpoint, we need working log systems that meet the minimum requirements of Art 12(3) for Annex III point 1(a) systems, or appropriate logging per Art 12(2). We don't need \"novel methods\" - we need compliant systems. Standard engineering practices (structured logging, event schemas, audit trails) already exist and satisfy the requirement.\n- The risk here: while resources are committed to this research project, the actual implementation of required logging capabilities might be delayed or underfunded.\n\n**My decision:** This is a research exploration, not a compliance implementation. While log