ent model types (NLP, vision, reinforcement learning, etc.) may have very different logging needs. A truly unified framework risks becoming a massive engineering project that takes too long to deliver value.\n- The infrastructure team is being tasked with this — are they already stretched? Adding a large middleware build could delay other critical work.\n- \"Standardized logging templates\" — there's a risk of over-standardization that doesn't fit diverse research contexts, leading to workarounds or non-adoption.\n- However, the proposal as stated is scoped to core elements (timestamps, input hashes, database references) which are fairly universal across architectures. It's not trying to solve everything.\n\n**Net assessment:** This is a strong resource-efficiency play. The lab has diverse projects; building logging separately in each is wasteful. A shared framework built by the existing infrastructure team is exactly how you maximize return on existing R&D infrastructure. The scope as described (timestamps, input hashes, database references) is reasonable and maps well to Article 12 requirements without being overambitious. The risk of scope creep exists but the proposal as written is appropriately bounded.\n\nI would approve this. It's a clear efficiency win that uses existing resources (infrastructure team) to solve a mandatory regulatory requirement across the portfolio. It frees researchers from compliance boilerplate, which directly serves scientific ambition within funding constraints.\n\nPromise score: This is very well-aligned with my concerns. I'd put it around 82-85. The main reservation is scope risk (\"across different model architectures\" could expand) and the load on the infrastructure team, but the core idea is exactly right for resource efficiency. I'll go with 83.\n",
