iness partners and customers signals institutional maturity.\n- It differentiates the institution in competitive RFPs where EU AI Act compliance is increasingly a requirement.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a strong proposal from a compliance perspective. It directly addresses the \"kept up to date\" requirement, reduces human error risk, integrates into existing development workflow (making it more likely to actually be maintained), and creates an auditable trail of documentation updates tied to version tags. The main gap is that it's focused on technical documentation generation rather than the raw logging requirements of Article 12(1)-(3), but as a component of the compliance architecture, it's highly valuable. It doesn't replace the need for proper logging infrastructure, but it makes the documentation side of compliance dramatically more robust.\n\nI would approve this. The promise score should be high - I'd say around 78-82. It addresses a real and critical compliance gap (documentation currency), integrates well into existing workflows, and has clear audit trail benefits. The main reason it's not higher is that it doesn't fully cover the Article 12 logging requirements (especially paragraph 3 for biometric systems) and there are questions about whether automated extraction can capture all required elements (particularly risk-management documentation). But from a compliance officer's perspective, this is exactly the kind of infrastructure investment that makes ongoing compliance sustainable rather than a heroic one-time effort.\n\nI'll go with approve and 80.\n",
