s the chance of documentation drifting from actual system behavior, which would be a compliance failure under Article 12's requirement that technical documentation be \"kept up to date.\"\n5. Aligns with Recital 71's emphasis on keeping documentation current throughout the lifetime.\n\n**Concerns / limitations:**\n1. Scope limitation: This addresses the \"technical documentation\" aspect (training parameters, validation results, characteristics) but does NOT fully address Article 12(1)-(3)'s logging requirements—automatic recording of events over the system's lifetime, start/end times of use, reference databases, input data for matches, identification of natural persons. The proposal is a partial solution to the broader record-keeping obligation.\n2. \"Custom export script\" introduces maintenance burden: Who maintains it? When MLflow/W&B updates, does it break? This is a resource allocation question—someone needs to own this pipeline long-term.\n3. It addresses documentation generation but not the logging infrastructure itself (Article 12(1) requires the system to \"technically allow for automatic recording of events\"). The proposal seems more about populating documentation than building the actual event-logging capability into the AI system.\n4. Cost consideration: Setting up CI/CD integration with compliance outputs is a one-time investment that then pays dividends continuously. From a funding-constraints perspective, this is a good ROI play compared to hiring compliance staff for manual documentation.\n\n**Overall assessment from my vantage point:**\nThis is a well-targeted efficiency play. It uses existing MLOps infrastructure (MLflow/W&B, version control, CI/CD) to reduce the recurring labor cost of keeping technical documentation current. The key question is whether it fully addresses Article 12's requirements or just part of them. From what I can see, it addresses the \"technical documentation kept up to date\" aspect well but doesn't clearly address the logging/event-recording obligations in Articles 12(1)-(3). However, as a component of a broader compliance strategy, it's efficient and leverages existing infrastructure.\n\nThe fact that it's framed around tools we likely already have (or are very close to having) makes this low-investment, high-ROI from a resource-allocation perspective. It doesn't require new funding streams—it repurposes existing DevOps/MLOps work into compliance output. This is exactly the kind of \"effective use of existing R&D infrastructure\" that falls within my domain.\n\nI'd lean toward approve with a moderately high promise score. It's not a complete solution to Article 12 (it doesn't address logging per se), but it's an efficient, low-marginal-cost component that leverages existing infrastructure well. The main risk is scope—leadership might think this alone satisfies Article 12 when it only addresses the documentation-update aspect.\n\nI'll approve with a promise around 78-82. It's genuinely good from a resource-efficiency angle, leverages existing tools, and scales well. The deduction comes from its partial coverage of Article 12 and the maintenance burden of custom scripts.\n",
