road in scope - this drifts into NLP/document analysis territory which is much harder to prototype well than log schema validation.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- If scoped narrowly (check that logging code emits the required fields per Article 12(3)), a prototype could be built quickly - it's essentially unit tests on the logging layer or a CI check on log schemas. This is very feasible.\n- But as described, the proposal bundles multiple concerns: log field validation + technical documentation scanning + pre-deployment gating + post-market monitoring support. This scope makes it harder to deliver a working prototype quickly.\n- The \"pre-deployment gatekeeper\" framing is problematic from an engineering standpoint: you don't have production logs before deployment. You'd be validating the logging *configuration* or *code*, which is better handled by unit tests and CI/CD pipelines that are already part of the development workflow. Building a separate tool for this adds overhead without clear benefit over simply adding assertions to existing test suites.\n\n**Key concerns from my vantage:**\n1. The conceptual framing (\"scans production logs\" as a \"pre-deployment gatekeeper\") is confused - you can't scan production logs before deployment. This suggests the team may not fully understand what they're building, which risks wasted prototyping cycles.\n2. Scope creep: bundling log validation with documentation scanning makes this harder to prototype and deliver.\n3. However, the narrow kernel (validate that logging infrastructure emits required fields) is technically trivial and could be a quick win - but it's already essentially what you'd do with good unit testing of your logging layer.\n4. The value proposition is somewhat redundant with existing CI/CD practices if scoped correctly.\n\nFrom my perspective: The technical core is feasible (log field validation is easy), but the proposal as framed has conceptual confusion (pre-deployment vs production logs) and