er, by separating regulatory concerns from research infrastructure, it actually PROTECTS prototyping speed - researchers aren't slowed down by compliance overhead on shared storage.\n- The proposal as written is somewhat abstract at the architecture level. It doesn't specify: what format? What query interface? How do logs get ingested (sidecar? embedded agent? middleware)? These are critical for actual implementation.\n- From a prototyping standpoint, if we're building high-risk AI systems subject to Article 12, having this infrastructure ready means each new system prototype can be connected to the logging pipeline without reinventing storage solutions each time. This is a force multiplier for rapid prototyping of compliant systems.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- \"Allocate a portion of our infrastructure budget\" - vague commitment. What portion? Is it enough?\n- The proposal doesn't address the actual Article 12(3) specific requirements (period of use, reference database, input data matches, identification of natural persons). It's about storage, not about what gets logged.\n- \"Immutable ledger\" could be over-engineering for what is essentially append-only log storage with retention policies.\n\n**Overall from my vantage point:**\nThis is a technically viable and sensible infrastructure decision that supports the regulatory requirement. The separation of regulatory archives from research data is good engineering practice. However, as written, it's more of an architecture principle than a concrete technical proposal - it lacks specifics on implementation approach, interfaces, and how it integrates with actual system components. From a prototyping speed perspective, it's enabling infrastructure but doesn't itself accelerate any specific prototype. It's a necessary supporting piece rather than a differentiator.\n\nI'd say this is moderately promising - the direction is correct and technically sound, but the proposal as stated is more of a budget/infrastructure allocation request than a concrete technical solution. It supports prototyping indirectly by keeping research infrastructure clean. I'll give it a moderate-to-good score. The idea is right but the execution details are thin.\n\nDecision: I'm leaning toward approve because the core engineering principle (separate storage tiers for different access patterns and retention requirements) is technically sound, well-understood, and directly addresses Article 12's requirement that logs be maintained \"over the duration of the lifetime of the system.\" The technical viability is high - this isn't speculative. It enables faster prototyping by preventing regulatory overhead from slowing research infrastructure. However, it's not a groundbreaking technical solution; it's good systems engineering applied to a compliance problem.\n\nI'll approve with a moderate promise score around 62-68. It's solid but not transformative from my vantage point.\n",
