ail, doesn't contribute to the literature on AI safety monitoring in a way that generalizes beyond \"yes, our logs work.\" It's essentially a checklist automation.\n\nHowever, I should consider: does building a framework to systematically probe the boundaries of logging systems in high-risk AI contexts have any research value? One could argue it might surface interesting failure modes in how ML systems degrade and whether standard logging captures them — but as described, this is framed purely as compliance verification (\"compliance unit test\"), not as exploratory research into system behavior. The purpose is to \"prevent expensive late-stage architecture redesigns\" — a project management concern, not a scientific one.\n\nFrom my vantage point (knowledge frontier, intellectual merit, broader scientific impact), this proposal doesn't clear the bar. It's competent engineering work but lacks the intellectual ambition or research novelty that would justify committing scarce implementation resources from a research perspective. It's a compliance deliverable masquerading as a \"test suite.\"\n\nDecision: reject\nPromise: This is low from my specific vantage point. It's useful engineering but not research-worthy in terms of advancing knowledge. I'd put it around 12-15. There's a slight nod because stress-testing logging under failure conditions could theoretically reveal something, but as framed, it's purely compliance QA.\n",
