        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P1624\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Article 12 imposes a present, hard obligation: high-risk systems must technically allow automatic event recording whose content is accessible to verify compliance, enable post-market monitoring (Art. 72), and support the operator's oversight duties (Art. 26(5)). A research initiative into ZKPs or SMPC for 'privacy-preserving' logs does not produce a working, regulator-inspectable logging mechanism within any applicable deadline; if the log content cannot actually be read by an authorised verifier, the system fails Article 12 regardless of how elegant the cryptography is. The GDPR/Article-12 tension is real but manageable today through conventional means (pseudonymisation, field-level minimisation, role-based access controls) and does not require novel cryptographic primitives to meet our compliance timeline; committing scarce implementation resources to exploratory research on this axis risks diverting effort from the straightforward engineering of conformant logging that we must have operational before the first high-risk systems reach their applicability date.\",\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"promise\": 28\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "The user asks me to screen a single proposal (P1624) from the vantage point of a Compliance Officer at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain of concern is: institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nLet me analyze proposal P1624: \"Privacy-Preserving Traceability Research Initiative\" — a dedicated research project focused on developing encrypted or differentially private logs that satisfy EU traceability requirements without compromising sensitive user data. It proposes zero-knowledge proofs, secure multi-party computation, prototyping within existing AI architectures.\n\nLet me think about this from the Compliance Officer's perspective:\n\n**Regulatory compliance angle:**\n- Article 12 requires high-risk AI systems to \"technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\" This is a hard requirement — the logs must exist and be accessible.\n- The purpose stated in Recital 71 is traceability, verifying compliance, monitoring operations and post-market monitoring. The information needs to be available to assess compliance and facilitate post-market monitoring.\n- If logs are encrypted or differentially private such that regulators or authorized persons cannot actually read them to verify compliance, this could directly conflict with Article 12's requirement. The logs must enable identification of risk situations (Art 12(2)(a)), facilitate post-market monitoring (Art 12(2)(b)), and monitor operation (Art 12(2)(c)).\n- There's a tension: GDPR requires data minimization, but Article 12 requires that logs be available for compliance verification. A \"privacy-preserving\" log that no one can actually inspect would fail to meet the traceability requirement. The regulator needs to be able to access and review these logs.\n- However, there IS a legitimate tension between Article 12 (logs) and GDPR (data protection). The proposal identifies this correctly. But the proposed solutions (ZKPs, SMPC) are research-stage technologies that may not produce compliant outputs in any foreseeable regulatory timeline.\n- As Compliance Officer, I need to ensure we can DEMONSTRATE compliance NOW or within the applicable timelines. A research initiative doesn't give us a compliance mechanism — it's exploratory.\n\n**Institutional survival angle:**\n- The EU AI Act has specific timelines (high-risk systems under Annex III have staggered applicability dates, generally 24 months from entry into force, so around August 2026). We need working log capabilities by then.\n- A research initiative doesn't produce a compliance artifact. It's exploratory R&D. If we invest scarce resources here instead of building straightforward, compliant logging infrastructure, we risk being non-compliant when the deadline hits.\n- However, if done in parallel with basic compliance work (not instead of it), it could be valuable long-term.\n\n**Market reputation angle:**\n- Positioning as a \"leader in ethical AI\" and solving the Article 12/GDPR tension is indeed good for market reputation.\n- But there's a risk: if we publicly tout privacy-preserving logs but then can't actually demonstrate them to a regulator (because ZKPs are still in research), that could backfire as overpromising or even appear evasive regarding transparency requirements.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThe proposal is well-intentioned and identifies a real tension, but from a pure compliance standpoint:\n1. It's a RESEARCH project, not an implementation plan. Compliance needs working solutions within defined timelines.\n2. The technologies proposed (ZKPs, SMPC) for this specific use case are at best early-stage. Log readability/inspectability is fundamental to Article 12 — if a regulator can't verify the log content, you're non-compliant regardless of privacy benefits.\n3. It doesn't address the immediate compliance obligation: building functional logging systems that meet Art 12(1), (2), and where applicable (3).\n4. The \"competitive advantage\" framing is marketing speak, not a compliance deliverable.\n\nHowever, I should note: there IS a legitimate research question here about how to minimize personal data in logs while still meeting traceability requirements. But as a Compliance Officer, my job is to ensure we meet the letter of the law within timelines. A research initiative doesn't do that. It might complement it, but on its own, it doesn't clear my bar for approving commitment of scarce implementation resources.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The instruction says \"approving a proposal means committing the lab's scarce implementation resources to pursue it.\" This is framed as an R&D institution — so research IS their business. But even within that framing, from a compliance vantage point, this doesn't directly produce a compliance artifact. It's exploratory.\n\nMy decision: I'm leaning toward reject or very low approval. The proposal conflates a research question with a compliance obligation. Article 12 requires working logs NOW (within applicable timelines). You can't say \"we'll figure out how to make them private later\" — you need functional, inspectable logs first. The GDPR tension is real but manageable through standard means (pseudonymization, data minimization in what's logged, access controls) without needing ZKPs.\n\nI'll reject it with a moderate promise score because the research question has some long-term value but doesn't meet my immediate compliance bar and could distract from actual implementation needs.\n\nLet me settle on:\n- Decision: reject\n- Promise: around 25-35. It's not terrible as an idea, but it doesn't clear my compliance bar. It's speculative research that doesn't produce a compliance deliverable within relevant timelines. I'd give it maybe 28 or 30.\n\nLet me write this up properly.\n",
