ou'd need a separate risk registry that references commit hashes, not the other way around.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping Impact:**\n- \"Mandatory internal workflow\" + \"every merge request\" — this is a significant friction point for prototyping speed. If every MR requires documentation updates on capabilities, limitations, data lineage changes before it can be merged, this adds latency to the development cycle. During rapid prototyping phases, researchers iterate quickly and may not want to fill out compliance forms for throwaway experiments.\n- However, if scoped correctly (e.g., only triggering when certain files/directories are modified, or only for production-bound branches), it could be manageable.\n- The \"real-time\" requirement for data lineage is a heavy engineering lift that would slow down prototyping significantly in the short term before paying dividends.\n- The proposal frames this as eliminating \"documentation debt\" — which is valid, but the implementation approach (mandatory prompts on every MR) may create a checkbox exercise rather than genuine documentation quality.\n\n**Assessment from my vantage point:**\nThe core idea — tying documentation updates to code change events via CI/CD integration — is technically sound and a well-known pattern. The Git-linked version-controlled doc repo is straightforward. However, the scope creep into \"real-time data lineage\" and \"risk management logs\" makes this more than just a workflow tool; it becomes an infrastructure project that touches ML pipeline instrumentation, metadata stores, etc.\n\nFrom a prototyping speed perspective:\n- Pro: Prevents documentation debt from accumulating (which does slow things down long-term when you need to produce docs for audits)\n- Con: Adds mandatory overhead per merge request during fast iteration phases\n- The \"every merge request\" framing is too broad — in practice, many MRs are refactors, test updates, or experimental branches that don't change capabilities\n\