rom the start. The Article 12(1) requirement says systems \"shall technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime.\" This is an architectural property — it's much harder to add later than to build in from the design phase.\n- The proposal frames this as a gate before \"formal testing\" which seems reasonable — you'd want logging infrastructure in place before you start generating test data, because those logs are part of what makes the system compliant.\n- Resource efficiency: A one-time setup cost (creating the checklist, defining the demonstration criteria) plus per-project verification time. Compared to the alternative of discovering non-compliance at a later stage and having to rework architecture, this is efficient use of resources.\n- \"Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure\" — this gate can be implemented using existing project management tools, review boards, etc. It doesn't require new infrastructure per se, just process integration.\n\n**Balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints:**\n- The gate could potentially slow down ambitious projects if it's too rigid or bureaucratic. However, the proposal targets only \"high-risk\" projects that are already subject to EU AI Act obligations, so it's appropriately scoped.\n- It doesn't constrain what you can build scientifically — it just ensures a specific technical capability (logging) is present before testing begins.\n\n**My overall assessment:**\nThis is a reasonably pragmatic process intervention. The core idea — verify logging architecture exists before you start generating test data and running the system — aligns well with resource efficiency. Retrofitting automatic event recording into a system that's already in formal testing or deployment is significantly more expensive than ensuring it's architecturally present at design time.\n\nThe main risk is over-bureaucratization: \"rigorous compliance checklist AND technical demonstration requirement\" could