bute to technical viability or prototyping speed/quality. It might actually slow things down by adding mandatory checkpoints.\n\n5. However, the core insight — that you need to know which systems are high-risk before investing in logging infrastructure — is technically sound. If I'm a dev engineer and I can spend 3 weeks building an elaborate logging pipeline for a system that turns out to be minimal-risk, that's wasted engineering time. So there IS a technical viability angle: right-sizing the effort.\n\n6. But the proposal doesn't actually solve any technical problem. It identifies scope. The actual implementation of logging (which is what Article 12 really demands) is not addressed here. This is a prerequisite/scope-setting exercise, not a solution.\n\n7. From speed/quality of prototyping: Knowing your regulatory burden upfront could help you design the right architecture from day one rather than retrofitting logs later. That's valuable for speed and quality. But the proposal as written doesn't get to that level — it stops at \"categorize projects.\"\n\n**Decision considerations:**\n- Is this technically viable? Yes, trivially — it's just a review process. It's not hard to do.\n- Does it address my domain (technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping)? Partially. It helps scope work but doesn't provide any technical solution or improve prototyping methodology.\n- Is it worth committing scarce implementation resources? This is more of a project management/legal function activity than an engineering one. The \"implementation resources\" here would be analyst hours doing classification, not engineering build time.\n\nI think from my vantage point, this is useful but not something I'd be the one to drive or that falls squarely in my domain. It's a necessary prerequisite for the actual technical work (designing logging architectures), but it's not itself a technical solution. It doesn't improve prototyping speed or quality directly — it prevents m