ly builds on what already exists — Git history, code comments, experimental logs, model metadata. It doesn't require new hardware, new data collection pipelines, or fundamental changes to how research is conducted. The pipeline is a software layer on top of existing systems. This is very efficient use of current infrastructure.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- LLM-generated documentation might miss critical nuances (limitations, failure modes) that only the researcher understands — but the proposal includes human review by lead researchers, which mitigates this.\n- The tool itself needs maintenance and updating as projects evolve — but this is a one-time build with incremental updates, far cheaper than ongoing manual documentation labor.\n- Quality of Git commits and code comments varies; if researchers write poor commit messages, the input data quality suffers. But this is an existing problem, not a new one introduced by the proposal.\n\n**Verdict:** This clears my bar. It's a high-leverage investment that converts existing scattered information into the structured documentation form required by the regulation, at a fraction of the cost of manual documentation efforts. It directly addresses Recital 71's requirement for clear, comprehensive, up-to-date documentation while preserving researcher time for actual science. The human-in-the-loop review step ensures quality without negating the efficiency gains.\n\nI'd rate this quite highly — maybe 82-85. It's very well-aligned with my concerns about resource efficiency and protecting research capacity. The only reason it's not higher is that LLM-generated documentation from code comments has inherent limitations in capturing the \"why\" behind design decisions, model limitations, and risk assessments — things that often live in researchers' heads or informal conversations rather than in Git commits. But the human review step addresses this. I'll go with 83.\n",
