on Article 12 record-keeping specifically (logs → documentation), which is squarely within the regulatory text I was given. It's not overreaching into other articles.\n- \"Real-time\" logging integration - this could have performance implications for running experiments, but hooks in existing pipelines suggest minimal disruption.\n- The scope seems appropriately bounded: it's a tool that converts what we already track into compliance format, not a new data collection system.\n\n**Resource-allocation assessment:**\nThis is a high-leverage investment because:\n1. It builds on existing infrastructure (low marginal build cost)\n2. It addresses a recurring operational cost (documentation) across ALL high-risk systems\n3. It frees researcher time for actual R&D work rather than compliance paperwork\n4. The \"living document\" model means ongoing maintenance is automated, reducing long-term compliance costs\n5. It's a one-time build that pays back with every audit cycle and every new system deployed\n\nThe balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints is well-served: this doesn't constrain research (it removes a friction point), and it uses existing infrastructure rather than requiring new capital expenditure on separate systems.\n\nThis clears my bar comfortably. It's efficient, leverages what we have, addresses a real bottleneck, and scales across the lab's portfolio of high-risk AI systems. I'd approve this with a high promise score.\n\nPromise: I'd put this around 82-85. It's very well-aligned with my concerns, but I reserve some points because \"automatically extracts\" into structured audit documents can have edge cases (novel architectures, unusual validation protocols) where the templates might not fit cleanly and manual intervention is still needed. Also, maintaining template updates as EU AI Act guidance evolves adds a small ongoing cost. But these are minor relative to the value.\n",
