red to capture these.\n\n**Compliance Officer assessment:**\n\nStrengths:\n- Directly addresses the core obligation of Art. 12(1) — automatic recording over lifetime\n- A standardized approach reduces risk of inconsistent implementation across projects (a major compliance gap)\n- \"Guaranteeing consistent record-keeping for compliance auditors\" speaks directly to my concern about audit readiness\n- Centralized infrastructure means easier updates when regulatory expectations evolve\n- Eliminates the \"forgot to add logging in this module\" failure mode that could be catastrophic during an audit or incident investigation\n\nConcerns:\n- The proposal is somewhat high-level. It says \"intercepts inputs, outputs, and timestamps\" but doesn't detail how it will capture the specific data points required by Art. 12(3)(b)-(d) for biometric systems (reference database identity, match results, verifier identity). A middleware that just logs in/out/timestamp may not be sufficient.\n- \"Throughout their entire lifecycle\" — does this include post-deployment? The proposal seems to focus on operational logging but Article 12 also implies documentation throughout lifetime (training, validation, etc.). However, Recital 71 distinguishes between technical documentation and automatic logs, so the middleware may appropriately scope itself to runtime events.\n- A single shared library creates a single point of failure — if it has bugs or is misconfigured, ALL high-risk systems lose their logging compliance simultaneously. This is an institutional survival risk.\n- \"Using decorators or API middleware\" — this is a technical implementation detail that may not scale across all model frameworks. If we have non-Python models or systems that don't use standard APIs, the wrapper might miss critical events.\n\nFrom my compliance vantage point:\n- This is clearly aligned with regulatory requirements and addresses a core obligation\n- Standardization reduces audit risk (auditors hate inconsisten