uctures in the systems it audits. If the underlying logging implementations vary wildly across the lab's high-risk systems, the toolkit needs adapters for each format, adding complexity. But a prototype targeting one system is still quick.\n- \"Real-time\" vs batch: a real-time streaming version is more complex but a batch/CI-integrated version could be prototyped very fast. The proposal says \"real-time\" which adds some engineering lift but isn't insurmountable.\n\n**Professional assessment:**\nThis is a well-defined, technically feasible tool with clear inputs (log streams) and outputs (compliance gap reports). It's the kind of thing that can be prototyped quickly because the validation logic is essentially pattern-matching against known required fields. The value proposition — catching compliance gaps early in R&D before costly deployment — aligns well with engineering efficiency. The scope is narrow enough to not become a bottomless pit, and broad enough to be genuinely useful across multiple high-risk systems.\n\nThe main technical risk is the \"real-time\" requirement if it means sub-second latency on very high-throughput logs, but even that is solvable with standard streaming infrastructure. For prototyping purposes, you could start with near-real-time (polling) and upgrade later.\n\nI'd rate this as technically viable, quick to prototype, and well-scoped. It's a solid engineering task with clear deliverables. I'd approve it.\n\nPromise score: From my vantage point, this is a good fit — bounded scope, clear technical path, fast prototyping potential. I'd say around 78-82. It's not a 90+ because \"real-time\" adds some complexity and the tool's usefulness depends on how well-standardized the logging is across systems (integration work). But it's clearly viable and prototype-able quickly.\n\nLet me settle on 80.\n",
