her than leveraging existing infrastructure.\n\n3. **Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure**: Labs typically already have identity management systems (SSO, access control), document management systems, and workflow tools. Building a bespoke \"identity-linked interface\" might duplicate functionality that exists in the lab's IT stack. A more resource-efficient approach would be to integrate logging into existing systems rather than creating a parallel database.\n\n4. **\"Without slowing down researcher workflows\"** - This is an aspirational claim. In practice, adding mandatory digital sign-off steps can create friction. The proposal doesn't address how this integrates with existing research workflows. If researchers are already using certain tools for their verification work, forcing them into a new system adds overhead.\n\n5. **Narrow utility**: This only addresses 12(3)(d) - the identification of natural persons in verification. It doesn't address 12(3)(a) (period of use), 12(3)(b) (reference database), or 12(3)(c) (input data for matches). So it's a very narrow piece of the compliance puzzle. The lab would still need to build/adopt solutions for the other sub-requirements.\n\n6. **Cost vs. benefit**: For a tool that only applies to Annex III 1(a) systems, building a dedicated system seems like over-investment unless the lab has many such systems. If it's just one or two systems, a simpler integration might suffice.\n\n7. **\"Immutable audit trail\"** - This suggests blockchain or similar technology, which adds complexity and cost. A well-designed database with proper access controls and change logging can achieve immutability without the overhead of distributed ledger technology.\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom a resource-allocation efficiency standpoint, this proposal:\n- Addresses a real but narrow compliance need\n- Risks building custom infrastructure where existing tools could be adapted\n- Only covers one sub-requirement of 12(3)\n- The \"without slowing