        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P1166\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The underlying idea—augmenting event logs with selected internal-state telemetry (e.g., layer activations or attention summaries at decision points)—is technically implementable via standard inference-time hooks, but the proposal as written is an open-ended research project with no defined prototype deliverable, no storage/compute-overhead budget for capturing tensor-level data on every high-risk trigger, and no specification of what a working prototype looks like versus \\\"deep forensic analysis.\\\" Article 12's mandatory logging requirements (timestamps, reference databases, matched inputs, verifier IDs) are straightforward event-recording tasks that do not require activation-pattern telemetry; committing scarce implementation resources to an unscoped interpretability research effort when the regulatory bar is met by conventional structured event logs is a poor allocation of engineering bandwidth.\",\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"promise\": 32\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal P1166 is \"Semantic Logging for AI Behavioral Analysis\" - it proposes developing semantic logs that record internal model states (activation patterns, attention weights) alongside mandatory regulatory events. It frames this as moving beyond simple event logging to a \"comprehensive map of system decision-making,\" designing \"new telemetry probes,\" correlating with Article 12 timestamps for \"deep forensic analysis.\"\n\nLet me assess from my technical viability and prototyping speed/quality perspective:\n\n**Technical Viability Concerns:**\n- Capturing activation patterns and attention weights during inference is technically feasible but comes at enormous computational cost. For transformer models, capturing full attention matrices across all layers for every inference event would be prohibitively expensive in production. This isn't a \"just add some logging\" task - it's fundamentally changing the inference pipeline.\n- \"Design new telemetry probes that capture activation patterns and attention weights during high-risk triggers\" - this implies instrumenting the model at the tensor level, which requires either modifying the model architecture or adding hooks. This is doable but adds significant latency to each inference call, especially for large models.\n- The proposal is framed as a \"research project\" - this signals it's exploratory rather than a well-defined engineering task with clear deliverables. For my function, I care about whether we can prototype and ship something reliable.\n- Storage implications: Attention weights for a model like GPT-scale would be gigabytes per inference. Even if you only log \"during high-risk triggers,\" the volume could be massive. This is a serious engineering challenge.\n- The correlation with Article 12 timestamps is straightforward, but the \"deep forensic analysis\" part is vague - what does that actually mean as an engineering deliverable?\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- This is described as a research project, not a prototyping task. It's open-ended (\"comprehensive map,\" \"scientific discovery\"). From my vantage point, I need to know: can we build a working prototype in a reasonable timeframe with clear success criteria? The proposal doesn't specify what the deliverable looks like at the prototype stage.\n- However, there IS a viable core here: you CAN add instrumentation hooks to capture selected internal states (e.g., specific layer activations, attention on certain tokens) at inference time. A scoped version of this is technically feasible and prototypable. The question is whether the proposal as stated has enough definition to be actionable.\n- The \"new telemetry probes\" language suggests building custom tooling rather than using existing frameworks like PyTorch hooks, TensorBoard, or similar. Starting from scratch on telemetry infrastructure when tools exist would slow prototyping significantly.\n\n**Compliance Fit:**\n- Article 12(1) requires automatic recording of events (logs). Article 12(2) requires logs that identify risk situations, facilitate post-market monitoring, and monitor operations. The mandatory requirements are relatively straightforward: timestamps, input data, match results, verifier IDs for Annex III point 1(a) systems.\n- This proposal goes far beyond what's mandated. While the \"comprehensive map\" might be useful, it's not required by the regulation. The regulatory text asks for logs of events, not internal model state telemetry. This is scope creep dressed as compliance.\n- However, having richer logs COULD help with Article 12(2)(a) - identifying situations that may present a risk. If you have activation patterns showing the model approaching decision boundaries, that could be an early warning signal.\n\n**My Assessment:**\nFrom a pure technical viability and prototyping standpoint:\n- The core idea (add internal state logging alongside event logs) is technically feasible but the proposal as written is too vague and research-oriented to commit implementation resources to.\n- \"Research project\" framing means unclear deliverables, unclear success criteria, no defined prototype milestone.\n- The storage/compute costs are not addressed - this is a critical engineering concern that would make or break feasibility.\n- However, there IS a viable engineering path here: instrument specific layers with hooks, capture compressed representations (not full attention matrices), correlate with event timestamps. A scoped version could be prototyped in weeks.\n\nThe problem from my vantage point: I'm asked to approve/reject committing scarce implementation resources. This proposal as written is too open-ended and research-flavored. It doesn't have a clear prototype target, doesn't address the engineering constraints (latency, storage, compute overhead), and conflates compliance logging with model interpretability research. The mandatory Article 12 requirements don't need this level of internal state capture.\n\nI'm going to reject it - not because the underlying idea has zero merit, but because as a proposal for committing implementation resources, it lacks the technical definition, scoping, and feasibility analysis that would make it prototypable at reasonable speed. It's a research direction, not an engineering task I can put a team on with confidence they'll deliver in a defined timeframe.\n\nPromise score: The idea has some merit - internal state telemetry could complement event logs for risk detection (Art 12(2)(a)). But as proposed, it's too vague and resource-hungry without clear constraints. I'd give it maybe a 35-40. It's not garbage, but from my engineering prototyping vantage point, it doesn't clear the bar for committing resources.\n",
