ching is problematic here because the regulation requires specific event-level recording (each use's start/end time, specific input data for each match). Sketching gives you approximate aggregates, not exact records of individual events. This could be a compliance gap.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping Assessment:**\n- This is framed as \"a theoretical project to develop\" - it's research-oriented, not prototyping-oriented.\n- The scope is vague: \"explore Merkle trees and probabilistic sketching algorithms.\" What would the prototype actually look like? There's no clear deliverable or milestone defined.\n- For my function (speed/quality of prototyping), I need proposals that can produce working artifacts relatively quickly. A theoretical research project on cryptographic logging mechanisms doesn't have a clear path to a deployable prototype in a reasonable timeframe.\n- The \"advances the knowledge frontier regarding observability in high-performance computing environments\" language suggests this is more of an academic/research pursuit than an engineering deliverable.\n\n**Relevance to Article 12 specifically:**\n- Article 12(1) requires automatic recording of events (logs). A logging mechanism is needed, but it doesn't need to be \"cryptographically secure\" or use \"Merkle trees.\" Standard structured logging with append-only storage satisfies the requirement.\n- Article 12(2)(a)-(c) specifies what events should be recorded for traceability, post-market monitoring, and operational monitoring. These are specific data points per event, not aggregates.\n- Article 12(3) for biometric systems (Annex III point 1(a)) requires very specific per-event recording: start/end times, reference database, input data for matches, identification of natural persons. This is fundamentally incompatible with probabilistic sketching which loses individual record granularity.\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom a prototyping and technical viability standpoint:\n1. The proposal is too theoretic