        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as Compliance Officer focused on institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nThe proposal P1100 is about \"Semantic Mapping of Latent States to Logs\" — essentially a research project that uses probing classifiers to translate internal AI model states into human-comprehensible event logs for Article 12 compliance.\n\nLet me think about this carefully from a compliance perspective:\n\n**What the regulation actually requires (Article 12):**\n- High-risk AI systems shall \"technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\"\n- Logging capabilities shall enable recording of specific events relevant to traceability, post-market monitoring, and operation monitoring.\n- For Annex III point 1(a) systems, minimum requirements are: start/end times, reference database, input data matching, identification of natural persons involved in verification.\n\n**Assessment from compliance vantage:**\n\nThe key question is: does this proposal actually help us comply with Article 12, or is it solving a different problem?\n\nArticle 12 requires that the system *technically allows* for automatic recording of events (logs). The requirements are about:\n- Recording specific operational data (timestamps, reference databases, input data, person IDs)\n- These are relatively straightforward engineering/logging tasks\n- The \"events\" referred to in Art. 12(2) are about situations presenting risk, substantial modifications, and post-market monitoring events\n\nThe proposal proposes using \"probing classifiers to map specific neural activation patterns to discrete events.\" This is a research project that:\n1. Is scientifically interesting but not what the regulation asks for\n2. The regulation doesn't require understanding internal model states — it requires recording of operational events (when used, against which database, what input matched