esn't require us to solve in this way. The actual Article 12 requirements are met through standard engineering (logging infrastructure, database queries, timestamp recording, access controls for person identification). This proposal would consume scarce implementation resources on an R&D exercise that doesn't produce a compliance deliverable and may introduce additional complexity/risk without proportional regulatory benefit.\n\nDecision: Reject\nPromise score: Maybe 15-20? There's a thin sliver of long-term value (understanding model internals could eventually help with broader traceability), but it doesn't address the actual compliance requirement and would misallocate scarce resources. I'll go with 15 — there's marginal intellectual value but from a pure compliance/institutional survival standpoint, this is not what we need to be doing.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The proposal says \"This advances the scientific understanding of model internals while meeting transparency goals.\" But does it actually meet the Article 12 requirements? Not directly. The minimum requirements for Annex III 1(a) are very specific and operational (timestamps, reference DB, input data, person IDs). Probing classifiers don't help with any of those. For the broader Art. 12(2) requirements, you need to log events — but identifying which events to log is a system design question, not necessarily a neural interpretability question.\n\nI'll stick with reject and give it around 12-15 on promise. There's no direct compliance path here.\n",
