evere reputational damage.\n- The decision-tree approach is practical — it's structured, auditable, and can be updated as guidance evolves.\n- Compliance office review adds a layer of expert judgment on top of the automated tool, which is important because Annex III categories have interpretive ambiguity.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- A decision-tree software has limitations: Annex III categories are not purely binary. There's nuance in \"intended purpose\" and whether a system is genuinely high-risk or falls outside scope (e.g., pure R&D exemption under Article 2(8)). A rigid tree might misclassify borderline cases.\n- The tool only works if researchers actually use it at the project proposal phase. Enforcement of the gate is an operational question, not just a software question. If the lab culture bypasses it, it's useless.\n- \"Gateway for ALL new R&D initiatives\" — this could create friction that slows research throughput. But from compliance perspective, a small bottleneck is worth it to prevent existential risk.\n- The tool needs maintenance as guidance, case law, and Annex III interpretations evolve. It's not a one-time build.\n\n**From my vantage point (institutional survival, regulatory compliance, market reputation):**\nThis is exactly the kind of structural control I would champion. Article 12 requires logging \"over the lifetime of the system\" — if you don't know at inception that your system is high-risk, you fundamentally cannot comply because you can't go back and install logging infrastructure that captures events from day one. This tool is a prerequisite for compliance with Article 12 specifically, and by extension, the entire Chapter III framework.\n\nThe risk of NOT having this: a researcher builds something in, say, Annex III point 5 (biometrics) or point 4 (education/access to essential services) without realizing it's high-risk. They deploy it. Six months later, a regulator or plaintiff identifies the classification. The lab has no logs from the early 