sts, which provides a check.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- We already have monthly resource reviews in place (implied by \"during monthly resource reviews\"). Embedding this classification step into an existing meeting cadence rather than creating new governance overhead is efficient.\n- The compute and storage resources we save on non-high-risk systems can be reallocated to projects that do need robust infrastructure.\n\n**Potential concerns from my vantage:**\n- \"Categorize projects as high, medium, or low risk\" — the Act doesn't really have a \"medium\" tier in the same way. It's binary: high-risk (Annex I product safety + Annex III standalone) or not. A three-tier system might create confusion, but as an internal resource-allocation tool it could work for prioritizing partial compliance measures.\n- The proposal is essentially a scoping/governance activity. It's relatively low-cost to implement (a few meetings per month, some legal time) and the payoff is potentially large (avoiding expensive logging infrastructure on non-applicable systems).\n- One risk: if we're in early R&D and projects are still morphing, a static classification might not hold. But \"monthly resource reviews\" addresses this by making it periodic rather than one-time.\n\n**Verdict:** This clears my bar. It's a low-cost governance mechanism that directly prevents wasted resources (compute, storage, engineering time) on compliance work that isn't legally required. It embeds into existing review cadences. The ROI from avoiding unnecessary logging infrastructure is substantial relative to the cost of doing the classification reviews. From a resource-allocation standpoint, this is almost essential — you can't efficiently allocate resources if you don't know which systems carry which regulatory burden.\n\nI'd give this a high promise score. It's not flashy R&D, but it's exactly the kind of operational efficiency that protects our funding and lets scientists focus on science. Maybe 82-85? It's strong but there are minor concerns about the three-tier classification being slightly imprecise relative to the binary nature of the Act, and the fact that early-stage projects might shift categories. But overall, this is a clear yes from my vantage point.\n\nLet me settle on 83.\n",
