ge\" reduces the pool available for actual R&D discovery. If we're already funding-constrained, this is a real cost. It's not free money — it's a reallocation within a fixed envelope (or an increase in total cost).\n- The proposal is somewhat generic and doesn't address *how* to determine the right percentage or how to calibrate it to actual Article 12 scope. It could become a bureaucratic overhead exercise in itself.\n- However, the fundamental principle — that compliance engineering needs dedicated, protected resources rather than being an unfunded add-on — is correct and important for resource-allocation efficiency.\n\nOverall assessment: The principle is sound and directly addresses my domain. A dedicated line item for regulatory compliance engineering is a proven approach in regulated R&D environments. However, the \"specific percentage\" mechanism is too crude and could lead to misallocation (over/under-funding depending on actual Article 12 scope per system). It's a reasonable structural idea that needs refinement but addresses a real gap. I'd lean toward approving it because the core problem it solves is genuine and the alternative (compliance as an unfunded mandate that silently erodes research budgets) is worse. The percentage mechanism can be refined in implementation.\n\nPromise score: This is a solid structural/financial governance proposal. It doesn't solve the engineering problem itself but addresses the resource-allocation question I care about most. I'd put it around 62-70. The principle is right (maybe 75) but the mechanism (flat percentage) is under-specified and potentially misallocating (pulls down to ~60). I'll go with 65.\n\nDecision: Approve — the core insight about ring-fencing compliance engineering resources is correct and directly in my domain. The percentage mechanism needs calibration work, but the structural decision to make it a protected line item is sound resource-allocation practice.\n",
