antage point:**\n- Building a custom middleware library is itself a significant engineering commitment. If the lab has only 1-2 high-risk AI projects, the ROI may be thin. But the proposal frames it as \"across all high-risk AI projects,\" suggesting multiple teams need this.\n- There's a risk of over-engineering: if requirements change under the Act (or interpretive guidance from the AI Office evolves), the middleware might need rework. However, the modular \"plug-and-play\" design mitigates some of this.\n- The proposal says our infrastructure team will develop it - this is an internal resource commitment rather than a new funding ask, which is favorable.\n\n**Overall assessment:** This is strongly aligned with my domain concerns. It eliminates redundancy (resource efficiency), protects researcher time for actual science (ambition vs. constraints), and builds on existing infrastructure rather than requiring new capital expenditure. The one-time build cost is well-justified if multiple teams are affected, which the \"across all high-risk AI projects\" framing implies.\n\nI'd rate this quite highly - maybe 82-85. It's a strong efficiency play. I'm not at 100 because there's some execution risk (building custom middleware is non-trivial), and the scope of \"all high-risk AI projects\" needs to be validated against actual pipeline volume to confirm ROI. But from my vantage point, this is clearly worth committing resources to.\n\nLet me settle on: approve, promise 84.\n",
