tes massive data volumes.\n\n3. Scope creep concern: The proposal conflates Article 12 compliance (which is about runtime logging of deployed systems) with research reproducibility and transparency of published work. Article 12(1) specifically says \"over the lifetime of the system\" for high-risk AI systems in operation, not during R&D. While capturing development provenance is valuable, bundling it into an Article 12 compliance solution muddies the technical requirements and makes the prototype scope unclear.\n\n4. From a prototyping speed perspective: Building this \"integrated software layer\" as a unified framework before understanding the specific logging needs of each system type under Annex III (paragraph 3 has very specific minimums) is premature. The right approach would be to prototype the logging capabilities for specific high-risk use cases first, then generalize.\n\n5. The proposal says it will \"drastically increase reproducibility and transparency of our published research\" - this is a non-technical justification that doesn't help with prototyping speed. It actually suggests the scope is being broadened beyond what's needed.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping Assessment:**\n- Building a single integrated framework is slow to prototype compared to building targeted logging for specific system types.\n- The \"wrapper\" approach assumes we can intercept all relevant events without modifying pipelines, which is often not true in practice.\n- Real-time immutable ledger introduces infrastructure complexity that slows down iteration during development.\n- However, the core idea of automated telemetry capture IS aligned with what Article 12 requires (automatic recording of events), so there's a valid technical kernel here.\n\n**Verdict:** The proposal has a valid technical core (automated event capture for compliance) but is over-scoped, technically ambitious in ways that will slow prototyping, and conflates R&D provenance with runtime logging requirements. 