        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2767\",\n      \"rationale\": \"This directly attacks the most common source of duplicated engineering spend in a multi-project lab: each research group independently building bespoke, potentially non-compliant logging stacks. A shared library amortises one-time development across all high-risk projects over their full lifetime (as Article 12(1) requires), frees researcher-hours back into science rather than compliance plumbing, and creates a single maintenance surface for future regulatory updates — exactly the kind of infrastructure leverage that makes fixed funding stretch further. The main reservation is scope realism: truly 'minimal code changes' across heterogeneous architectures (biometric verification pipelines vs. general-purpose models under Annex III) may require per-domain adapters, so I'd want phased delivery rather than a Day-1 universal claim.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 84\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as Lab Director concerned with resource-allocation efficiency, the balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints, and effective use of existing R&D infrastructure.\n\nThe proposal is a \"Unified Compliance Logging Library\" - a standardized software library that provides automatic event recording for all high-risk AI projects. Let me assess this:\n\n**Resource-allocation efficiency:**\n- This directly addresses my core concern. If every research group builds its own logging solution, that's duplicated engineering effort across the lab. A shared library means one-time development cost amortized across all projects.\n- \"Minimal code changes\" is attractive - reduces integration friction for researchers who should be focused on their science.\n- However, I need to think about whether a truly universal library can work across heterogeneous AI systems. The EU AI Act's logging requirements vary by category (Annex III point 1(a) has specific minimums like biometrics verification). A single library might need significant customization per project anyway, undermining the \"minimal code changes\" claim.\n- But even if some customization is needed, having a shared base layer with common patterns (timestamps, database references, event schemas) is more efficient than each group starting from scratch.\n\n**Balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints:**\n- This frees up research staff time - they don't need to spend weeks building logging infrastructure for each project. That's time redirected toward actual R&D.\n- The upfront cost of building the library is a sunk investment, but if it serves multiple projects over their lifetime (the Act requires logs \"over the duration of the lifetime of the system\"), the ROI is strong.\n- Risk: if the lab only has 1-2 high-risk AI projects at any time, the amortization doesn't work as well. But even then, building it once vs. rebuilding per project is still more efficient.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- A shared library can leverage existing internal tooling (our CI/CD pipelines, monitoring dashboards, data storage).\n- It creates a single point of maintenance - when the Act or its implementing measures are updated, we update one library rather than N custom solutions.\n- Potential risk: creating a dependency bottleneck if the library team is small. If they're busy, all projects wait on logging updates. But this is manageable with proper engineering governance.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- \"All high-risk AI projects\" - the scope might be too ambitious for an initial build. Starting with the most common patterns and iterating is more realistic than a Day 1 universal solution.\n- The proposal doesn't mention maintenance, versioning strategy, or how it handles the specific Annex III point 1(a) requirements (which are quite detailed). But as a screening exercise at the Lab Director level, I'm assessing whether to commit resources, not reviewing the engineering spec.\n- The claim of \"minimal code changes\" is optimistic but reasonable for common logging patterns.\n\n**Overall assessment:** This is squarely in my domain of concern and addresses it well. It's a force multiplier that reduces duplicate work, frees researcher time, and creates a single maintenance point. The resource efficiency argument is strong. I would approve this with moderate-to-high promise. The main risk is scope creep or over-engineering, but the core idea - shared logging infrastructure to avoid N redundant builds - is exactly the kind of efficiency lever I should pull.\n\nI'll give it an 82-85 range. It's a clear win on resource allocation, but I'd want to see phased delivery and not overpromise on Day 1 universality. The \"minimal code changes\" claim needs validation in practice. From my vantage point specifically (resource efficiency), this scores high because the alternative - each group building custom solutions - is exactly the wasteful pattern I'm trying to prevent.\n\nLet me settle on 84. Strong alignment with my mandate, clear efficiency gains, but some uncertainty about whether the \"minimal code changes\" and \"all projects\" scope will hold in practice across diverse AI system architectures.\n",
