his could create a bottleneck at the compliance function (me), creating internal friction.\n- The proposal is somewhat high-level as a \"governance framework\" — it doesn't specify granularity, frequency of gates, or what happens when a gate isn't met (pause? escalate? terminate?). But that's an implementation detail, not a fundamental flaw.\n- There's a risk this becomes bureaucratic overhead if over-applied to projects at early stages where documentation naturally evolves rapidly.\n\n**Overall assessment from Compliance perspective:**\nThis is strongly aligned with my mandate. The EU AI Act requires lifetime record-keeping and up-to-date technical documentation. A gated budget model that ties funding to compliance milestones is a legitimate, enforceable mechanism to ensure the institution doesn't accumulate debt (in regulatory risk) on systems it can't legally deploy. It protects institutional survival by preventing non-compliant deployments, supports regulatory compliance structurally rather than just procedurally, and enhances market reputation as an organization with disciplined governance.\n\nThe proposal is not a perfect implementation plan — it's a framework/governance principle — but that's appropriate for what seems to be an ideation-stage proposal. It identifies the right problem (continuous compliance over lifecycle) and proposes a structurally sound mechanism (funding gates tied to verification milestones).\n\nI would approve this. From my vantage point, it directly addresses a key vulnerability: without structural enforcement, teams may deprioritize record-keeping in favor of model development, leaving the institution exposed at deployment time. The gated budget model makes compliance a prerequisite for continued resourcing, not an afterthought.\n\nPromise score: This is highly relevant to my function. I'd say around 82-85. It's well-aligned but somewhat high-level and could benefit from more specificity on implementation (gate frequency, escalation paths, handling of iterative R&D where documentation evolves). But the core principle is sound and directly serves institutional survival and regulatory compliance.\n\nI'll go with approve, promise 83.\n",
