equirements that a generic middleware would need to handle with significant configuration or extension points.\n- A centralized \"secure vault\" introduces a single point of failure and a potential bottleneck for R&D teams who may be iterating rapidly. If the logging layer has issues, it could block prototyping cycles.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- This is where I have the biggest concern. An R&D institution's core value is speed of iteration. Requiring every research team to integrate a common API and stream telemetry to a centralized vault adds a dependency. If that middleware has bugs, changes requirements, or introduces latency, it slows down prototyping.\n- During early prototyping, teams are constantly changing their architectures, swapping models, experimenting with new data pipelines. A rigid logging layer that was designed for \"production\" high-risk systems may be overkill and actually slow down the experimentation phase.\n- The proposal frames this as removing burden, but in practice, integrating with a centralized system (auth, schema compliance, network latency to vault) can ADD friction during rapid prototyping. Teams would need to wait on middleware team for API changes, deal with versioning issues, etc.\n- That said, if done well (lightweight SDK, local buffering before streaming, flexible schema), it could genuinely help. The key question is whether the lab has enough engineering capacity to build and maintain this infrastructure while also doing R&D.\n\n**Assessment:**\nThe idea has merit from a resource-allocation standpoint — you don't want 10 teams each building their own logging. But from my vantage point, I'm concerned about:\n1. The \"one size fits all\" nature conflicting with the diversity of research pipelines\n2. The added dependency slowing prototyping speed\n3. The risk that the middleware team becomes a bottleneck for R&D iteration\n4. The technical complexity of making one API work across very different model architectures 