tly supports the Article 12(2)(a) requirement about identifying risk-triggering events\n- The engineering effort is bounded and doesn't require novel technology\n\nBut my promise score wouldn't be super high because:\n- It's more of a process/governance initiative than a technical prototyping challenge I'd naturally champion\n- The \"compliance task force\" framing suggests overhead that might slow things down\n- For AI systems specifically, defining what constitutes a \"system failure\" or \"substantial modification\" that needs to be tested is non-trivial and not fully addressed\n\nI'll approve with a moderate-to-high promise score. Maybe 68-72. The technical viability is clear, prototyping speed can be good (it's test automation), but it's somewhat outside my primary concern of rapid product prototyping and more in QA territory. Still, from a pure \"can we build this technically and quickly\" standpoint, yes.\n\nLet me settle on 70 - technically viable, buildable at reasonable speed, but not the most exciting prototyping challenge from my specific vantage point, and some AI-specific complexities are under-specified.\n",
