how who verified).\n- Market reputation: having a visible, formal verification process signals to clients and partners that the institution takes human oversight seriously.\n- Resource commitment: \"lightweight dashboard\" suggests this is achievable without massive investment. The scope is narrow and well-defined.\n- Risk of NOT doing this: If we deploy Annex III 1(a) systems without documented human verification, we're in direct violation of Article 12(3)(d), which could lead to fines (up to €35M or 7% for prohibited practices, up to €15M or 3% for non-compliance with other obligations under Art. 99). Also potential market withdrawal orders.\n\nThis is a clear compliance necessity for the specific systems it targets. I should approve it. The promise score should be high — this is a direct regulatory requirement implementation with a clean scope and strong control design. Maybe 82-88. It's not 100 because: (a) it only addresses one sub-requirement of Article 12(3), not the full picture; (b) \"lightweight dashboard\" needs to be validated as actually sufficient for audit purposes; (c) integration with broader logging infrastructure isn't addressed here. But from a pure compliance-necessity standpoint, this is nearly mandatory work if we're in Annex III 1(a).\n\nI'll go with 84.\n",
