ogging pipeline using existing columnar storage + compression libraries (e.g., Parquet with zstd), benchmark it against our log volumes, and validate that we meet Article 12(3) requirements for Annex III point 1(a) systems.\" That's an engineering task with clear success criteria, doable in weeks/months.\n- The proposal as written is too vague: \"compression and indexing techniques\" - what specifically? What's the hypothesis? What would \"success\" look like? Without defined metrics beyond \"minimize storage footprint,\" this will drift.\n- \"Staying within existing infrastructure funding constraints\" - this is a nice constraint, but it also suggests they're not willing to just buy more storage or use cloud object storage (S3-compatible) which solves 90% of the problem trivially for most workloads.\n\n**Article 12 Specifics:**\n- The regulation requires logging of: period of use, reference database, input data matches, and identification of natural persons in verification. These are relatively structured data points. They're not massive video streams or raw training data (though \"input data\" could be large).\n- For biometric matching systems (Annex III 1(a)), the \"input data for which the search has led to a match\" could indeed be substantial, but it's bounded - you only log matches, not all queries.\n- The reference database is finite and known; storing a pointer/hash rather than the full DB is trivially achievable.\n\n**My Assessment:**\nThe underlying problem (storage efficiency for mandatory logs) is valid and important for technical viability. However:\n1. It's framed as \"research\" when it's mostly an engineering problem with known solutions.\n2. The scope is too vague to produce a concrete, shippable prototype on a timeline that matters for regulatory compliance deadlines.\n3. It doesn't address the more urgent prototyping questions: What log schema do we need? How do we integrate logging into existing inference pipelines without latency regression? What's our retention policy? How does this interact with data protection requirements (GDPR) for the logged personal data?\n4. The \"scientific ambition in scaling models\" justification feels like a stretch - the storage problem is orthogonal to model size in most cases; it depends on query volume and match rate, not parameter count.\n\nFrom my vantage point: I'd want this reframed as an engineering project with specific deliverables (a working logging pipeline prototype meeting Article 12(3) requirements, benchmarked for latency < X ms added overhead, storage cost per million events < Y). As a \"research project,\" it's too diffuse and slow. But the problem space is legitimate.\n\nI'm leaning toward reject or low-approve. The proposal as stated doesn't clear my bar for \"technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping\" because:\n- It's research, not engineering → slower to produce a usable artifact\n- Vague success criteria → risk of drift\n- Known solutions exist that don't require \"research\"\n- Doesn't address the full logging architecture (schema, integration, retention, access patterns)\n\nBut it's not a terrible idea - the problem is real. I'll give it a moderate promise score but reject because as framed, it won't produce a fast, high-quality prototype that addresses the regulatory requirement. It's solving 20% of the problem in an inefficient way while ignoring the other 80%.\n\nDecision: reject\nPromise: around 35-40. The problem is real but the approach (open-ended research on well-understood engineering problems) won't produce what I need quickly or at quality.\n",
