e point of failure to fix when regulations update. This is operationally efficient.\n\n**Concerns:**\n1. **Upfront cost and engineering time**: Developing a \"unified\" framework across diverse pipelines is non-trivial. Different research groups may use different stacks (Python, R, specialized hardware). The \"universal API hooks\" may be harder to implement uniformly than the proposal suggests. This could become a significant upfront investment before any benefit materializes.\n2. **Centralized immutable storage**: This has infrastructure costs (storage, backup, access control) that need budgeting. But it's also a one-time build with predictable ongoing costs.\n3. **\"Integrated into all lab pipelines\"**: The word \"all\" is ambitious. In practice, legacy systems, specialized research setups, and rapid-prototyping environments may resist integration. There's a risk of scope creep or the framework becoming so rigid that it hinders scientific experimentation.\n4. **Platform engineers' time**: Diverting platform engineers to build this takes them away from other infrastructure work that supports research. However, if done well, the long-term ROI is positive because every future project benefits automatically.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nFrom my vantage point, this is a strong proposal. It directly addresses resource-allocation efficiency by building one solution rather than many ad-hoc ones. It protects scientific ambition by minimizing the compliance tax on researchers. It makes effective use of existing infrastructure (the lab already has pipelines; this integrates with them). The main risk is upfront engineering cost and the difficulty of truly universal integration across heterogeneous research stacks, but even a 70-80% coverage rate would be far more efficient than the alternative of each team solving logging independently.\n\nThe proposal aligns well with Article 12's requirements (automatic recording, timestamps, database references, input data, user identific