edicated research project when off-the-shelf solutions already get you 5-20x reduction on structured logs.\n- \"Maintain full traceability\" is the constraint — but this means lossless compression, which further limits the novelty space. You're basically asking for better lossless compression on structured event data, where the state of the art (Zstandard, etc.) is already very good.\n- For prototyping speed: during development and testing, I'd use local disk or object storage with standard compression. The marginal savings from a custom algorithm wouldn't significantly accelerate my prototyping cycle. The bottleneck in prototyping is typically compute for model training/inference, not log storage.\n- \"New compression algorithms specifically tailored\" — this language suggests building something novel rather than engineering a solution. From a speed-of-prototyping perspective, I'd want to pick an existing library and integrate it, not wait months/years for a custom algorithm to be researched.\n- The proposal conflates \"legal necessity of exhaustive record-keeping\" with infrastructure optimization. Article 12 requires logging capabilities; it doesn't mandate any particular storage format or compression scheme. We can meet the requirement with standard tools.\n\n**My assessment:** This is a research/exploration project dressed up as an engineering need. From my vantage point (technical viability and prototyping speed), the technical problem is solvable with existing well-understood techniques. Committing scarce implementation resources to a \"dedicated research project\" to reinvent wheel-level compression for structured logs doesn't clear my bar. The actual engineering task — designing a logging pipeline that meets Article 12 requirements with reasonable storage costs — can be done as part of the main system development using existing tools, without needing a separate research track.\n\nHowever, I should note: if the system is at truly massive scale (billions of e