ns/Concerns:\n- The proposal is narrowly scoped to biometric identification (Annex III point 1(a)). This is fine - it's one module among many needed. It doesn't need to cover everything.\n- \"Hard-coded into the API\" - this is actually a good compliance design choice because it makes logging non-negotiable at the system architecture level rather than relying on developer discipline.\n- The proposal mentions \"severe regulatory penalties\" which aligns with my institutional survival concern.\n- It addresses traceability, which is explicitly called out in Recital 71 as essential.\n\nFrom a compliance standpoint:\n- This is a directly responsive implementation of mandatory legal requirements (Article 12(3) uses \"shall provide, at a minimum\")\n- The hard-coded approach reduces risk of non-compliance\n- It addresses the most specific and prescriptive parts of Article 12\n- The focus on verifiers (Art. 14(5)) is important because that's where human oversight meets documentation\n\nHowever, I should note:\n- The proposal seems to only address some of Art. 12(3)(a)-(d) - it mentions reference databases and verifier identities but doesn't explicitly mention the start/end timestamps or the matched input data. But \"high-granularity traceability\" might encompass those.\n- It's a focused, implementable module rather than an aspirational program.\n\nFrom my compliance perspective, this is a solid, directly actionable proposal that addresses mandatory requirements with a design pattern (hard-coded API fields) that minimizes the risk of non-compliance. This protects institutional survival and market reputation. I would approve it.\n\nPromise score: This is a strong proposal from a compliance standpoint. It addresses explicit \"shall\" requirements with a technically robust implementation approach. The main reservation is whether it's complete (does it cover all four sub-points of Art. 12(3)?) but the core concept is sound. I'd rate it around 82-85. It's not a 90+ because the p