nting a mandatory obligation as an optional research pursuit could undermine our credibility with regulators and market partners.\n\nThis proposal, while intellectually interesting, does not clear my bar for approval from a compliance perspective. It's a research idea that borrows the language of compliance but doesn't commit to any compliance deliverable. I would reject it — not because logging-for-interpretability is bad (it could be a useful byproduct), but because as framed, it's not a response to Article 12's obligations and consuming resources on it risks diverting attention from actual implementation.\n\nMy promise score: It has some peripheral value — the causal analysis of logs could eventually inform better compliance monitoring practices. But it's fundamentally misaligned with what I need approved from this function. I'd say around 15-20. The idea isn't harmful, but it's not a compliance deliverable and the framing is problematic for our regulatory posture.\n",
