        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from the vantage point of a Development Engineer concerned with technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal P2240 is about \"Research into Automated Provenance Tracking for Large-Scale AI\" — essentially funding a small research team to explore compressed logging formats and cryptographic hashing for input/output verification, aiming to publish in top-tier venues.\n\nLet me think about this from my specific professional vantage point:\n\n**Technical Viability of Solutions:**\n- The proposal frames Article 12 compliance as a \"new scientific initiative\" rather than an engineering implementation task. Article 12 requires that high-risk AI systems \"technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs)\" — this is an engineering requirement on our systems, not a research question. We need logging infrastructure that works in production.\n- The idea to shift \"regulatory burden into a formal research objective\" is a concerning framing from my perspective. Article 12 isn't ambiguous enough or novel enough to require \"research\" — it's an engineering implementation problem. We know what logs are, we know how to record events, we know timestamps, database references, input data recording. The challenge is making this efficient at scale and low-overhead, which is a well-understood systems engineering problem (structured logging, append-only logs, content-addressable storage, etc.), not an open research question.\n- \"Compressed logging formats\" — this is a solved problem. We have brotli, zstd, protobuf, flatbuffers. The overhead concerns are addressed by existing engineering solutions.\n- \"Cryptographic hashing for input and output verification\" — again, well-understood technology (SHA-256, Merkle trees). This isn't a research gap.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- The proposal explicitly frames this as a \"research initiative\" with publication goals. From my vantage point, this is the opposite of what I need: I need working logging infrastructure in our systems within the compliance timeline (Article 12 applies to high-risk systems placed on the market). Research timelines are open-ended; compliance deadlines are not.\n- Funding a \"small team\" for exploratory research with publication goals means those engineers are NOT building the actual logging system we need to ship. This is an opportunity cost — engineers who could be implementing Article 12 compliance in our product pipeline are instead writing papers.\n- The framing of \"turns a regulatory requirement into a peer-reviewed scientific contribution that enhances our institutional prestige\" suggests the primary motivation is reputational rather than engineering delivery. That's not aligned with my function of ensuring technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\n**What would be technically viable from my perspective:**\n- A concrete engineering spec for logging infrastructure integrated into our AI system pipeline\n- Prototypes of structured event loggers with the fields specified in Article 12(3)(a)-(d)\n- Performance benchmarks showing overhead is acceptable (e.g., <5% latency impact)\n- Integration into CI/CD and monitoring pipelines\n\n**Assessment:**\nThe proposal has some adjacent technical content (logging, hashing) but fundamentally mischaracterizes an engineering implementation problem as a research question. From my vantage point on technical viability and speed of prototyping, this is problematic because:\n1. It doesn't produce a shippable solution within compliance timelines\n2. The \"research\" framing delays actual engineering work\n3. The technologies mentioned are not genuinely novel — they're implementation challenges, not research gaps\n4. Publication goals don't serve the function of getting compliant logging into production systems\n\nHowever, I should note there's a kernel of value: understanding compression and hashing for large-scale data provenance is relevant to making logging practical at scale. But that's an engineering design task, not a \"scientific initiative.\"\n\nMy decision: reject. The proposal mischaracterizes the nature of the work (engineering vs. research), doesn't commit to a deliverable timeline aligned with compliance needs, and prioritizes publication prestige over prototyping speed. From my vantage point, I need working logging infrastructure, not papers on hashing algorithms.\n\nPromise score: This is low from my perspective. There's a small kernel of relevant technical content (compression for large logs), but the framing as open-ended research with publication goals is misaligned with what I need to deliver. Maybe 15-20? It's not zero because the underlying technical questions (how to log high-dimensional data efficiently) are real engineering concerns, but the proposal as stated doesn't serve my function.\n",
