t might not handle all deployment topologies (cloud, on-prem, edge).\n\n- That said, building a shared logging SDK/library is a very common and technically sound pattern. Most ML platforms already have some form of experiment tracking or execution logging. Extending this to meet specific regulatory requirements is feasible.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- Pro: Centralizing compliance logging in infrastructure means individual researchers don't need to build their own logging for each project. This reduces per-project setup time and ensures consistency.\n- Con: There's a risk of over-engineering or creating a rigid framework that doesn't fit novel architectures. If the middleware is too opinionated, it could slow prototyping when you're trying something new (e.g., a different serving architecture, distributed inference).\n- Con: \"Minimizes researcher overhead\" — this is true in steady-state, but there's upfront development cost. The systems engineers need to build and maintain this library. For early-stage prototypes where the system design isn't stable yet, forcing a standardized wrapper might add friction rather than reduce it.\n- Pro: Once built, every new high-risk project gets compliance logging \"for free\" (or nearly free), which accelerates time-to-compliance for subsequent projects.\n\n**Overall assessment from my vantage point:**\nThe core idea is technically sound and a well-established pattern in software engineering. A shared logging library/SDK is viable. However, the proposal is somewhat high-level and underestimates the heterogeneity of \"all high-risk projects.\" The specific fields required by Article 12(3) for biometric systems (reference database, input data for matches, verification person IDs) are quite system-specific and won't be uniformly capturable by a generic middleware wrapper.\n\nBut the general architecture — a base-environment library that handles common concerns (timestamps, metadata, user IDs) while providing extension poin