nted per-model.\n\n**From my Compliance Officer vantage point:**\n\nPositives:\n- Directly addresses Article 12(1) — automatic logging over lifetime\n- \"Immutable database\" supports audit trails and traceability (Recital 71 emphasis on traceability)\n- \"Standardized, system-wide\" approach reduces compliance risk of inconsistent implementation across models\n- \"Export logs instantly during audits\" directly supports regulatory inspection readiness\n- \"Middleware wrapper for deployment pipeline\" is a practical architectural choice that doesn't require retrofitting individual models\n- \"Secure\" addresses data protection concerns (GDPR interplay)\n- Protects against liability — important for institutional survival\n\nConcerns:\n- The proposal is somewhat high-level; it mentions \"event timestamps and data inputs\" but doesn't explicitly map to the specific event categories in 12(2)(a)-(c). A compliance officer would want assurance that the framework captures risk-relevant events, not just operational telemetry.\n- Doesn't mention retention period requirements or how logs are kept \"up to date\" (Recital 71 says technical documentation should be kept up to date throughout lifetime)\n- \"Permanent record\" is good but doesn't address what happens at end-of-life\n- Doesn't explicitly address the Annex III point 1(a) specific minimums in 12(3), though that could be a sub-implementation detail\n- No mention of who has access, governance of the logging system itself, or how log integrity is maintained beyond \"immutable\"\n\nHowever, as a proposal at this level (it's an ideation candidate, not a full implementation plan), it captures the right architectural direction. The core commitment — building a standardized automatic logging layer with immutable storage and audit-ready export — directly maps to Article 12(1) and supports 12(2). The \"standardized\" aspect is important for compliance because it ensures consistent coverage across all high-risk models.\n\nFrom my 