 of Article 12(1): automatic recording of events over system lifetime\n- The \"uniform compliance across the entire lab portfolio\" language is exactly what a regulator would want to see — consistent traceability\n- Reduces risk of individual projects having gaps in logging (a common audit finding)\n- \"Without requiring manual intervention\" aligns with the \"automatic\" requirement\n- Timestamps and user identities are foundational for traceability\n- A shared, standardized approach makes it easier to demonstrate compliance across multiple systems\n\nConcerns/Risks:\n- The proposal is somewhat high-level. It doesn't specify how it handles the specific minimum requirements in Article 12(3) (e.g., reference database recording, match data). These are domain-specific and may not be captured by a generic \"events, timestamps, user identities\" layer.\n- \"Integrated into our existing compute clusters\" — what about systems that run outside those clusters? Edge deployments, third-party integrations?\n- The proposal doesn't mention retention periods, log immutability, access controls, or how logs would be made available to notified bodies/regulators upon request (which is implied by the traceability purpose in Recital 71).\n- \"Maintaining research velocity\" is a good framing but from compliance perspective I'd want more specificity on what events are captured and how they map to Article 12(2)(a)-(c) categories.\n- No mention of how the middleware handles updates/modifications to logging schemas as regulations or system designs evolve (Recital 71: \"kept up to date, appropriately throughout the lifetime\")\n\nOverall: This is a strong foundational proposal that directly addresses the letter and spirit of Article 12. It's strategically sound for compliance because it creates a single point of accountability, reduces per-project risk, and scales. The concerns are about implementation detail rather than conceptual direction. From my vantage point (institutional survival, regulatory compliance, market reputation), this is exactly the kind of infrastructure investment that demonstrates to regulators we take record-keeping seriously and have systemic controls in place.\n\nI would approve this. It's a core enabler for Article 12 compliance across the portfolio. The promise score should be high — maybe 85-90. It's not a perfect plan (lacks detail on retention, immutability, specific Annex III(1)(a) data capture, log export formats for regulators), but as a candidate proposal it points in exactly the right direction and addresses the most fundamental requirement.\n\nLet me settle on 87: strong directional alignment with Article 12, addresses institutional-level compliance risk, but needs more specificity to fully cover all sub-requirements.\n",
