        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2368\",\n      \"rationale\": \"This proposal sits squarely at the interpretability/mechanistic-observability frontier: capturing real-time latent activations and attention traces from production high-risk systems creates a data resource that does not exist in current ML research, where most internal-state analysis is confined to static or controlled settings. The intellectual merit is strong—transforming a regulatory logging mandate into a systematic population-level observability instrument for studying how deployed architectures actually route information through their representations is a qualitatively new research capability with clear downstream impact on reproducibility, distribution-shift detection, and cross-architecture comparative science.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 83\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as a Researcher at an R&D institution, focused on: the knowledge frontier, intellectual merit of research, and broader scientific impact.\n\nThe proposal is P2368: \"AI Observability Framework for Scientific Reproducibility.\" It proposes building a high-fidelity telemetry system that captures internal model states (latent activations, attention maps) alongside mandatory regulatory logs, stored in queryable format for post-hoc analysis, elevating compliance data into a research asset.\n\nLet me assess this from my research vantage point:\n\n**Knowledge frontier:** This is genuinely at the knowledge frontier. Understanding internal mechanics of high-risk AI systems—how they make decisions, what their latent representations look like during inference—is one of the most active and important frontiers in ML interpretability research. Capturing real-time latent activations and attention maps from production high-risk systems (not just lab models) is a significant step forward. Most current interpretability work happens on static datasets or controlled environments; getting this data from actual deployment contexts with real inputs, real edge cases, and the full decision pipeline would be enormously valuable scientifically.\n\n**Intellectual merit:** The idea of integrating specialized hooks into neural architectures to export latent states during real-time execution is technically substantive. It's not just logging I/O (which Article 12 already requires) but capturing the internal representational geometry. This connects to active research in mechanistic interpretability, causal tracing, and circuit discovery. Storing these in queryable format enables population-level analysis across many inference events—something that's very hard to do in controlled experiments because you can't easily reproduce the exact conditions of production edge cases. The \"scientific reproducibility\" framing is apt: having a comprehensive record of internal states alongside inputs/outputs makes it possible to verify whether another researcher could reconstruct the same decision pathway, which is a deeper form of reproducibility than just matching output distributions.\n\n**Broader scientific impact:** If done well, this creates a dataset resource that goes far beyond compliance. It could:\n- Enable cross-system comparative studies of how different architectures handle similar high-risk decisions\n- Provide ground-truth data for interpretability methods (you can validate whether your circuit-discovery method actually identifies the relevant pathway by checking against the full activation trace)\n- Support research into distribution shift detection, as you'd have both the input context AND the internal state to see how representations degrade\n- Create a benchmark for \"how well do we understand\" deployed systems\n\n**Concerns from my vantage:**\n- The proposal is somewhat vague on specifics: what neural architectures? What's the storage format? What's the computational overhead of capturing full latent states at inference time? This could be significant and might alter model behavior (observer effect).\n- \"Specialized hooks\" into production models raises engineering concerns but from a research standpoint, this is actually the right instinct—you need purpose-built instrumentation, not just logging.\n- The proposal frames this partly as compliance response, which slightly undersells its scientific value. But that's framing, not substance.\n- Privacy and data governance questions around storing latent activations (could they be reverse-engineered to reveal training data or sensitive information?) are real but don't negate the research merit—they're engineering/legal constraints.\n\n**Assessment:** From my research vantage point, this is a strong proposal. It transforms a compliance obligation into genuine scientific infrastructure. The intersection of regulatory logging requirements with interpretability research is underexplored, and having mandated, high-fidelity internal state capture across production deployments would be a major asset for the lab's research agenda. It directly addresses gaps in current knowledge (we have very little data on how models behave internally during real-world edge cases in deployment). The intellectual merit is high—it's not incremental logging but a qualitative expansion of what's observable about model behavior.\n\nHowever, it's somewhat underspecified as written. \"Specialized hooks\" and \"queryable format\" are placeholders for substantial R&D work. But the core research question—can we build systematic observability into production AI to study internal mechanics at scale—is exactly the kind of frontier question I should be backing.\n\nI'll approve this with a high promise score. It's genuinely at the knowledge frontier, has clear intellectual merit as a research infrastructure project, and broader impact potential is significant (the data asset could serve multiple research programs). The main risk is execution complexity and resource demands, but that's an implementation concern, not a merit concern from my vantage.\n\nPromise: I'd put this around 82-85. It's very compelling scientifically, slightly held back by the vagueness of the proposal (it reads more like a vision statement than a research plan with clear hypotheses) and the practical question of whether \"hooks\" into production models will be feasible without significant performance degradation or behavioral alteration. But the core idea is strong and well-aligned with frontier research directions.\n",
