t aside the fundamental interpretability challenge, building counterfactual probe infrastructure that works reliably across all inference paths would be a massive engineering effort - months to years of work for uncertain results.\n- Article 12 as written requires logging of events (start/end times, reference databases, input data matches, personnel identification). It does NOT require causal explanation logs or counterfactual reasoning records. The proposal significantly over-engineers the regulatory requirement.\n- From a prototyping standpoint, this would consume enormous resources on a speculative technical challenge while leaving the actual Article 12 requirements (which are more straightforward: timestamps, data provenance, personnel IDs) unaddressed by this specific proposal.\n\n**Quality of Prototyping:**\n- A prototype that can't reliably produce causal explanations is not a viable prototype for compliance purposes. If the logging system produces \"causal triggers\" that are actually just post-hoc rationalizations (which is what most interpretability methods currently do), it could be worse than useless from a regulatory standpoint - it would look like compliance while providing no actual traceability.\n- The \"scientific breakthrough\" framing is a red flag for engineering viability. We're not building a research lab; we need working systems that log events reliably and reproducibly.\n\n**Regulatory fit:**\n- Article 12(1) says \"automatic recording of events (logs)\" - this is about event logging, not causal inference logging.\n- Article 12(2)(a)-(c) specifies what the logs should enable: identifying risk situations, facilitating post-market monitoring, monitoring operations. These are all achievable with standard structured event logging without needing counterfactual probes.\n- Article 12(3) for Annex III point 1(a) systems is very specific: start/end times, reference database, input data matches, natural person identification. None of these require causal interpretation.\n\n**My assessment:** This proposal is technically infeasible to deliver as described within any reasonable timeline or resource budget. It conflates an open research problem with a compliance engineering task. It over-engineers well beyond what Article 12 actually requires. From my vantage point of technical viability and prototyping speed, this would consume massive resources on speculative technology while not clearly addressing the actual regulatory text in a reliable way. I should reject it.\n\nPromise score: This is genuinely interesting as a long-term research direction, but from my specific vantage point (technical viability NOW, speed of protoping), it fails. The fundamental interpretability challenge hasn't been solved. I'd give it maybe an 12-15 - there's some intellectual merit to the idea, and if interpretability tools mature, a simplified version might have value. But as a commitment of implementation resources today? No. I'll say 13.\n",
