t a flaw per se; it's just a partial solution, and the proposal is scoped to what it claims to be.\n- \"Streamlined\" is subjective — without seeing the actual templates, there's risk that \"streamlined\" means insufficient detail for Article 12(3) minimums (for Annex III point 1(a) systems). But this is an implementation detail concern, not a fundamental flaw in the proposal.\n- The proposal says \"during model development\" — but Recital 71 and the Act require documentation throughout the system's lifetime, including post-market monitoring. However, having a solid development-phase foundation is a necessary precondition for lifetime documentation. You can't maintain what you never initially captured well.\n\n**From institutional survival perspective:**\n- This reduces regulatory risk significantly. If the EU AI Office or national supervisory authority conducts an inspection, having organic, version-controlled documentation is far more defensible than cobbled-together retroactive records.\n- Market reputation: being able to demonstrate a mature compliance infrastructure is a competitive advantage when selling to enterprise clients who need to verify their suppliers' AI systems comply with the Act (Article 26 obligations on deployers).\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a well-conceived, proportionate proposal that addresses a core compliance requirement in a way that's practical for an R&D institution. The integration into existing workflows (Git) rather than creating parallel administrative processes is exactly the kind of \"compliance by design\" thinking that reduces institutional risk while not strangling research productivity. It's a necessary foundational step even if it doesn't cover all of Article 12 (runtime logging is separate).\n\nI'd approve this. It clears my bar as something that meaningfully advances our compliance posture with reasonable resource commitment. The promise score: it addresses a real and important gap, the implementation approach is sound (leveraging existing infrastructure), and it's scoped appropriately. I'd give it around 78-82. It's not a 90+ because it only covers part of Article 12/Recital 71 (development documentation, not runtime logging), but as a standalone proposal doing what it says, it's solid.\n\nLet me settle on 80.\n",
