tegrate across heterogeneous pipelines.\n- \"Tamper-evident format\" — This is technically achievable (hash-chained logs, WORM storage, digital signatures). Not overly complex.\n- The scope says \"across all projects\" which is broad. From a prototyping speed perspective, this could be a very long-tail project.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- A unified framework for ALL projects is a significant engineering investment before any single high-risk system needs it. This could slow down prototyping if researchers have to wait for the framework or adapt their pipelines to fit it.\n- However, once built, it does reduce per-project overhead — you don't rebuild logging compliance every time. The \"removes manual burden from researchers\" claim is valid and would speed up iteration on individual high-risk projects.\n- The modular design (API-driven wrapper) suggests it could be incrementally deployed — start with one pipeline, prove the concept, then expand. That's good for prototyping velocity.\n- Risk: building \"the perfect unified framework\" before you know which specific high-risk systems are actually in development could mean over-engineering. Better to prototype logging integration per-project and extract common patterns.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThe technical approach is sound — structured, tamper-evident logging with standardized hooks is well within current engineering capability. The modular/API-driven design is the right architectural choice for prototyping speed (incremental adoption). However, the \"across all projects\" scope and \"unified...for any high-risk system\" language suggests a big-bang infrastructure project rather than an incremental prototype-first approach. From a pure technical viability standpoint, this is very doable. From a speed-of-prototyping standpoint, it's a solid investment IF scoped incrementally, but the proposal as written sounds like a large platform build that could take many months before delivering value to any single project.\n\nI think this clears my bar. It's technically viable (high confidence), and the modular design supports fast prototyping once built. The main risk is scope creep in implementation timeline, but the architecture itself is sound and would accelerate compliance integration for subsequent projects. I'd approve it with a moderate-to-high promise score — maybe 72-78. The technical feasibility is high (it's not cutting-edge R&D), but the \"unified across all projects\" framing makes me slightly cautious about whether it delivers fast enough vs. building per-project logging first and then consolidating.\n\nLet me settle on: approve, promise 74. It's technically very viable, the architecture supports prototyping velocity once in place, but the breadth of scope (\"across all projects,\" \"any high-risk system\") introduces timeline risk that could slow initial delivery.\n",
