ses the requirement that documentation be \"kept up to date throughout the lifetime\"\n- Version control (MLflow/DVC) creates a clear audit trail — this is exactly what traceability requires\n- Automated scripts during training cycles ensure records are created as part of the development process, not after the fact (reduces compliance risk of missing records)\n- Links model versions to specific data and validation logs — critical for demonstrating which version was deployed when, with what data, passing what tests\n- Market reputation angle: transparency to regulators and external partners is a genuine compliance benefit\n- This addresses the \"comprehensible information\" requirement by making it structured and linked\n\nWeaknesses/Concerns:\n- The proposal focuses heavily on the technical documentation side (Recital 71) but doesn't explicitly address Article 12(1)'s requirement for automatic recording of events (logs) over the system's operational lifetime. It mentions \"validation logs\" but the emphasis is on development-phase documentation, not runtime event logging during deployment.\n- However, it does say \"risk assessments in real time\" which could encompass some operational monitoring.\n- The proposal says \"researchers will be required to update these logs via automated scripts during training cycles\" — this is good for compliance (automation reduces human error and ensures consistency), but it only covers training cycles, not the full operational lifetime as Article 12(1) requires.\n- No mention of the specific minimum requirements in Article 12(3) for biometric systems (if applicable).\n- Doesn't explicitly address post-market monitoring logging (Article 72) or the risk identification logging under Article 12(2)(a).\n\nOverall: This is a strong, well-targeted proposal that addresses a significant portion of the compliance landscape. It directly maps to Recital 71's core requirements and provides a concrete technical implementation path (MLflow/DVC). The automation aspect is crucial for compliance — manual documentation is a major risk. From my vantage point on institutional survival and regulatory compliance, this reduces the risk of being unable to produce compliant technical documentation during a regulator audit. It also supports market reputation by demonstrating structured transparency.\n\nHowever, it's not a complete solution for all of Article 12 — it covers the technical documentation/record-keeping side well but the operational logging (automatic recording of events during deployment) is only partially addressed. Still, as a standalone proposal, it addresses a real and significant compliance gap with a concrete implementation path.\n\nI would approve this. It's directly relevant to our regulatory obligations, has a clear implementation mechanism, automates compliance-critical processes (reducing the risk of gaps), and supports both institutional survival (compliance) and market reputation (transparency). The promise score should be high but not perfect because it doesn't fully cover Article 12(1)-(3)'s operational logging requirements — those would need to be addressed separately or as an extension.\n\nI'd give this around 78-85. It's a strong, well-scoped proposal that addresses the core documentation compliance requirement with a concrete technical path. The gap is that it's primarily focused on development-phase records rather than full operational lifetime logging, but it's still a critical piece of the compliance architecture.\n\nLet me settle on 82.\n",
