d approach protects scientific ambition by concentrating the compliance burden in one place.\n- The modular API design means researchers integrate it rather than build it — low ongoing friction for science teams.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- This leverages existing codebases and project structures (integrated into them) rather than building a parallel system.\n- It creates a shared infrastructure asset that the lab doesn't currently have but will need anyway.\n- The \"modular\" aspect suggests it can adapt to different project architectures without forcing a monolithic rebuild.\n\n**Risks/Concerns from my vantage point:**\n- Building an internal software library is itself a non-trivial engineering investment. If the lab only has 1-2 high-risk AI projects, the ROI on a \"unified\" framework may not be justified versus each team implementing minimal logging.\n- The proposal says \"across all high-risk AI projects currently in development\" — if that's just one or two, the unification benefit is marginal. But if it's several, the efficiency argument strengthens considerably.\n- There's a risk of over-engineering: building an elaborate framework when Article 12's minimum requirements (especially for Annex III point 1(a) systems) are relatively specific and bounded. The compliance bar might be simpler than this proposal implies.\n- Maintenance burden: who owns and maintains this library? It becomes a shared dependency — if it breaks, all projects are affected. This is an operational risk that needs dedicated staffing.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nFrom a resource-allocation perspective, this is a strong efficiency play IF multiple projects need the same logging capabilities. The \"single compliant tool that scales\" language directly addresses the redundancy problem I'm responsible for. The modular API design minimizes disruption to ongoing scientific work. The main risk is over-investment relative to actual compliance requirements and number of 