l works. Article 12(3) specifically requires recording things like \"the reference database against which input data has been checked\" and \"the input data for which the search has led to match\" — these are domain-specific and may not be captured by a generic wrapper.\n- The emphasis on \"fast prototyping cycle\" suggests prioritizing speed of compliance over depth of documentation. From a research integrity standpoint, shallow logging that merely timestamps I/O without capturing the reasoning chain or internal model states may satisfy the letter but miss the spirit of traceability.\n- There's no mention of how this middleware would handle the diversity of high-risk system types in Annex III (biometrics, critical infrastructure, education, employment, etc.). A truly generic wrapper risks being too shallow for some and irrelevant for others.\n- From a broader scientific impact perspective: does a logging middleware library contribute anything to the scientific community? Potentially yes if it's open-sourced or published as a tool, but the proposal frames it purely as an internal efficiency measure.\n\n**Assessment:**\nThis is a competent engineering solution to a compliance problem. However, from my research-knowledge vantage point, it doesn't advance intellectual merit or the knowledge frontier in any meaningful way. It's infrastructure that enables compliance so researchers can focus on other things — which has indirect value but isn't itself a research contribution. The risk is that by making logging \"frictionless\" and generic, it may produce logs that are technically compliant but scientifically shallow — capturing I/O timestamps without the internal reasoning traces that would be valuable for understanding model behavior, debugging edge cases, or advancing our understanding of how these systems work.\n\nThat said, I should be fair: reducing compliance overhead does free research capacity. And a well-designed logging layer could include hooks for richer instrumentation. But as described, this proposal is primarily an engineering efficiency play, not a research-knowledge advancement.\n\nI'll lean toward reject or low-approve. The promise score should reflect that while it has some indirect value (freeing researcher time), it doesn't clear my bar from the perspective of advancing knowledge, intellectual merit, or broader scientific impact. It's necessary plumbing but not something I'd champion from a research-integrity standpoint. In fact, there's a mild concern that \"frictionless\" generic logging could incentivize shallow documentation practices.\n\nDecision: reject\nPromise: 25-30 range. It has some indirect value (reducing researcher burden) but doesn't advance knowledge or scientific impact. The framing as \"eliminating manual logging\" and keeping things \"frictionless\" slightly worries me from a research-depth perspective. I'll say 28.\n",
