ualitative/narrative parts of technical documentation (describing limitations, risk-management reasoning)\n- The \"real-time\" claim might be over-engineering for what regulators actually need; periodic snapshots may suffice and be simpler to implement\n- There's a prerequisite assumption that experiment tracking tools are already in place\n\nOverall, from my vantage: this is technically feasible, relatively well-scoped, and directly improves prototyping velocity by removing documentation overhead. It's not groundbreaking but it's practical engineering that addresses a real pain point aligned with the regulation. I'd approve it with moderate-to-high confidence. The promise score should reflect that it's solidly viable but not revolutionary — maybe 72-78 range.\n\nLet me settle on: approve, promise around 75. It's technically sound, uses existing building blocks, directly serves prototyping speed, and addresses a real compliance requirement. Minor concerns about overpromising \"real-time\" and the qualitative documentation gap keep it from being higher.\n",
