signed specifically\" could mean over-engineering from day one. A full deployment environment with its own infrastructure layer is a significant engineering commitment before we even know if the underlying model works well.\n- Counterpoint: The Article 12(3) requirements are very specific (start/end times, reference database, input data matching, natural person identification). These are relatively simple to implement in a middleware pattern - timestamps, database references, input logging, user ID capture. Not complex ML engineering.\n\n**My assessment:**\n- Technically viable: Yes, this is standard infrastructure/middleware work. No novel technical challenges.\n- Prototyping speed concern: The risk is that building this wrapper becomes a prerequisite for ANY deployment testing, even for low-risk prototypes. But the proposal explicitly says it isolates these requirements to only Annex III systems, which mitigates this.\n- Quality concern: A dedicated wrapper means one place to maintain compliance logic rather than scattered across individual model deployments. This is actually good for quality.\n- The \"infrastructure level\" implementation during inference is the right layer - you don't want to pollute the model code or training pipeline with compliance logging concerns.\n\nOverall, this is a reasonable, technically sound approach. It's not novel but it's practical. The main risk from my perspective is scope creep on the \"specialized deployment environment\" - if it becomes a massive platform project, that hurts prototyping speed. But as described (enforcing mandatory fields at inference), it's achievable with modest engineering effort and actually protects prototyping velocity by keeping compliance concerns separate.\n\nI'd lean toward approving this because:\n1. It's technically straightforward (middleware/interceptor pattern)\n2. It explicitly protects low-risk prototyping from compliance overhead\n3. The Article 12(3) fields are simple to capture at the serving layer\n4. It centralizes a concern that would otherwise scatter across projects\n\nThe promise score: I'd say around 72-78. It's solid, practical engineering with clear scope. Not groundbreaking, but exactly the kind of well-scoped infrastructure work that makes compliance manageable without killing dev velocity. The \"specialized deployment environment\" language is slightly concerning (could balloon), but the actual requirements listed are bounded and implementable.\n",
