uable for the lab's work in that domain.\n- By catching logging gaps early, it prevents \"costly late-stage redesigns\" which directly benefits prototyping speed - you don't waste time building features on top of a system whose logging foundation is broken.\n- The test suite itself is relatively quick to build (maybe 1-2 sprints for a basic version) and provides ongoing value.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- It's somewhat narrow in scope (only Annex III point 1(a)), which limits its applicability across the lab's portfolio unless extended later. But as a starting point, focusing on one category makes sense.\n- The \"compliance gate\" language could be interpreted as blocking deployment entirely, which might be too rigid for early prototyping phases. A softer \"warning\" mode during development and \"hard fail\" at release would be better.\n- However, the core idea - automated log validation in CI - is sound, technically feasible, and directly supports both compliance AND engineering velocity.\n\n**Overall assessment:** This is a technically viable, well-scoped engineering task that directly serves my domain concerns. It's not revolutionary but it's practical, implementable with existing tooling patterns, and provides real value for prototyping speed by catching structural gaps early. The main risk is scope creep or over-engineering the \"simulation\" aspect, but the core concept is solid.\n\nI'd say this clears my bar. It's a legitimate engineering investment that pays back in reduced rework and faster iteration. I'll approve it with a moderately high promise score - not 100 because it's somewhat narrow and the \"simulation\" aspect could balloon in complexity, but it's clearly worth doing.\n\nPromise: I'd put this around 72-78. It's technically very feasible, directly supports prototyping speed (catches gaps early), is well-scoped, but has some risk of scope creep in the simulation harness and is limited to one Annex III category.\n",
