o implementation pattern. From my vantage point (technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping), this is a process proposal that sits upstream of actual development work.\n- The risk: if classification happens too late or the checklist is ambiguous, it could create rework cycles. If a project is mid-development and gets flagged as high-risk, retrofitting Article 12 compliance is painful. But the proposal says \"during the project proposal phase,\" which is early enough to be architecturally meaningful.\n- Counterpoint: The classification process itself doesn't directly improve my prototyping speed or quality. It's a necessary upstream dependency but not something I'd champion as a dev engineer. My job would be to design the actual logging infrastructure once I know it's needed. This proposal is more of a PMO/governance deliverable.\n- However, there IS a legitimate technical viability concern: if we don't classify early, we risk building systems without the necessary technical hooks for automatic event recording (Article 12(1)). Retrofitting \"technically allow for the automatic recording of events\" into a system that wasn't designed with it in mind can be architecturally expensive. So having this classification gate DOES protect technical viability by ensuring the right architectural decisions are made upfront.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a reasonable governance process that indirectly supports my work by ensuring I get clear requirements early. It's not a technical proposal per se, but it reduces the risk of costly rework. From a speed/quality perspective, knowing your constraints at project proposal phase is better than discovering them in month 3. The compliance officer gate adds some latency but is a small cost compared to the alternative of mid-project reclassification.\n\nI'd lean toward approve with a moderate promise score. It's useful but not something I'd drive — it's enabling infrastructure for my work rather than directly improving prototypin