andpoint, I need to ask: does the lab actually have or plan to have Annex III 1(a) high-risk systems? If yes, this is a necessary compliance investment. If no, it's speculative resource allocation.\n- The scope is appropriately narrow - it's not trying to solve all of Article 12, just the Annex III 1(a) specific requirements in paragraph 3. This is good for resource containment.\n- A \"dashboard\" with digital sign-off could be relatively straightforward to build if we have existing auth/ID infrastructure. The main new work would be the UI and the logging integration.\n\n**Assessment:**\nThe proposal is well-scoped and legally targeted. It addresses a specific, mandatory requirement (not optional or aspirational). The narrow focus on Annex III 1(a) means it won't sprawl. However, from a pure resource-allocation standpoint:\n\n- If we have or are planning Annex III 1(a) systems, this is a necessary and appropriately-sized compliance investment.\n- The \"build\" language suggests new development rather than configuration of existing tools. But the scope is limited enough that it shouldn't be a massive engineering effort.\n- It's not over-engineering - it's doing exactly what the regulation requires for that specific system category.\n\nI think this clears my bar. It's a targeted, legally-mandated compliance module with clear scope boundaries. The resource commitment should be moderate (a dashboard + logging integration), and the legal risk of NOT having it is high if we deploy Annex III 1(a) systems. Even as a precautionary measure for potential future deployments, it's proportionate.\n\nMy concern: \"We will build\" - I want to make sure this leverages existing infrastructure rather than creating parallel systems. But that's an implementation detail, not a reason to reject the proposal itself. The proposal is sound in its scope and legal mapping.\n\nI'll approve with a moderate-to-good promise score. It's solid compliance work but doesn't stretch scientific ambition 