m falls through the cracks; institutional-wide compliance posture.\n- Reduces manual overhead — lowers risk of human error in logging, which is a common compliance failure point.\n\n**Weaknesses/Concerns (compliance perspective):**\n- The proposal mentions Art 12(3) requirements (timestamps, database references, input data, user identification), but doesn't explicitly mention Art 12(2)(a)-(c): identifying situations presenting risk under Art 79(1), facilitating post-market monitoring under Art 72, and monitoring operation under Art 26(5). The \"specifically target\" language around timestamps and input data might suggest the scope is narrower than full Article 12 compliance.\n- However, the broader framing of \"automatically captures required event logs across diverse AI models\" could encompass those as well — it's just not explicit.\n- \"Without degrading system performance\" is a nice operational constraint but from a pure compliance standpoint, performance isn't the primary concern (though it affects adoption and therefore actual compliance).\n- The proposal doesn't explicitly mention retention periods or accessibility for regulators, though those are somewhat implied by \"lifetime of the system.\"\n- \"Immutable ledger\" — this is good for integrity but I'd want to ensure it also allows for authorized access by notified bodies and market surveillance authorities.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a strong, well-scoped compliance infrastructure proposal. It directly maps to the letter of Article 12, addresses both the general requirement (para 1-2) and the specific Annex III point 1(a) requirements (para 3). The modular/unified approach reduces institutional risk. The automated nature removes human-dependency failure modes. From a compliance survival standpoint, having a unified logging infrastructure is arguably one of the highest-value investments we can make — without it, every single high-risk system would need its own ad-hoc logging solution, creating com