ure.\n- But there's a practical issue: we don't need a complete scientific taxonomy to start prototyping a logging system. We can start with the events explicitly listed in Article 12(3) for Annex III point 1(a) systems, and iteratively expand based on our specific intended purpose.\n- The \"scientific study\" framing suggests months or years of analysis before we have actionable output. For prototyping speed, I'd prefer to build a configurable logging framework now and refine the event set as we learn more about failure modes in practice.\n\n**Is it necessary?**\n- Article 12(2) requires events relevant to (a) risk identification, (b) post-market monitoring, (c) operational monitoring. The specific events depend on \"the intended purpose of the system\" — so this is somewhat system-specific and can be determined during design without a grand cross-system taxonomy study.\n- For Annex III 1(a) systems (biometrics), Article 12(3) already gives minimum logging requirements explicitly. No taxonomy needed there.\n- The \"substantial modification\" trigger in 12(2)(a) is somewhat vague, and understanding what constitutes a substantial modification could benefit from analysis. But that's more of a regulatory/legal question than a pure engineering one.\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom my development engineering perspective focused on technical viability and prototyping speed:\n- This is a research project, not an engineering deliverable. It doesn't directly produce a prototype or a buildable component.\n- The over-logging concern is valid but can be addressed through practical engineering approaches (configurable event schemas, tiered logging) without waiting for a \"formal taxonomy.\"\n- The timeline and scope of a \"scientific study\" is unclear and potentially very long, which conflicts with the speed-of-prototyping priority.\n- However, having SOME structured understanding of what events matter IS valuable before we lock in our logging architecture. But this doesn't require 