e as if it needed full Article 12 compliance).\n\n**Cons/Concerns:**\n- The proposal is somewhat vague on execution: \"apply a regulatory rubric\" — who builds the rubric? How often is it re-applied? What's the cost of maintaining this classification system itself? A project that starts as low-risk experimental prototype might evolve into high-risk. There needs to be a mechanism for reclassification.\n- The risk of being too rigid: R&D projects evolve. A \"planned\" project today might shift in scope tomorrow. The rubric needs to account for trajectory, not just current state.\n- However, these are execution details, not fundamental flaws in the proposal's logic.\n\n**From my specific domain:**\n- Resource-allocation efficiency: This is squarely on-point. It's a gating mechanism that ensures compliance spend is targeted. High value per unit of implementation effort (the audit itself is relatively cheap compared to building logging infrastructure for 50 projects when only 5 need it).\n- Balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints: By ring-fencing compliance costs only where required, this proposal protects the R&D budget for actual scientific work. It prevents the \"compliance tax\" from bleeding into experimental budgets unnecessarily.\n- Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure: Knowing which projects are high-risk lets you plan how to integrate logging into existing pipelines (e.g., if you already have monitoring dashboards for production systems, you can extend them rather than building new ones).\n\nThis is a strong proposal from my vantage point. It's a low-cost, high-leverage decision tool that directly serves resource efficiency. The main risk is execution quality (who maintains the rubric, how often it's updated), but conceptually it's sound and necessary before any of the other compliance proposals make sense — you can't allocate resources to build logging systems if you don't know which projects need them.\n\nDecision: approve\nPromise: