emplate engines for document generation.\n- Risk: Over-scoping. \"Converts raw R&D data into compliance reports\" is vague - the actual regulatory documentation has specific structural requirements that may not map cleanly to automated extraction from code metadata.\n- However, even a partial automation (extracting training logs, model architecture specs, validation metrics) would be immediately useful and prototypeable in weeks rather than months.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- The proposal somewhat conflates \"technical documentation\" (Recital 71, Annex IV) with \"logging capabilities\" (Article 12(1)-(3)). Article 12 is specifically about automatic recording of events (logs), while technical documentation is a broader requirement. The proposal addresses both but the mapping to the specific legal requirements needs care.\n- For Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometrics, etc.), the specific logging requirements in Article 12(3) are quite granular and may not be fully captured by \"parsing model cards and configuration files.\"\n- The \"real-time\" claim might be over-promising for a CI/CD extension - more likely it's batch or near-real-time per pipeline run.\n\n**Overall Assessment:**\nThis is a technically sound, well-scoped engineering project that directly addresses the spirit of Article 12 (automatic recording) and Recital 71 (technical documentation). It leverages existing infrastructure patterns (CI/CD, version control), has clear technical approaches, and can be prototyped quickly. The main risk is scope creep in trying to automate everything versus a phased approach that handles the programmatically-extractable elements first.\n\nFrom my vantage point: this is highly viable technically, quick to prototype a useful MVP, and directly serves the regulatory requirement. I'd approve it with good confidence. The promise score should reflect high technical viability but acknowledge some scope risks - maybe 78-82 range. It's not a 90+ because \"automatically generates required technical documentation\" overstates what code metadata parsing can achieve for the full regulatory text, and the specific Article 12(3) requirements for Annex III 1(a) systems need more targeted engineering than a generic CI/CD extension provides. But it's clearly above average - solidly approvable.\n\nI'll go with approve at 80.\n",
