d documents might miss context, nuance, or risk-management narrative that Regulators expect.\n- It's primarily an R&D efficiency tool dressed as a compliance solution. The actual compliance requirement is about what information MUST be captured and available - not how it's formatted.\n- The proposal doesn't explicitly address Article 12(3) minimum logging requirements (start/end times, reference database, input data for matches, identification of natural persons in verification). It seems focused on the technical documentation side rather than the event logging side.\n- \"Custom script\" introduces a single point of failure - if the pipeline breaks, compliance documentation generation stops, creating an immediate regulatory risk.\n- The proposal doesn't mention version control of the documents themselves (who approved what, when), which is critical for demonstrating compliance to regulators.\n- It addresses documentation but not the actual \"automatic recording of events\" requirement in Article 12(1) - the logging capability itself must be built into the system architecture, not just documented afterwards.\n\n**From my vantage point (institutional survival, regulatory compliance, market reputation):**\nThis is a genuinely useful tool for maintaining compliance posture over time. The \"documentation debt\" problem is real and a known failure mode in AI governance. Having automated generation of technical documentation from actual system metadata means the documentation reflects reality rather than being aspirational or stale. This directly supports our ability to demonstrate compliance to regulators (Article 12, Recital 71).\n\nHowever, it's not sufficient on its own - it doesn't cover the full scope of Article 12 (event logging architecture, minimum requirements for Annex III systems). But the question is whether THIS proposal clears my bar as a standalone commitment. Does committing resources to this reduce our compliance risk? Yes, significantly. Does it create n