        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P1507\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The proposal targets a genuine and under-explored tension at the intersection of privacy-preserving computation and regulatory auditability: Article 12(3) demands exact, individual-event records (timestamps per use, specific matched input data, named natural persons) for Annex III 1(a) biometric systems, yet GDPR's data-minimization principle pushes in the opposite direction. Standard differential-privacy machinery is designed for aggregate-query privacy, not forensic reconstruction of single events, so applying DP and MPC to this specific logging pipeline raises real open questions about where in the storage/retrieval stack one can introduce cryptographic obfuscation without destroying the per-event precision Article 12(3)(a)-(d) requires. The knowledge-frontier contribution is substantive (characterizing the feasible privacy–forensic-precision trade-off curve for biometric audit logs), and the benchmarking against standard logging overheads grounds the work in practical relevance; broader impact extends well beyond AI regulation into medical, financial, and law-enforcement audit-trail design.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 83\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "The user asks me to screen proposal P1507 from the vantage point of a Researcher at an R&D institution whose domain of concern is \"the knowledge frontier, intellectual merit of research, and broader scientific impact.\"\n\nLet me analyze this proposal:\n\n**Proposal P1507: Privacy-Preserving Forensic Logging Frameworks**\n\nThe proposal investigates how to satisfy exhaustive logging requirements (as mandated by Article 12) without compromising user privacy or violating data minimization principles. It targets the tension between transparency and confidentiality. The team will develop novel differential privacy techniques and secure multi-party computation methods for encrypted log storage and retrieval, benchmarking against standard logging overheads.\n\nLet me assess this from my professional vantage point (knowledge frontier, intellectual merit, broader scientific impact):\n\n**Knowledge Frontier:**\n- The tension between exhaustive logging requirements (Article 12) and data minimization principles (GDPR Article 5(1)(c)) is a genuine and significant open problem. The EU AI Act essentially mandates \"log everything\" while GDPR says \"collect as little as possible.\" This is not a trivial conflict to resolve.\n- Differential privacy for logs is an active research area, but applying it specifically to forensic/audit logging (where you need to reconstruct specific events, not just aggregate statistics) is genuinely challenging. Standard DP is designed for statistical queries; forensic logs require individual event reconstruction. There's a real tension there that isn't fully solved.\n- Secure multi-party computation for log storage and retrieval is interesting but has well-known scalability issues. Applying MPC to high-throughput logging pipelines (where Article 12(1) requires \"automatic recording of events over the lifetime\") introduces significant engineering challenges alongside theoretical ones.\n- The specific application to biometric verification systems (Annex III point 1(a), which includes things like facial recognition, fingerprint matching) makes this concrete and relevant.\n\n**Intellectual Merit:**\n- The proposal identifies a real conflict: Article 12(3)(c) requires logging \"the input data for which the search has led to a match\" — this could be biometric data or PII. Logging that data in plaintext contradicts data minimization. This is a genuine and important research problem.\n- The combination of DP + MPC + encrypted storage/retrieval is ambitious. The challenge: DP adds noise, but forensic logs need precision (you can't say \"approximately this person was verified at approximately this time\" for compliance). There's a fundamental tension between the statistical nature of DP and the exact-record requirement of Article 12(3)(a)-(d).\n- Actually, wait — let me think more carefully. Differential privacy is about protecting individual contributions in aggregate queries. But Article 12 logging is about recording individual events (start/end time of each use, specific input data that matched, specific natural persons involved). These are exact records, not statistical outputs. Applying DP to the *storage* layer (e.g., k-anonymity on stored logs, or encrypted with access control) is different from applying it to the *content* of individual log entries. The proposal says \"differential privacy techniques... for encrypted log storage and retrieval\" — this is a bit ambiguous. If they mean DP as an obfuscation layer on top of logs before encryption, that could work for aggregate queries but conflicts with the need for exact forensic reconstruction.\n- This ambiguity is actually part of why it's interesting research: figuring out where in the pipeline you can apply privacy-preserving techniques without destroying the forensic utility that Article 12(3) demands.\n\n**Broader Scientific Impact:**\n- If solved, this would be applicable far beyond AI regulation — to any domain requiring audit trails with PII (medical records, financial transactions, law enforcement).\n- The benchmarking against \"standard logging overheads\" is good methodology — it grounds the research in practical feasibility.\n- Broader impact: could inform how regulators design future transparency mandates; provides technical evidence about what's feasible and what isn't.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- The scope is somewhat broad: DP + MPC + encrypted storage + retrieval + benchmarking. This is a lot for one project. But as a \"research project\" (not a product), it's reasonable to have multiple workstreams.\n- There's a risk of overpromising: \"solving a fundamental conflict\" — the conflict may not be fully solvable; you can only trade off between privacy and forensic precision. The research might produce useful partial solutions rather than a clean solution. But that's still valuable knowledge.\n- The connection to Article 12(3) specifically (biometric verification systems in Annex III 1(a)) makes this very concrete and grounded, not just abstract.\n\n**Overall assessment from my vantage point:**\nThis is a genuinely interesting research problem at the intersection of privacy-preserving computation, information security, and regulatory compliance. The specific tension between Article 12's exhaustive logging mandate (especially for biometric systems) and data minimization is real, unsolved, and has broad implications. The proposed methods (DP, MPC) are appropriate tools, though the application to individual-event forensic logs (vs. aggregate queries) introduces genuine open questions. This pushes the knowledge frontier in privacy-preserving computation applied to a concrete regulatory context.\n\nI'd rate this as quite promising from a research merit standpoint. It's not a trivial extension of existing work — the specific constraints of Article 12(3) (exact timestamps, specific input data, identification of natural persons) create real technical challenges for standard privacy tools. The broader impact is significant.\n\nHowever, I should note: as a researcher evaluating intellectual merit, I want to see that this isn't just \"apply DP to logs\" which would be incremental. The proposal frames it as solving the tension between transparency and confidentiality, which suggests they understand it's not a straightforward application. The benchmarking component adds rigor.\n\nI'll approve with a high promise score — maybe 82-85. It's genuinely at the frontier but has some scope concerns and the fundamental solvability of the conflict is uncertain (you may only get trade-offs, not clean solutions). But from a knowledge-frontier perspective, even characterizing the exact trade-off boundary is valuable research.\n\nLet me settle on 83.\n",
