igh-risk AI systems do produce complex model outputs where \"events\" aren't straightforward (e.g., the system flags something as risky based on a combination of internal signals), then having some form of event summarization could be useful. But even then, Article 12's language is about recording events, not interpreting them. The interpretability angle is more aligned with Article 13 (transparency and provision of information to deployers) than Article 12.\n\nMy assessment: This proposal misidentifies the engineering problem. It proposes a research initiative for an open-ended scientific question when Article 12 requires concrete, specifiable logging infrastructure. From a prototyping speed perspective, this would tie up a small team in uncertain research while the actual compliance work (straightforward log capture) remains unaddressed. I would reject this.\n\nPromise score: From my vantage point, maybe a 15-20. There's some peripheral value in interpretability for broader AI governance, but it doesn't map to what Article 12 technically requires, and the research framing is antithetical to fast prototyping. I'll say 18.\n",
