ture what Article 12 requires while another's does. Inconsistent compliance is a major regulatory risk.\n\n5. The \"minimizes human error\" aspect directly supports the \"automatic recording\" requirement in Art. 12(1). If logs depend on manual entry, they're not truly automatic and may be incomplete or altered.\n\n6. From a market reputation standpoint: if we can demonstrate to regulators (and potentially to business partners) that we have a systematic, automated compliance infrastructure, this is a strong signal of organizational maturity. It shows we take the AI Act seriously at an institutional level rather than per-project.\n\n7. Recital 71 emphasizes \"automatic recording of events, by means of logs\" — this proposal directly implements that.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- Implementation risk: building middleware that hooks into all existing pipelines could be technically challenging and time-consuming. But from a compliance perspective, the regulatory deadline doesn't care about our internal technical challenges.\n- The proposal mentions \"timestamps and reference database queries\" specifically, which aligns with Art. 12(3)(a) and (b), but I'd want to ensure it also covers (c) input data for matches and (d) identification of natural persons in verification. However, the text says \"mandatory data points\" broadly, so presumably all of these would be included.\n- Resource commitment: this is a significant infrastructure project. But the alternative — not having automatic logging — means non-compliance with Article 12(1), which carries enforcement consequences (up to €35M or 7% global turnover for prohibited practices, though Art. 12 violations would fall under other penalty tiers).\n\n**Compliance risk if NOT implemented:**\n- Without systematic automated logging, we rely on individual R&D teams to implement their own logging solutions. This creates:\n  - Risk of incomplete logs (missing required data points)\n  - Inconsistency across systems\n  - Human error in log capture\n  - Difficulty demonstrating compliance to authorities during audits\n  - Potential for a system to go to production without proper logging capability\n\n**Verdict:** This is squarely within my domain. It directly implements Article 12's core requirement (automatic recording), creates institutional controls that prevent non-compliant systems from reaching market, and reduces regulatory exposure across the portfolio. The \"required dependency\" mechanism is particularly important for compliance governance — it creates a gate that ensures no high-risk system reaches production without the logging infrastructure in place.\n\nThis is a strong approval from my vantage point. I'd rate this quite highly — perhaps 82-85. It's not a 100 because:\n- The proposal as stated focuses on \"timestamps and reference database queries\" which covers parts of Art. 12(3) but I want explicit confirmation it covers all four sub-points (a)-(d), including the identification of natural persons in verification (Art. 14(5)) and input data for matches\n- The technical implementation details are thin — \"middleware layer that hooks into existing pipelines\" is vague; different AI architectures may require different hooking approaches\n- It doesn't explicitly mention retention periods or how logs are protected from tampering, which would be important for audit readiness\n\nBut the core concept is exactly what Article 12 demands and what a compliance function needs. I'll approve with a promise score of around 83.\n",
