is not a massive engineering project, it's a modest software component that leverages existing infrastructure (MLflow).\n- Risk: over-engineering the prototype phase could slow down research iteration cycles. If every experiment now requires logging middleware to be configured, researchers might work around it or resist adoption.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- This explicitly integrates with MLflow orchestration — good, leverages what's already there rather than building parallel systems.\n- \"Reusable\" is key — one build, many uses across teams.\n- The Python decorator pattern is lightweight and non-invasive to the research code itself.\n\n**Critical assessment:**\n- The proposal is well-scoped: it targets high-risk AI experiments specifically (not all research), integrates with existing MLflow (no new infrastructure), and uses a lightweight pattern (decorator).\n- It addresses a real compliance need — Article 12(1) requires technical capability for automatic logging. Having this in prototypes means you're not starting from scratch when a prototype becomes a product candidate.\n- The \"by default\" aspect is important — it removes the burden on individual researchers, which is good for adoption and consistency.\n- Concern: \"all high-risk AI experiments during the prototyping phase\" could be interpreted very broadly. If we have 50 parallel prototypes, do they all get this? That's where resource efficiency gets tested. But the middleware approach means marginal cost per additional prototype is near zero after initial build.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThis is a well-designed proposal from an infrastructure/resource perspective. It's:\n- Low upfront cost (Python decorator integrating with existing MLflow)\n- High reuse value (applies to all future high-risk prototypes)\n- Addresses real regulatory need without over-building\n- Leverages existing infrastructure rather than requiring new tooling\n- Removes per-researcher compliance burden at scale