y maps to Article 12(3)(d) and partially to 12(3)(c). It's specifically for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification/verification/categorization — which are the \"search match\" type systems).\n- The digital sign-off mechanism with IDs and timestamps is a concrete, implementable compliance feature.\n- However, I notice it only addresses part of Article 12(3):\n  - It doesn't explicitly cover 12(3)(a) — recording start/end date and time of EACH USE of the system (not just verification actions). The proposal focuses on verification timestamps, not general usage periods.\n  - It doesn't address 12(3)(b) — the reference database against which input data has been checked.\n- So it's a partial solution to Article 12(3), specifically addressing the human identification requirement (d) and partially (c).\n\n**From my compliance vantage point:**\nPros:\n- Directly maps to a specific mandatory requirement (Article 12(3)(d)) — this is legally required, not optional.\n- The digital sign-off with IDs creates an auditable trail that regulators can verify.\n- Focused scope means it's implementable without enormous resource expenditure.\n- It supports post-market monitoring (Article 72) by creating a record of who verified what and when.\n- Market reputation: demonstrating robust human oversight documentation is a strong signal to clients and regulators.\n\nCons/Limitations:\n- It only covers part of Article 12(3) — doesn't address reference database logging (b) or full usage period recording (a). But this isn't necessarily a reason to reject it; other proposals might cover those gaps, or the system's general logging might handle them.\n- The scope is narrow: specifically Annex III point 1(a) systems. This limits applicability but also makes it focused and feasible.\n- It doesn't address Article 12(2)(a) — identifying situations that may present a risk under Article 79(1). Though verification failures could be flagged as such.\n\n**Decision:** From my compliance perspective, this is a well-targeted module that addresses a specific mandatory requirement (Article 12(3)(d)) with concrete implementation details. It's not comprehensive for all of Article 12, but it's a necessary component. The fact that it's scoped to Annex III point 1(a) systems means it won't create unnecessary scope creep. A compliance officer would want this in place because without it, the lab cannot demonstrate compliance with the human identification requirement for those specific high-risk systems. It's not optional — failure to implement it is a direct regulatory violation.\n\nI'll approve it with a reasonably high promise score. It directly addresses mandatory requirements, is well-scoped, and creates audit trails that protect institutional survival (regulatory penalties) and market reputation (client trust in compliance). However, I note it's only one piece of the Article 12(3) puzzle, so I won't give it a 95+ — maybe around 78-82. It's clearly needed but not sufficient on its own for full compliance with Article 12(3).\n\nLet me settle on: approve, promise 80.\n",
