active compliance posture, which can be valuable if we're ever examined by authorities.\n\nCons:\n- This is NOT implementing Article 12 compliance itself. It's an R&D/advocacy project about schemas, not about actually building logging capabilities into our systems. The lab's immediate regulatory obligation is to ensure our high-risk systems CAN log events — that's a hard requirement with enforcement implications.\n- \"Invite other research institutions to validate them through shared benchmarks\" — this is open-ended, collaborative, and has no clear completion criteria or timeline. It could consume resources indefinitely without delivering concrete compliance outcomes for OUR systems.\n- The stated motivation is \"establishing the lab as a global leader in AI safety governance, enhancing our broader scientific impact\" — that's a reputation/marketing goal, not a compliance goal. From my vantage point, I care about whether we're actually compliant and whether we can demonstrate it to regulators.\n- There's no mention of how this connects to OUR specific systems' logging requirements or to our internal compliance program. It's an external-facing initiative.\n- \"Open-source\" standards — while noble, the EU AI Act doesn't require us to publish our logging schemas publicly. In fact, some technical documentation may be sensitive. Making them open could create competitive or security concerns, though for metadata schemas this is less of a risk.\n- The proposal doesn't specify which of our systems are in scope, what regulatory deadlines apply, or how the output feeds into our compliance documentation (technical documentation under Article 11, etc.).\n\n**Institutional survival angle:** If we're ever scrutinized by a market surveillance authority, having contributed to logging standards is a nice-to-have but not a substitute for actually having compliant logging in place. The proposal doesn't directly address whether OUR systems meet Article 12 requirements.\n\n**Regulator