sh an open-source specification\" — but maintaining and updating a standard over the lifetime of systems (as Article 12 requires documentation to be kept up to date) means ongoing commitment that may outlast any single funding cycle.\n- There's no clear mechanism described for how this translates into reduced implementation cost for our own systems. We could end up spending years defining schemas while our own high-risk systems are still non-compliant.\n- The \"institutional influence\" and \"scientific interoperability\" benefits are real but diffuse — they benefit the ecosystem broadly, not specifically our lab's compliance timeline or resource constraints.\n- Article 12 is about technical capability of the system to log events. A schema standard is one layer above that — it helps with interoperability but doesn't directly solve the engineering challenge of building logging into AI systems. Our core need is implementing compliant logging in OUR systems, not defining what logs look like across all EU labs.\n- The risk: we commit scarce resources to a multi-year coordination project while our own compliance deadline approaches. If the working group doesn't produce a standard quickly enough (or at all), we've lost senior engineering time with no tangible output for our own systems.\n\n**My assessment:** From a pure resource-allocation efficiency standpoint, this is a high-cost, diffuse-benefit activity. The lab's primary obligation under Article 12 is to ensure OUR systems have compliant logging capabilities. Leading an EU-wide standards body is a noble goal but it's a secondary benefit at best for our own compliance. The coordination overhead of multi-institutional work is well-known to be enormous relative to output. With limited R&D funding and infrastructure, committing senior staff to lead this effort risks diverting them from the actual implementation work that will determine whether we're compliant when enforcement begins.\n\nHowever, I should note: if our lab