        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2942\",\n      \"rationale\": \"From a resource-allocation standpoint, this proposal wraps a structured-logging requirement (start/end timestamps, database reference, matched input, verifier ID) in an extraordinarily complex cryptographic stack—ZKPs, secure enclaves, differential privacy—that would consume specialised engineering talent, potentially new hardware, and multi-year development cycles to solve what is fundamentally an access-controlled audit-log problem. Worse, the ZKP component is in direct tension with Recital 71's demand for 'comprehensible' and 'clear and comprehensive' documentation: a regulator conducting post-market monitoring under Article 72 or investigating a risk event under Article 79(1) needs to *read* the actual logs, not receive an opaque attestation that a match occurred; differential-privacy noise on those same records could further degrade their evidentiary reliability. The legitimate privacy concern behind storing input data and verifier identities (Art. 12(3)(c)-(d)) is real but can be addressed through standard encryption at rest, role-based access controls, and GDPR-mandated retention schedules using infrastructure we already operate—none of which requires a bespoke cryptographic vault or ZKP pipeline.\",\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"promise\": 18\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "The user wants me to evaluate proposal P2942 as a Lab Director at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain of concern is: resource-allocation efficiency, the balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints, and effective use of existing R&D infrastructure.\n\nLet me analyze this proposal from my vantage point:\n\n**Proposal P2942: Privacy-Preserving Traceability Protocol**\n\nThe proposal suggests using differential privacy or secure enclaves to store input data required by Article 12(3), implementing a cryptographic vault for logs, and integrating Zero-Knowledge Proofs (ZKPs) for verification of result matching.\n\nFrom my resource-allocation efficiency perspective:\n\n1. **Resource intensity**: This proposal is extraordinarily resource-heavy. It involves:\n   - Differential privacy implementation\n   - Secure enclaves infrastructure\n   - Cryptographic vault system\n   - Zero-Knowledge Proof integration\n   - Audited, purpose-limited query mechanisms\n   \n   This is essentially proposing to build a new cryptographic infrastructure layer on top of our existing logging systems. ZKPs alone are an extremely research-intensive and computationally expensive technology. Differential privacy adds significant engineering complexity. Secure enclaves require specific hardware (like Intel SGX or ARM TrustZone) which may not be in our current R&D infrastructure.\n\n2. **Scope creep / over-engineering**: Article 12(3) requires recording of:\n   - Period of use (start/end dates)\n   - Reference database used for checks\n   - Input data that led to a match\n   - Identification of natural persons in verification\n   \n   The proposal takes this relatively straightforward logging requirement and wraps it in an extremely complex cryptographic architecture. A \"cryptographic vault\" with ZKPs for verification is massively over-engineered for what is essentially an audit log. The regulation requires the records to be accessible for compliance verification — ZKPs that prove a match occurred without revealing the data actually work *against* the spirit of traceability, because the regulator or authorized body needs to be able to inspect the actual logs, not just verify their existence cryptographically.\n\n3. **Contradiction with regulatory purpose**: Recital 71 emphasizes \"comprehensible information\" and documentation in a \"clear and comprehensive form.\" The entire point is that regulators can trace what happened, when, against which database, with what data. ZKPs fundamentally obscure the underlying data — you can prove something was true without revealing what it was. This creates tension with Article 12's traceability purpose and Article 72's post-market monitoring. If a regulator needs to investigate an incident, they need to see the actual logs, not just get a cryptographic attestation that \"a match occurred.\"\n\n4. **Existing infrastructure**: We likely already have logging infrastructure. The question is whether it meets the specific requirements of Article 12(3). Wrapping existing logs in ZKPs and differential privacy layers is an enormous engineering undertaking with uncertain payoff for our resource base.\n\n5. **Funding constraints**: This proposal would require specialized cryptographic engineers (ZKP expertise is rare), potentially new hardware for secure enclaves, significant development time, and ongoing maintenance of a complex system. For a lab that needs to balance scientific ambition with funding constraints, this is a very expensive way to solve what is essentially a structured logging problem.\n\n6. **Risk**: The complexity introduces its own risks — if the cryptographic layer has vulnerabilities or becomes deprecated, we're in worse shape than simple encrypted storage. Also, differential privacy on logs could make them unreliable for actual compliance purposes (the noise added might obscure actual events).\n\nHowever, there IS a legitimate concern about data protection when storing input data and identifying natural persons. But the solution proposed is dramatically disproportionate to the problem. Standard encryption at rest, access controls, data minimization practices, and GDPR-compliant storage would address privacy concerns without needing ZKPs and differential privacy on audit logs.\n\nMy assessment: This proposal, while showing ambition and awareness of privacy concerns, is massively over-engineered for the regulatory requirement from a resource-efficiency standpoint. It introduces enormous complexity, cost, and technical risk to solve what is fundamentally a structured logging problem with standard access controls. The ZKP component actually undermines traceability. As Lab Director concerned with efficient use of scarce resources and existing infrastructure, I would reject this as a disproportionate allocation of engineering effort.\n\nHowever, I should note there's a kernel of legitimate thinking here — privacy in logs IS important when Article 12(3)(c) requires storing input data and (d) identifies natural persons. But the solution is wildly disproportionate.\n\nDecision: Reject\nPromise score: From my resource-allocation perspective, this is very expensive for modest regulatory gain, with actual contradictions to the traceability purpose. I'd put it around 15-20. There's some legitimate thinking but the execution path is resource-prohibitive and somewhat counterproductive to the regulation's stated goals.\n\nI'll go with 18 — there's a real privacy concern being identified that deserves attention, but this particular solution path is a terrible use of our limited engineering resources and introduces risks that work against compliance.\n",
