r, as a tool for compliance staff rather than a deployed high-risk AI system, this should be manageable.\n- The proposal focuses on \"substantial modifications or emerging risks\" but doesn't explicitly address the Article 12(3) specific logging requirements for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification). However, that's a narrower subset and this is a general platform.\n- \"Transforms raw logs into actionable compliance intelligence\" — this language is somewhat marketing-flavored rather than regulatory-precise. But the core concept is sound.\n- The proposal doesn't mention who has access to the dashboards, data retention periods for the analytics layer, or how it integrates with the technical documentation requirements of Article 18/Annex IV.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nFrom a compliance officer's perspective, this is a genuinely valuable and directly relevant proposal. Article 12 requires not just that logs exist but that they enable traceability, post-market monitoring, and risk identification. An automated system that actively scans those logs for anomalies is the operational backbone of making logging \"meaningful\" rather than performative. Without active monitoring, you're storing data but potentially missing the very risks the Act wants you to catch.\n\nThis directly supports:\n- Article 12(1) - automatic recording (the platform consumes these)\n- Article 12(2)(a) - identifying risk situations and substantial modifications\n- Article 12(2)(b) - facilitating post-market monitoring under Article 72\n- Article 12(2)(c) - monitoring operation per Article 26(5)\n- Recital 71's emphasis on traceability, compliance verification, and monitoring throughout lifetime\n\nThe promise score: This is highly relevant to my domain. It's not a perfect proposal (could be more specific on implementation, data governance of the analytics layer itself, etc.) but the core concept is exactly what a compliance function needs operationally. I'd rate it around 82-85. It's clearly worth pursuing as an implementation commitment, though I'd want to refine scope and ensure it doesn't create its own compliance obligations.\n\nDecision: Approve. The proposal directly operationalizes Article 12's monitoring requirements and supports the institution's ability to demonstrate ongoing compliance throughout the AI system lifetime.\n",
