le point of failure and potential latency concerns for real-time AI systems. But it does simplify audit trails. A hybrid approach (local + centralized) might be more robust, but centralization is workable.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- The big concern from my vantage: this is a \"build one framework to rule them all\" approach. For an R&D lab developing AI systems, prototyping speed is critical. Requiring every research team to integrate with a centralized logging framework before they can even prototype could be a significant bottleneck.\n- However, the proposal says it's a \"modular API wrapper\" — if well-designed, the integration cost should be minimal (a few lines of code to initialize and call log methods). This is analogous to how structured logging libraries like structlog or zap work in production systems.\n- The risk: building this framework itself takes time. During development, what do research teams do? Do they build ad-hoc logging that gets thrown away later? This could slow prototyping in the short term while creating technical debt migration work later.\n- But once built, it should actually ACCELERATE prototyping for new high-risk systems because the compliance plumbing is already there. Engineers don't have to reinvent logging for each new system.\n\n**Quality considerations:**\n- A single, well-tested library means consistent log format, easier debugging across systems, and a single place to fix bugs or add fields when regulations update.\n- \"Immutable centralized ledger\" — if this means something like an append-only database with WAL (write-ahead logging) and cryptographic chain hashes, it's technically achievable and performant enough for most AI system event rates.\n- The framework needs to handle different types of events from Article 12(2)(a), (b), (c) beyond just the Annex III 1(a) minimums in paragraph 3. The proposal mentions \"all high-risk AI systems\" so it should be scoped broadly enough, but the specific fields mentioned (t