nts over-allocation to low-complexity projects and under-allocation to high-complexity ones.\n- It balances scientific ambition against operational reality — you can't just launch a project and ignore the fact that logs must be maintained for the system's lifetime.\n- It uses existing infrastructure (grant application templates, budget committee) rather than requiring new organizational structures.\n\nWeaknesses/Concerns:\n- \"Lifetime record-keeping\" is somewhat vague in terms of what \"lifetime\" means operationally. The Act says \"over the duration of the lifetime of the system\" but doesn't define a hard cap. This could lead to unbounded storage commitments that are hard to budget for.\n- The proposal assumes we can accurately estimate these costs at project vetting time, which may be difficult early in development when the system architecture isn't finalized.\n- It's primarily a process/template change rather than an engineering or infrastructure investment — so it's low-cost to implement but also its impact depends on whether people actually fill in the regulatory overhead section meaningfully.\n- The risk-classification-based allocation is reasonable but could be overly rigid if requirements shift during development.\n\nOverall assessment: This proposal is squarely within my domain of concern. It's a process improvement that makes resource allocation more explicit and accountable regarding compliance costs. It's low-cost to implement (template updates, committee protocol changes), uses existing structures, and directly addresses the risk of underfunding long-term operational obligations. The main weakness is the uncertainty around \"lifetime\" duration, but this is an inherent regulatory ambiguity rather than a flaw in the proposal itself.\n\nI think this clears my bar. It's a sensible, proportionate governance improvement that doesn't require large new investments but does create better visibility into resource commitments. From my vantage point, this is ex