esearchers will develop new methods.\" That's open-ended R&D with no clear endpoint tied to compliance deadlines.\n- The lab has finite implementation resources. Approving this commits those resources to an exploratory research effort whose connection to the actual Article 12 requirement (automatic event logging) is indirect at best.\n- The regulation requires that systems \"technically allow for automatic recording of events.\" This is an engineering task with defined scope, not a fundamental research problem. The proposal conflates the regulatory compliance need (logging infrastructure) with a broader scientific ambition (model interpretability/provenance tools).\n- The justification \"protects our market reputation as a leader in trustworthy AI\" is a nice-to-have positioning goal, not a resource-allocation priority driven by the regulation itself.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- It does say they'll \"test these tools on our existing high-risk prototypes,\" which suggests leveraging existing assets rather than building new testbeds. That's a point in its favor.\n- However, developing entirely new auditing methods is a substantial research investment that may not yield results within the timeframe needed for compliance.\n\n**Key concern:** Article 12 is about logging - automatic recording of events over the system's lifetime. It specifies what events to log and minimum fields. This is an engineering/implementation task with clear scope. The proposal proposes a fundamental R&D research project on model provenance and internal state auditing, which is a much larger and more open-ended endeavor than what the regulation demands. The compliance need can be met by building logging infrastructure that captures the required events - you don't need new methods for \"auditing internal model states\" to comply with Article 12's requirement to log start/end times, reference databases used, input data matched, and verification personnel.\n\nThat said, there's a legitimate argument that understanding how models process inputs could help ensure that logs are meaningful (e.g., knowing which parts of the model were activated during inference could be relevant context for post-market monitoring). But this is speculative value-add beyond the regulatory minimum.\n\nFrom my vantage point as Lab Director concerned with resource-allocation efficiency:\n- The proposal commits scarce R&D resources to an open-ended research project\n- The direct compliance need (Article 12 logging) can likely be met by more targeted engineering work without needing \"new methods for auditing internal model states\"\n- The scope is ambitious and the connection to the specific regulatory requirement is indirect\n- There's no clear timeline, success criteria, or defined endpoint\n- The market reputation argument is nice but doesn't justify diverting resources from compliance-critical implementation\n\nI think this should be rejected. It's a well-intentioned research ambition that goes beyond what the regulation requires and would consume significant R&D capacity without a direct compliance deliverable. The actual Article 12 requirement (automatic event logging with specific fields) is an engineering task, not a fundamental research problem.\n\nPromise score: I'd give it maybe a 30-40. It's not worthless - the idea of linking model states to datasets has some value and could inform better logging design. But from my resource-allocation perspective, this isn't where scarce R&D resources should go when there are more direct compliance engineering needs. I'll say 35.\n",
