e I have significant concerns. A \"mandatory internal API\" that must be integrated into all R&D pipelines is a classic example of compliance overhead that slows down prototyping. If every prototype, experiment, or new model variant needs to integrate with this framework before it can run in production, it adds friction to the development cycle.\n- The proposal frames this as eliminating \"human error\" — but for prototyping, what matters is iteration speed. A mandatory integration layer means that even throwaway experiments need to comply with logging requirements, which slows things down.\n- However, if done well (e.g., a thin wrapper, decorator pattern, or sidecar approach), it could be minimally invasive and actually save time in the long run by not having to bolt on logging after the fact.\n\n**Quality considerations:**\n- A standardized approach does ensure quality of logs — consistent format, guaranteed fields, no gaps. This is good for downstream compliance verification.\n- But \"standardized\" can also mean rigid. If the framework doesn't allow for flexible extension points for domain-specific logging (like the biometric-specific requirements in Art 12(3)), it becomes a constraint rather than an enabler.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThe core idea is sound and technically viable — building a shared logging/instrumentation layer is standard practice. The main risk from my vantage point is that making it \"mandatory\" across ALL R&D pipelines (not just production) could slow prototyping. The proposal conflates \"all R&D pipelines\" with compliance requirements, which apply to high-risk systems in operation. A prototype or research experiment doesn't need the same logging infrastructure as a deployed system.\n\nAlso, the scope is quite broad — \"all R&D pipelines,\" \"all production environments\" — without specifying how it handles heterogeneous architectures. The technical viability depends heavily on implementation details not present here. But the fundamental app