        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2119\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The core idea of a shared, modular logging library is technically sound in principle, but the proposal as scoped—mandating integration into *all* high-risk projects and *every active model* from inception—would impose compliance plumbing on experimental prototypes that are still iterating rapidly on architecture and data pipelines, directly taxing prototyping speed. The event types enumerated (input matches, reference-DB queries, session durations) appear tailored to a single Annex III 1(a) verification use-case and do not generalise cleanly to the broader Article 12(2)(a)-(c) logging obligations across diverse high-risk system architectures; building this as a one-size-fits-all in-house library also diverts scarce engineering cycles away from actual model development.\",\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"promise\": 40\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "The user wants me to evaluate a single proposal (P2119) from the perspective of a Development Engineer at an R&D institution focused on technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping. Let me assess this proposal carefully.\n\nThe proposal is: \"Automated Compliance Logging Framework\" - A standardized software library integrated into all high-risk projects to automate recording of system events and timestamps. It's described as a modular API that captures input matches, reference database queries, and session durations for every active model. The goal is uniform regulatory compliance across diverse R&D projects and eliminating manual errors.\n\nLet me think about this from my vantage point: technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\n**Technical Viability:**\n- A modular API/library approach is technically sound in principle. Standardized logging libraries are a well-established software engineering pattern (think structured logging frameworks like SLF4J, Serilog, Python's logging module).\n- However, the proposal says \"integrated into all high-risk projects\" and \"every active model.\" This is where it gets problematic from a prototyping speed perspective. High-risk AI systems are diverse - they could be different architectures (CNNs, transformers, ensemble models, retrieval-augmented systems, etc.). A single standardized library that captures \"input matches, reference database queries, and session durations\" assumes a fairly uniform system architecture.\n- Article 12(3) specifically applies to Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification), which have specific logging requirements: start/end timestamps, reference database, input data for matches, and identification of natural persons in verification. The proposal mentions \"input matches, reference database queries, and session durations\" - this partially covers the Annex III 1(a) requirements but misses the identification of natural persons involved in verification (Article 14(5)).\n- For other high-risk systems under Article 12(2), the logging needs to be \"appropriate to the intended purpose\" - meaning it's not one-size-fits-all. A rigid standardized library might not capture the right events for all system types.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- This is where I have significant concerns. As a development engineer focused on prototyping speed and quality:\n  - Requiring every high-risk project to integrate a specific compliance logging library from day one adds friction to the prototyping process. During early prototyping, you're iterating rapidly on model architecture, data pipelines, etc. Forcing integration of a compliance framework at the prototype stage can slow things down significantly.\n  - \"Every active model\" is very broad. In an R&D lab, models are constantly being created, tested, and discarded. Requiring compliance logging infrastructure for every experimental model is overkill and resource-intensive.\n  - The proposal commits to developing a custom modular API in-house. This is a significant engineering investment that competes with actual R&D work. Building and maintaining this library across \"diverse R&D projects\" is ongoing maintenance overhead.\n  - However, there IS value in having a shared logging infrastructure. If you're building high-risk AI systems, you WILL need logging eventually. Having a shared library means each project doesn't reinvent the wheel.\n  - The risk: if the library is too rigid (one API for all), it will be awkward to use for projects that don't fit the template, leading to workarounds or poor-quality implementations. If it's modular enough to handle diversity, it becomes complex and hard to maintain.\n\n**Assessment:**\nFrom my vantage point, this proposal has a reasonable core idea (shared logging infrastructure) but is framed in a way that could hurt prototyping velocity:\n1. \"Integrated into all high-risk projects\" - too broad, should be scoped to systems actually classified as high-risk and close to deployment\n2. \"Every active model\" - R&D labs have many experimental models; requiring compliance logging for all of them is wasteful\n3. The specific fields mentioned (input matches, reference DB queries, session durations) are somewhat narrow and assume a particular system type (looks like it's tailored to biometric/verification systems under Annex III 1(a))\n4. Building this as an in-house project competes with R&D resources\n5. However, the modular API approach is technically reasonable if done right - you could have a base logging framework with pluggable event types\n\nThe technical viability is moderate - it's doable but the one-size-fits-all framing creates risks. The speed/quality of prototyping impact is negative as stated (integrated into ALL projects from the start). But the core concept of a shared, reusable logging component has merit if properly scoped.\n\nI think this is a borderline case. The idea isn't bad, but the scope and timing are problematic for a prototyping-focused R&D lab. I'd lean toward... let me think about what bar to set. My job is technical viability AND speed/quality of prototyping. \n\nThe proposal as written would likely slow down prototyping by mandating early integration of compliance infrastructure into all projects, including experimental ones that may never become high-risk systems or may evolve in ways the fixed API doesn't accommodate. However, a properly scoped version (shared logging library available for use when a project transitions to high-risk deployment readiness) would be very reasonable engineering practice.\n\nI'll lean toward reject because as stated, it imposes overhead on all R&D projects and competes with prototyping velocity, but I'll give it a moderate promise score because the underlying concept is sound and could be re-scoped. Actually, let me reconsider - \"reject\" means I wouldn't commit resources to this. The core idea (shared logging library) IS something I'd want in my toolbox. But the framing as a mandate for ALL projects from inception is wrong for prototyping speed.\n\nI'll reject it because:\n1. It mandates integration into ALL high-risk projects, adding prototyping friction\n2. \"Every active model\" is too broad for an R&D lab with many experimental models\n3. The specific event types mentioned are narrow (seem tailored to one use case) and don't cover the full Article 12(2)(a-c) requirements across different system types\n4. It's a significant in-house development commitment that diverts engineering resources from actual R&D\n5. The \"standardized\" framing risks rigidity that will cause friction with diverse model architectures\n\nBut I'll give it maybe a 40-50 promise because the underlying concept (shared, modular logging infrastructure) is technically reasonable and would save time IF properly scoped to deployment-stage projects rather than all R&D activity.\n\nLet me settle on: reject, promise 42.\n",
