ld be more expensive and error-prone).\n\n**Cons / Risks:**\n- Building a \"universal\" framework has upfront development cost — this is an implementation commitment, not exploration. It requires engineering time up front before any team benefits.\n- \"All high-risk AI projects\" — if our lab has diverse architectures (as stated), a single middleware may face edge cases that require customization, potentially creating maintenance burden.\n- The proposal says \"We will develop\" — this is an internal build. Could there be off-the-shelf solutions? However, given the specific regulatory requirements of Article 12 (especially Annex III point 1(a) specifics like reference databases, input data matching, verification person identification), a generic solution likely won't cover all cases without customization.\n- Centralizing log management could create a single point of failure or bottleneck, but this is more of an operational concern than a resource-allocation one.\n\n**My assessment from the Lab Director's vantage point:**\nThis proposal is strongly aligned with my domain concerns. It:\n1. Reduces per-project implementation cost (economies of scale in compliance).\n2. Leverages existing infrastructure (compute clusters) rather than requiring new procurement.\n3. Frees up research engineers to focus on scientific work rather than re-implementing logging boilerplate for each project.\n4. The upfront investment is bounded and the recurring savings across multiple projects justify it — as long as we have a reasonable number of high-risk AI projects in pipeline (which, given we're an R&D institution developing such systems, we presumably do).\n5. It's an implementation commitment (not exploration), which aligns with the framing that approving means committing resources to pursue, not explore.\n\nThe main risk is over-engineering: building a \"universal\" framework when perhaps 80% of our projects share similar architectures and a simpler shared module would suffice. But the