straints:**\n- This is fundamentally a compliance infrastructure investment. It doesn't advance any scientific capability directly. However, without it, the lab faces regulatory risk that could halt deployments or require costly retroactive documentation efforts.\n- The cost should be moderate: Git workflows are cheap to set up, the automation layer adds some engineering time, and the review process uses existing compliance staff (or requires one FTE if none exists).\n- It protects the ability to actually deploy and commercialize AI systems - so it's an enabler of revenue-generating activity rather than a pure cost center.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- Git is almost certainly already in use at any serious R&D lab for code versioning. Extending this to documentation files (markdown, YAML configs describing model architecture, data lineage records) is very natural and low-friction.\n- The \"automatic recording\" aspect aligns with what logging systems likely already capture - the proposal essentially formalizes the link between operational logs and technical documentation updates.\n- However, there's a risk of over-engineering: building an elaborate \"automated system\" when a well-designed commit workflow + CI checks could achieve 90% of the value at 20% of the cost.\n\n**Specific assessment:**\nThe proposal is sound in principle and relatively low-cost to implement given existing infrastructure. The Git-based approach is pragmatic and leverages what's already there. The key risk is scope creep: \"automated system\" could balloon into a large engineering project when the core need (version-controlled, reviewable documentation that updates with model changes) can be met with modest tooling.\n\nThe compliance officer review gate is important for quality but I'd want to ensure it doesn't become a deployment bottleneck in an R&D environment where iteration speed matters. A lightweight \"documentation diff\" review rather than full re-review each