ction.\n\nI'd approve this. It's a high-efficiency, low-marginal-cost approach that leverages existing infrastructure. From my resource-allocation perspective, it's one of the better ways to handle compliance: embed it where work already happens rather than creating a separate compliance layer.\n\nPromise score: I'd give this around 82-85. It's very well-aligned with my concerns about efficiency and using existing infrastructure. The slight uncertainty is in execution — \"restructuring milestones\" could be politically/organizationally harder to implement than it sounds, and the automated checks need to be designed well to avoid becoming a bottleneck that slows scientific iteration. But conceptually from an efficiency standpoint, this is strong.\n\nLet me settle on 83.\n",
