ructure engineers for this scope seems thin if it truly covers \"all high-risk projects.\" But if scoped to the common denominator (timestamps, event types, basic audit trail), two engineers might be feasible for a first version.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping Impact:**\n- PRO: If done well, teams won't need to build their own logging from scratch each time. This accelerates prototyping because compliance logging is handled by the infrastructure layer.\n- PRO: Standardization means less rework and fewer integration bugs when a prototype moves toward production.\n- CON: If the library is over-engineered or imposes rigid abstractions, it could slow down early-stage prototyping where you just want to get something running quickly. A \"compliance telemetry layer\" that must be integrated before you can even test your model adds friction.\n- CON: The risk of a shared library is that it becomes a bottleneck - if there's a bug or the API doesn't fit a particular project's needs, every team is blocked waiting on two infrastructure engineers.\n- CON: \"Plug-and-play\" for diverse AI systems is technically challenging. Different model types (LLMs, computer vision, tabular models), different serving stacks, different data sources - making a truly generic logging layer that captures the right events automatically without deep integration knowledge is hard.\n\n**Assessment:**\nThe core idea is sound and aligns well with what Article 12 requires - automatic recording of events, standardized logging. The technical approach (shared library) is appropriate for the problem domain. However:\n\n1. The scope \"across all high-risk projects\" is very ambitious for two engineers. A more realistic first version might target a subset or provide extensible hooks rather than trying to be fully plug-and-play everywhere.\n2. From a prototyping speed perspective, there's a tension: the library needs to be in place before you can ship, but building it up front delays projects that could start 