        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2820\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The direction is sound—consistent logging across teams avoids the real engineering debt of N ad-hoc implementations—but as scoped ('all high-risk AI systems developed in-house' via a single internal API), it faces a heterogeneity problem that will make the library either too thin to capture Article 12(3)-specific fields (reference databases, match inputs, verifier identity) or too rigid for rapid prototyping cycles across wildly different model architectures. The proposal also offers no indication of leveraging existing observability infrastructure (OpenTelemetry, structured-logging stacks), which would dramatically reduce build time and integration friction; instead it implies a from-scratch internal project that consumes significant engineering bandwidth before any prototype benefits, directly at odds with speed-of-prototyping objectives.\",\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"promise\": 35\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "The user wants me to evaluate proposal P2820 from the vantage point of a Development Engineer at an R&D institution whose domain is \"technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\"\n\nLet me analyze the proposal:\n\n\"Unified Compliance Logging Framework. A standardized software library that automatically captures and stores event logs for all high-risk AI systems developed in-house. Engineering teams will integrate this internal API into their model pipelines to ensure consistent timestamps, input tracking, and database references across different projects. This prevents fragmented implementation and ensures a baseline of technical compliance that protects our market reputation.\"\n\nFrom my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on:\n- Technical viability of solutions\n- Speed/quality of prototyping\n\nLet me think about this carefully:\n\n**Technical Viability:**\n- A unified logging framework is technically very viable. This is well-understood software engineering — building a library that wraps around model inference/training pipelines to capture timestamps, inputs, database references, etc. The tech exists and is mature (similar to OpenTelemetry patterns, structured logging libraries, etc.).\n- However, \"for all high-risk AI systems developed in-house\" is a very broad scope. Different systems have wildly different architectures — some are LLMs, some are real-time inference systems, some are batch processing. A single \"internal API\" that fits all of them cleanly is ambitious and could be technically challenging to get right across heterogeneous stacks.\n- The proposal says \"integrate this internal API into their model pipelines\" — this implies a specific integration point. In practice, different models/pipelines have very different architectures. Making one library work seamlessly across all of them without adding significant friction or requiring heavy refactoring is non-trivial.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- This is the key concern from my vantage point. A unified framework means every new prototype has to integrate with this logging API before it can be deployed as a high-risk system. That adds overhead to the prototyping cycle.\n- However, if done well (as a thin wrapper or middleware), it could actually speed things up because teams don't each build their own ad-hoc logging. The \"prevents fragmented implementation\" point is valid — right now every team might be doing logging differently, which wastes time and creates inconsistency.\n- But there's a risk: building this framework itself takes significant engineering resources before any system benefits from it. That's a large upfront investment that delays prototyping of actual AI systems. The \"build vs. buy\" question is relevant — could we just use OpenTelemetry or similar off-the-shelf solutions with some customization?\n- Also, the proposal is somewhat high-level. It doesn't specify: what storage backend? What log schema? How does it handle different Article 12(3) requirements for Annex III point 1(a) systems (which need specific fields like reference database, input data for matches, person identification)? The level of detail needed for compliance (especially the biometric/match-related logging in 3(c)) is quite different from general event logging.\n\n**Assessment:**\nThe idea is sound and technically viable at a high level — building internal tooling for consistent logging is good engineering practice. But from my vantage point:\n\n1. The scope (\"all high-risk AI systems\") is very broad, making the technical design challenge significant.\n2. It's a large infrastructure project that would consume substantial prototyping/engineering bandwidth before delivering value to any specific system.\n3. There's no mention of leveraging existing open-source logging/observability frameworks (OpenTelemetry, etc.), which could dramatically reduce build time and risk.\n4. The \"internal API\" approach risks becoming a bottleneck or adding friction to rapid prototyping cycles if it's heavyweight.\n5. However, the core problem it solves is real — inconsistent, fragmented logging across teams would be a genuine compliance and engineering headache.\n\nI think this is a reasonable direction but as stated, it's somewhat generic and doesn't address the key technical risks (heterogeneity of systems, integration friction, build vs. buy). From a speed/quality of prototyping perspective, I'm cautious because:\n- It's a big upfront investment\n- \"All high-risk AI systems\" is extremely broad scope\n- No mention of leveraging existing tooling\n- Could slow down prototyping if the API is inflexible\n\nBut it's not a terrible idea. The direction is correct. It's just that as proposed, it lacks the technical specificity to be immediately actionable, and the breadth of scope raises viability concerns for a single library.\n\nI'll lean toward reject or low-approve. Let me think... \n\nActually, the proposal IS technically viable — building a logging library is not rocket science. The question is whether it can do what's asked (unified across ALL high-risk systems) without being so generic that it doesn't capture the specific compliance fields required by Article 12(3). And whether it will slow down prototyping or speed it up.\n\nI think I'll reject it because:\n- The scope is too broad for a single library to be effective without significant customization per system type\n- It's a large upfront project that delays actual AI development\n- No mention of leveraging existing observability tooling (which would make this much more viable and faster)\n- From a prototyping speed perspective, adding a mandatory internal framework integration is friction\n- The proposal doesn't address the specific compliance requirements in enough detail to be technically actionable\n\nBut it's not zero — the direction has merit. I'd give it maybe a 35-40 promise score. It's a reasonable instinct but poorly scoped from an engineering feasibility standpoint for rapid prototyping.\n\nLet me settle on: reject, promise ~38.\n",
