t would otherwise each build their own logging from scratch, a shared library accelerates time-to-compliance. Reduces redundant engineering effort. This is a strong argument for a lab with multiple concurrent projects.\n- CON: Building the framework itself is an upfront investment. If we're in early prototyping phases, spending significant engineering cycles on infrastructure before the projects are even mature can slow down individual project velocity initially. The \"build the platform\" tax.\n- CON: Over-engineering risk. If we build a generic framework that tries to handle every case, it may become complex and hard to maintain, potentially slowing prototyping more than helping it. A simpler per-project approach might be faster for early prototypes.\n- PRO: Once built, it genuinely reduces the \"tax\" on each new high-risk project. Researchers don't need to understand logging requirements - they just integrate with the library. This is a real speedup at scale.\n- CON: The proposal says \"without requiring researchers to write custom logging code.\" In practice, for truly novel architectures or unusual data flows, some customization will always be needed. The framework can reduce but not eliminate this.\n\n**Overall assessment from my vantage point:**\nThis is a reasonable and technically viable engineering investment. It addresses a real problem (redundant per-project logging implementation) with a standard solution pattern. However:\n- It's infrastructure that has lead time before it pays off (you need at least 2-3 projects to see the benefit).\n- The \"unified\" aspect is ambitious - truly unifying across heterogeneous architectures in an R&D lab where projects evolve rapidly is challenging.\n- For prototyping speed specifically, there's a tension: building the framework slows initial prototyping, but accelerates subsequent work.\n\nI think this is approvable from my vantage point because:\n1. It's technically viable (not experimental)\n2. It addresses a real e