tential concerns:**\n- Could this slow down R&D velocity? Potentially, but from a compliance standpoint, building something you can't deploy is worse than slowing development. A project that reaches validation only to fail because logging wasn't architecturally embedded is a wasted investment AND a compliance risk if it was already being tested in the market.\n- The \"gate\" mechanism (funding tranche release) is strong governance — it creates real teeth. Without consequences, process gates get bypassed.\n- Is this overkill for all projects? Not necessarily — Article 12 applies to high-risk AI systems specifically. But as a compliance officer, I'd want the gate to apply at minimum to any project that could plausibly be classified as high-risk. The proposal says \"internal R&D lifecycle before projects transition from prototype to validation\" which seems appropriately scoped.\n- From institutional survival: if we deploy a high-risk system without proper logging and get caught by a regulator, fines under the EU AI Act can be up to €35 million or 7% of global turnover. Preventing that through early architectural gating is cost-effective insurance.\n\n**Overall assessment:** This is a strong compliance-aligned proposal. It addresses the exact risk I'm most worried about: systems reaching market deployment without the technical capability for automatic logging as required by Article 12(1). The funding-gate mechanism gives it real enforcement power. From my vantage point, this directly serves institutional survival (avoiding regulatory penalties), regulatory compliance (ensuring Article 12 is met architecturally), and market reputation (demonstrating robust internal governance to regulators and clients).\n\nThe only minor reservation: I'd want the gate criteria to be explicitly mapped to Article 12(1)-(3) requirements so that \"logging capabilities are integrated\" isn't a vague standard. But as stated, the proposal is sound in principle. The level of detail can be speci