ms, building a full unified platform might be over-engineering compared to using existing logging infrastructure or commercial solutions.\n- \"The engineering team will build\" — this implies pulling engineers away from their current workstreams. In a resource-constrained R&D lab, that's an opportunity cost.\n- However, the proposal frames it as reducing *individual* development burden, which is net positive if we have multiple projects.\n- The \"immutable ledger\" requirement might be overkill for internal compliance — but it does align with the spirit of Article 12 (records should be kept up to date and available). Actually, immutability supports traceability and verification by regulators, so this is reasonable.\n- One risk: if the framework is too rigid or complex, researchers might work around it or resist integration, creating shadow compliance efforts that are harder to audit. But a \"modular API\" suggests flexibility.\n\n**From my specific vantage point:**\n- Resource-allocation efficiency: HIGH. Building once and reusing across projects is the most efficient path vs. per-project custom solutions.\n- Balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints: GOOD. The proposal explicitly frames itself as minimizing individual research burden, protecting scientific throughput. The upfront engineering cost is a one-time investment that pays off across all high-risk systems.\n- Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure: This builds ON TOP of existing systems (it's an API layer). It doesn't require replacing anything major. It leverages the engineering team as shared infrastructure providers rather than having each lab reinvent logging.\n\nThe main question is whether building a custom internal framework is more efficient than adopting an off-the-shelf solution. But given that it needs to be tailored to Article 12 specifics (Annex III hooks, specific event types), and must integrate with our particular model architectures, some degree of custom development